window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["aws"]=[{"id":"aws-f0001","card":"q-aws-001-cloud-vs-on-premises","sec":"aws-s002","q":"What is cloud computing vs. on-premises?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Cloud</strong>: rent resources online, managed by the provider. Flexible and scalable.</li>\n  <li><strong>On-premises</strong>: own and maintain hardware locally. Costly and manual.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0002","card":"q-aws-001-cloud-vs-on-premises","sec":"aws-s002","q":"What are the key benefits of AWS over others?","a":"<p>Global reach, 200+ services, pay-as-you-go pricing, strong security and a big ecosystem.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0003","card":"q-aws-001-cloud-vs-on-premises","sec":"aws-s002","q":"What is the difference between capital expenditure (CapEx) and operational expenditure (OpEx), and how does that relate to moving from on-premises to AWS?","a":"<p>CapEx is money spent up front on assets such as servers and data centers. OpEx is money spent as you consume a service. On-premises is mostly CapEx: you size for peak load and the hardware sits idle the rest of the time. AWS is mostly OpEx: you pay for what you use and can scale down, which lowers the cost of experiments and removes the need to forecast capacity years ahead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0004","card":"q-aws-001-cloud-vs-on-premises","sec":"aws-s002","q":"When might a company still choose on-premises or a hybrid setup instead of moving everything to AWS?","a":"<p>Typical reasons are data-residency or regulatory rules, very low latency to on-site equipment (factory floors), large existing hardware investments, and steady, predictable workloads that are cheaper to run on owned hardware. Hybrid keeps those workloads on site and uses AWS for burst capacity, backup or new services, linked by Direct Connect or Site-to-Site VPN. The trade-off is that you run two environments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0005","card":"q-aws-001-cloud-vs-on-premises","sec":"aws-s002","q":"Name the main advantages of cloud computing that an interviewer expects you to list.","a":"<p>Trade up-front cost for pay-as-you-go cost, economies of scale (lower unit price), no capacity guessing (scale up and down), faster time to market (minutes to provision), no spend on running data centers, and a global footprint you can deploy to in minutes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0006","card":"q-aws-002-iaas-paas-and-saas","sec":"aws-s003","q":"IaaS vs. PaaS vs. SaaS with AWS examples?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>IaaS</strong>: EC2, S3. You manage the OS and apps.</li>\n  <li><strong>PaaS</strong>: Elastic Beanstalk. You deploy code and AWS handles the rest.</li>\n  <li><strong>SaaS</strong>: WorkMail. You just use it.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0007","card":"q-aws-002-iaas-paas-and-saas","sec":"aws-s003","q":"In a service model, how do you decide which layer you are responsible for, and how does that change from IaaS to PaaS to SaaS?","a":"<p>Responsibility moves to the provider as you go up the stack. With IaaS (EC2) you manage the guest OS, runtime, patching and application. With PaaS (Elastic Beanstalk) you manage the application and its configuration, and the provider manages OS and platform. With SaaS you only manage your data and user access. Higher levels give less control and less operational work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0008","card":"q-aws-002-iaas-paas-and-saas","sec":"aws-s003","q":"Where does AWS Lambda fit in IaaS, PaaS and SaaS, and why is it hard to classify?","a":"<p>Lambda is usually described as FaaS (function as a service), a form of serverless that sits next to PaaS. You supply only code, and AWS runs the servers, OS and scaling. It is not IaaS because you do not manage servers, and not SaaS because you still write the application.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0009","card":"q-aws-002-iaas-paas-and-saas","sec":"aws-s003","q":"When would you pick IaaS over PaaS for a new application?","a":"<p>Pick IaaS when you need control that the platform does not allow: custom OS packages, specific kernel settings, licensed software, unusual networking, or a lift-and-shift of an existing app. Pick PaaS when the team wants to ship code without managing servers and the app fits the platform's model. The price of IaaS is more operational work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0010","card":"q-aws-003-elasticity-scalability-and-shared-respon","sec":"aws-s004","q":"What is elasticity in AWS?","a":"<p>Resources auto-scale to match demand. Auto Scaling adds EC2 instances on a traffic spike and removes them when load is low.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0011","card":"q-aws-003-elasticity-scalability-and-shared-respon","sec":"aws-s004","q":"Scalability vs. elasticity?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Scalability</strong>: adding capacity (manual or planned).</li>\n  <li><strong>Elasticity</strong>: automatic, real-time adjustment.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0012","card":"q-aws-003-elasticity-scalability-and-shared-respon","sec":"aws-s004","q":"What is the shared responsibility model?","a":"<p>AWS secures the infrastructure (data centers, hardware). You secure your setup (data, IAM, configuration).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0013","card":"q-aws-003-elasticity-scalability-and-shared-respon","sec":"aws-s004","q":"How does AWS ensure high availability and fault tolerance?","a":"<p>Multi-AZ and multi-Region design with redundant systems, for example ELB across AZs and RDS failover.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0014","card":"q-aws-003-elasticity-scalability-and-shared-respon","sec":"aws-s004","q":"Give an example of an architecture that is scalable but not elastic.","a":"<p>A fleet of EC2 instances that an engineer resizes or adds to by hand before a known event. It can grow, so it is scalable, but it does not adjust automatically to demand and does not shrink on its own. Adding an Auto Scaling group with scaling policies makes it elastic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0015","card":"q-aws-003-elasticity-scalability-and-shared-respon","sec":"aws-s004","q":"In the AWS shared responsibility model, who is responsible for patching the guest OS on an EC2 instance, and who for patching the host of an RDS instance?","a":"<p>For EC2 you patch the guest OS. For RDS, AWS patches the underlying host and database engine on the maintenance schedule you configure, and you control things like the maintenance window, parameters and which users can connect. The general rule: AWS is responsible for security <strong>of</strong> the cloud, and you are responsible for security <strong>in</strong> the cloud. The line moves toward AWS for managed services.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0016","card":"q-aws-003-elasticity-scalability-and-shared-respon","sec":"aws-s004","q":"What does \"security of the cloud\" versus \"security in the cloud\" cover for an S3 bucket?","a":"<p>AWS secures the facilities, hardware, network and the S3 service itself. You secure the bucket policy, access permissions, public access settings, encryption choices and the data inside. A bucket left public is a customer misconfiguration, not an AWS failure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0017","card":"q-aws-003-elasticity-scalability-and-shared-respon","sec":"aws-s004","q":"What is the difference between high availability and fault tolerance?","a":"<p>High availability means the system keeps serving with minimal downtime, usually by redundancy and fast failover (RDS Multi-AZ, with a short failover pause). Fault tolerance means the system keeps running without any interruption when a component fails, which needs more redundancy and costs more. Fault tolerance is a stricter goal than high availability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0018","card":"q-aws-003-elasticity-scalability-and-shared-respon","sec":"aws-s004","q":"Why does an Auto Scaling group help with both elasticity and fault tolerance?","a":"<p>It adds or removes instances based on policies (elasticity), and it also replaces instances that fail health checks and spreads them across Availability Zones (fault tolerance). That means one group handles load changes and instance failures.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0019","card":"q-aws-004-global-infrastructure","sec":"aws-s005","q":"Region, Availability Zone and Edge Location?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Region</strong>: a geographic area (for example us-east-1).</li>\n  <li><strong>AZ</strong>: an isolated site inside a Region.</li>\n  <li><strong>Edge location</strong>: a CloudFront cache point near users.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0020","card":"q-aws-004-global-infrastructure","sec":"aws-s005","q":"What factors do you consider when choosing an AWS Region for a workload?","a":"<p>Data residency and compliance rules, latency to your users, service availability (not every service exists in every Region), and price (it differs by Region). Disaster recovery planning can add a second Region.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0021","card":"q-aws-004-global-infrastructure","sec":"aws-s005","q":"Why do you deploy across multiple Availability Zones, and does that also protect you from a Region outage?","a":"<p>Availability Zones are isolated failure domains with separate power and networking, linked by low-latency connections. Spreading across them protects against a data center failure. It does not protect against a Region-wide issue, so for that you need a multi-Region design with replicated data and DNS failover.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0022","card":"q-aws-005-pricing-and-the-well-architected-framewo","sec":"aws-s006","q":"How does AWS pricing work and how do you optimize cost?","a":"<p>Pay per use: EC2 by the hour, S3 by the GB. Optimize with Reserved or Spot Instances, right-sizing and Cost Explorer.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0023","card":"q-aws-005-pricing-and-the-well-architected-framewo","sec":"aws-s006","q":"What is the AWS Well-Architected Framework and its pillars?","a":"<p>A best-practices guide. Pillars: Operational Excellence, Security, Reliability, Performance Efficiency, Cost Optimization.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0024","card":"q-aws-005-pricing-and-the-well-architected-framewo","sec":"aws-s006","q":"In AWS, how do On-Demand, Reserved Instances/Savings Plans and Spot pricing compare and when would you use each?","a":"<p>On-Demand has no commitment and is the most expensive per hour, for unpredictable or short workloads. Reserved Instances and Savings Plans commit to one or three years of usage for a large discount, for steady baseline load. Spot uses spare capacity at a deep discount but can be interrupted with a two-minute notice, so it suits fault-tolerant, flexible work such as batch jobs and stateless workers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0025","card":"q-aws-005-pricing-and-the-well-architected-framewo","sec":"aws-s006","q":"Which Well-Architected pillar would you point to when someone asks how to react to failure, and what practices belong to it?","a":"<p>Reliability. Practices include distributing across AZs, automated recovery, testing recovery procedures, scaling horizontally, and managing service quotas. Operational Excellence covers running and improving the workload (IaC, runbooks, small reversible changes), so the two are distinct.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0026","card":"q-aws-005-pricing-and-the-well-architected-framewo","sec":"aws-s006","q":"Besides the five pillars named in older material, which pillar was added to the AWS Well-Architected Framework?","a":"<p>Sustainability, added in 2021 as the sixth pillar, about minimizing the environmental impact of running workloads (for example right-sizing and using efficient managed services). Many older articles still list only five pillars.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0027","card":"q-aws-005-pricing-and-the-well-architected-framewo","sec":"aws-s006","q":"Name AWS tools that help you control cost and what each one is for.","a":"<p>Cost Explorer shows and forecasts spend. AWS Budgets sets thresholds and alerts. Cost and Usage Reports give detailed line-item data. Cost allocation tags attribute spend to teams or projects. Compute Optimizer recommends right-sizing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0028","card":"q-aws-006-ec2-basics","sec":"aws-s007","q":"What is Amazon EC2 and its features?","a":"<p>Virtual servers with flexible instance types, scalable, pay-as-you-go, integrated with S3 and VPC, with Elastic IPs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0029","card":"q-aws-006-ec2-basics","sec":"aws-s007","q":"EC2 instance types: t2.micro vs. m5.large?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>t2.micro</strong>: 1 vCPU, 1 GB, burstable. Good for small tasks.</li>\n  <li><strong>m5.large</strong>: 2 vCPUs, 8 GB, steady performance. Good for web apps.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0030","card":"q-aws-006-ec2-basics","sec":"aws-s007","q":"What are AMIs?","a":"<p>Templates (OS plus software) for EC2, stored in S3 and used for consistency and scaling.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0031","card":"q-aws-006-ec2-basics","sec":"aws-s007","q":"How do you secure an EC2 instance?","a":"<p>Security Groups, IAM roles, EBS encryption, private subnets, regular updates and monitoring.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0032","card":"q-aws-006-ec2-basics","sec":"aws-s007","q":"How do you monitor EC2 performance?","a":"<p>CloudWatch for CPU, memory and I/O metrics plus alarms. Add the SSM Agent and CloudTrail.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0033","card":"q-aws-006-ec2-basics","sec":"aws-s007","q":"What is the difference between a burstable instance type such as t3 and a fixed-performance type such as m5?","a":"<p>Burstable instances earn CPU credits while idle and spend them when busy, so they have a low baseline CPU with the ability to burst. If credits run out, performance is throttled to the baseline (unless unlimited mode is on, which can add charges). Fixed types like m5 give sustained CPU, so use them for steady load. Use t-types for small, spiky workloads.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0034","card":"q-aws-006-ec2-basics","sec":"aws-s007","q":"Why can you not monitor memory usage of an EC2 instance with default CloudWatch metrics?","a":"<p>The hypervisor can see CPU, network and disk from outside the instance, but memory is internal to the guest OS. To get memory and disk-space metrics you install the CloudWatch agent on the instance, which publishes them as custom metrics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0035","card":"q-aws-006-ec2-basics","sec":"aws-s007","q":"What is the difference between stopping and terminating an EC2 instance, and what happens to the data on the volumes?","a":"<p>Stopping is like shutting down: you stop paying for compute, the EBS volumes stay and you keep paying for them, and you can start it again. Terminating deletes the instance, and the root EBS volume is deleted by default (the <code>DeleteOnTermination</code> setting) while additional volumes are kept by default. Instance store data is lost on stop or terminate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0036","card":"q-aws-006-ec2-basics","sec":"aws-s007","q":"What is the difference between an Elastic IP and a normal public IP on EC2?","a":"<p>A normal public IPv4 address is released when the instance stops and changes after restart. An Elastic IP is a static public address that belongs to your account and you can remap to another instance, which helps with failover. Public IPv4 addresses are charged, so release unused Elastic IPs and prefer a load balancer or DNS name over a fixed IP where you can.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0037","card":"q-aws-006-ec2-basics","sec":"aws-s007","q":"What is user data on an EC2 instance, and when does it run?","a":"<p>User data is a script or cloud-init configuration passed at launch. By default it runs once, at the first boot, as root. It is used to install packages and start services so that a plain AMI becomes a configured server. The trade-off is slow boot if the script is long, so bake heavy setup into a custom AMI.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0038","card":"q-aws-006-ec2-basics","sec":"aws-s007","q":"What are the three main ways to secure network access to an EC2 instance and which would you use for SSH?","a":"<p>Security groups (stateful firewall at the instance), network ACLs (subnet level), and keeping the instance in a private subnet. For SSH, avoid opening port 22 to <code>0.0.0.0/0</code>. Prefer Systems Manager Session Manager, which needs no inbound port or key, or restrict port 22 to a known address range.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0039","card":"q-aws-006-ec2-basics","sec":"aws-s007","q":"What are EC2 placement groups and which strategy fits which need?","a":"<p>A cluster group packs instances close together in one AZ for the lowest network latency and highest throughput (HPC), at the cost of a shared failure domain. A spread group puts each instance on distinct hardware (up to seven per AZ), for a small number of critical instances. A partition group spreads groups of instances over separate racks, for large distributed systems such as Hadoop, Cassandra or Kafka.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0040","card":"q-aws-006-ec2-basics","sec":"aws-s007","q":"How do you run a workload on Spot Instances safely given that they can be reclaimed?","a":"<p>Make the work stateless or checkpointed, and diversify across several instance types and AZs so one capacity pool running out does not stop you. AWS gives a two-minute interruption notice through the instance metadata and EventBridge, which you use to drain, checkpoint or deregister. Use an Auto Scaling group with a mixed instances policy and the <code>price-capacity-optimized</code> allocation strategy, and keep a small On-Demand base for work that cannot be interrupted.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0041","card":"q-aws-006-ec2-basics","sec":"aws-s007","q":"How do EC2 instance families differ, and how do you pick one?","a":"<p>Families are tuned for a resource: general purpose (m, t) balance CPU and memory, compute optimized (c) for CPU-bound work, memory optimized (r, x) for in-memory databases and caches, storage optimized (i, d) for high local disk IOPS, and accelerated (p, g) for GPU training or graphics. Measure the bottleneck first, then choose the family and size, and re-check with Compute Optimizer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0042","card":"q-aws-006-ec2-basics","sec":"aws-s007","q":"What is the difference between Dedicated Hosts and Dedicated Instances on EC2?","a":"<p>Both run on hardware used only by your account. A Dedicated Host gives you the whole physical server with visibility of sockets and cores, which lets you bring server-bound software licences and control placement. A Dedicated Instance gives single-tenant hardware but you do not see or control the host. Choose a Dedicated Host for licence compliance and a Dedicated Instance only for isolation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0043","card":"q-aws-006-ec2-basics","sec":"aws-s007","q":"When would you bake an AMI instead of configuring instances with user data?","a":"<p>Bake an AMI when launches must be fast and repeatable, such as in Auto Scaling, because the software is already installed and nothing can fail on a package download at boot. Use user data for small, changing steps such as fetching the latest config. Many teams combine both: a baked base image built by a pipeline and light user data for environment settings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0044","card":"q-aws-007-auto-scaling","sec":"aws-s008","q":"What is an Auto Scaling Group?","a":"<p>It manages a fleet of EC2 instances, scales on demand and pairs with ELB.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0045","card":"q-aws-007-auto-scaling","sec":"aws-s008","q":"What is the difference between target tracking, step scaling and scheduled scaling?","a":"<p>Target tracking holds a metric near a target (for example average CPU at 50%) and is the simplest choice. Step scaling adds or removes different amounts depending on how far an alarm metric goes past a threshold. Scheduled scaling changes capacity at set times for predictable patterns. Predictive scaling also exists and forecasts load from history.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0046","card":"q-aws-007-auto-scaling","sec":"aws-s008","q":"Why use ELB health checks with an Auto Scaling group, not only EC2 status checks?","a":"<p>EC2 status checks only tell whether the instance is running. An application can hang while the instance is still \"healthy\". With ELB health checks enabled on the group, an instance that fails the load balancer's HTTP check is replaced too.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0047","card":"q-aws-007-auto-scaling","sec":"aws-s008","q":"What does the cooldown (or instance warm-up) setting do in Auto Scaling?","a":"<p>It stops the group from launching or terminating more instances before the effect of the last action shows up. Without it, a slow-starting instance can make the metric still look high, so the group over-scales. Warm-up tells the group how long a new instance takes before its metrics count.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0048","card":"q-aws-007-auto-scaling","sec":"aws-s008","q":"Why should the app tier in an Auto Scaling group be stateless?","a":"<p>Instances can be removed at any time on scale-in, so any session or file stored only on one instance is lost. Keep state in external stores such as ElastiCache, DynamoDB, RDS or S3, so any instance can serve any request.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0049","card":"q-aws-007-auto-scaling","sec":"aws-s008","q":"What is a launch template and why is it preferred over a launch configuration?","a":"<p>A launch template defines how instances are created (AMI, instance type, key, security groups, user data) and supports versions, multiple instance types and Spot options. Launch configurations are immutable and no longer receive new features, and AWS recommends launch templates for new Auto Scaling groups.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0050","card":"q-aws-007-auto-scaling","sec":"aws-s008","q":"What is an Auto Scaling lifecycle hook and when would you use one?","a":"<p>A lifecycle hook pauses an instance in <code>Pending:Wait</code> during launch or <code>Terminating:Wait</code> during scale-in so you can run custom actions before it continues. Use it to finish configuration or register with a system before the instance takes traffic, or to drain work and upload logs before termination. The instance waits until you complete the action or the timeout expires, then proceeds with the default result.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0051","card":"q-aws-007-auto-scaling","sec":"aws-s008","q":"How do you roll out a new launch template version to the instances already in an Auto Scaling group?","a":"<p>Changing the launch template does not touch running instances; only new instances use it. Start an instance refresh, which replaces instances in batches while keeping a minimum healthy percentage, so the fleet is updated without a full outage. The trade-off is reduced capacity during the refresh, so set the minimum healthy percentage and warm-up to match your headroom.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0052","card":"q-aws-008-lambda-and-elastic-beanstalk","sec":"aws-s009","q":"What is AWS Lambda and when do you use it?","a":"<p>Serverless compute that runs code on events. Use it over EC2 for short, event-driven tasks such as file processing.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0053","card":"q-aws-008-lambda-and-elastic-beanstalk","sec":"aws-s009","q":"Elastic Beanstalk vs. EC2 vs. Lambda?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Elastic Beanstalk</strong>: PaaS, auto-manages deployment.</li>\n  <li><strong>EC2</strong>: full control, manual.</li>\n  <li><strong>Lambda</strong>: serverless and event-based.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0054","card":"q-aws-008-lambda-and-elastic-beanstalk","sec":"aws-s009","q":"What is a Lambda cold start, and how can you reduce it?","a":"<p>A cold start is the extra latency when Lambda must create a new execution environment, download your code and start the runtime before the handler runs. Reduce it with provisioned concurrency, smaller packages, lighter runtimes and initialization code kept outside the handler.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0055","card":"q-aws-008-lambda-and-elastic-beanstalk","sec":"aws-s009","q":"What are the main Lambda limits an interviewer expects you to know?","a":"<p>As of 2024, the maximum timeout is 15 minutes, memory ranges from 128 MB to 10,240 MB (CPU scales with memory), the deployment package is 50 MB zipped directly and 250 MB unzipped (container images up to 10 GB), and the default account concurrency is 1,000 per Region (it can be raised). Long jobs belong on ECS, Fargate, Batch or Step Functions instead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0056","card":"q-aws-008-lambda-and-elastic-beanstalk","sec":"aws-s009","q":"How is Lambda billed, and when does it become more expensive than EC2?","a":"<p>You pay per request and per GB-second of duration, with nothing charged while idle. For low or spiky traffic it is far cheaper. For a constant high-throughput workload that keeps many functions busy all day, always-on EC2 or containers with reserved pricing can cost less.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0057","card":"q-aws-008-lambda-and-elastic-beanstalk","sec":"aws-s009","q":"Which services can trigger a Lambda function, and what is the difference between synchronous and asynchronous invocation?","a":"<p>Triggers include S3 events, SQS, SNS, DynamoDB Streams, Kinesis, EventBridge and API Gateway. In synchronous invocation (API Gateway) the caller waits for the result and handles errors. In asynchronous invocation (S3, SNS, EventBridge) Lambda queues the event and retries failures twice, and you can send failures to a dead-letter queue or on-failure destination.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0058","card":"q-aws-008-lambda-and-elastic-beanstalk","sec":"aws-s009","q":"When would you choose Elastic Beanstalk over assembling EC2, an ALB and Auto Scaling yourself?","a":"<p>Beanstalk is useful when a team wants to upload code (Java, Python, Node.js, Docker and others) and let AWS create the instances, load balancer, scaling and monitoring. You can still access the underlying resources. It suits standard web apps. It is less suited when you need fine-grained control over every component or are going to microservices on containers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0059","card":"q-aws-008-lambda-and-elastic-beanstalk","sec":"aws-s009","q":"In AWS Lambda, what is the difference between reserved concurrency and provisioned concurrency?","a":"<p>Reserved concurrency sets a guaranteed share of the account's concurrency for one function and also caps it, so it protects other functions from this one and this one from exhausting a downstream database. It costs nothing extra and does not remove cold starts. Provisioned concurrency keeps a number of execution environments initialized and ready, which removes cold starts but is billed while it is configured.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0060","card":"q-aws-008-lambda-and-elastic-beanstalk","sec":"aws-s009","q":"What happens when a Lambda function hits its concurrency limit, and how does the behavior differ by invocation type?","a":"<p>Extra invocations are throttled. A synchronous caller such as API Gateway receives a 429 <code>TooManyRequestsException</code> and must retry. For asynchronous events Lambda retries on its own for up to six hours before dropping or sending to the failure destination. For SQS triggers, throttled messages return to the queue and are retried after the visibility timeout. Reserved concurrency or a queue in front of a function controls this.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0061","card":"q-aws-008-lambda-and-elastic-beanstalk","sec":"aws-s009","q":"What changes when you attach a Lambda function to a VPC?","a":"<p>Lambda creates network interfaces in your subnets, so the function can reach private resources such as an RDS instance. It then loses default internet access, so it needs a NAT gateway in a public subnet to call the internet, or VPC endpoints to reach AWS services privately. Its security group controls outbound traffic, and subnets in several AZs should be given for availability. Do not put a function in a VPC unless it needs private resources.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0062","card":"q-aws-008-lambda-and-elastic-beanstalk","sec":"aws-s009","q":"Why must a Lambda handler be idempotent?","a":"<p>Lambda delivers events at least once: asynchronous invocations, SQS and stream event triggers can retry or deliver duplicates, and a timeout part-way leads to a retry. If the handler charges a card or inserts a row each time, a retry repeats the side effect. Use an idempotency key (for example the message ID or a client token) and a conditional write to a store such as DynamoDB so a second run does nothing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0063","card":"q-aws-008-lambda-and-elastic-beanstalk","sec":"aws-s009","q":"What are Lambda layers and environment variables used for?","a":"<p>A layer is a zip of libraries or runtime files that several functions share, so each deployment package stays small and shared dependencies are updated in one place. Environment variables hold configuration such as table names or feature flags without code changes; values are encrypted at rest and can use a customer managed KMS key. Keep secrets in Secrets Manager or Parameter Store, not in plain environment variables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0064","card":"q-aws-008-lambda-and-elastic-beanstalk","sec":"aws-s009","q":"How do Lambda versions and aliases support safe releases?","a":"<p>Publishing creates an immutable numbered version. An alias such as <code>prod</code> points to a version and can split traffic by weight between two versions, so you can send 10% to the new code, watch the error metric and then shift fully or roll back by moving the alias. CodeDeploy can automate this shift with alarms.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0065","card":"q-aws-009-containers","sec":"aws-s010","q":"ECS vs. EKS?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>ECS</strong>: AWS-native container orchestration. Simple.</li>\n  <li><strong>EKS</strong>: managed Kubernetes. Flexible and portable.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0066","card":"q-aws-009-containers","sec":"aws-s010","q":"What is AWS Fargate?","a":"<p>Serverless containers for ECS and EKS. There are no EC2 instances to manage; you only define resources.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0067","card":"q-aws-009-containers","sec":"aws-s010","q":"What are the ECS launch types and how do you choose between EC2 and Fargate?","a":"<p>With the EC2 launch type you manage the cluster instances (patching, scaling, capacity), which gives control over instance type, GPUs and cost optimization at scale. With Fargate you declare CPU and memory per task and AWS runs it with no servers to manage. Choose Fargate for less operational work, EC2 for specialized hardware or tight cost control on steady large loads.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0068","card":"q-aws-009-containers","sec":"aws-s010","q":"What is the difference between an ECS task definition, a task and a service?","a":"<p>A task definition is the blueprint (images, CPU, memory, ports, roles). A task is one running instance of that definition. A service keeps a desired number of tasks running, replaces failed ones and can register them with a load balancer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0069","card":"q-aws-009-containers","sec":"aws-s010","q":"What is Amazon ECR and why use it instead of Docker Hub for AWS workloads?","a":"<p>ECR is AWS's private container registry. It integrates with IAM for access control, scans images for vulnerabilities, supports replication across Regions, and pulls inside AWS are fast and avoid Docker Hub rate limits. ECS and EKS pull from it using their IAM roles.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0070","card":"q-aws-009-containers","sec":"aws-s010","q":"When would you choose EKS over ECS?","a":"<p>Choose EKS when you already use Kubernetes, want portability across clouds, need the Kubernetes ecosystem (Helm, operators, CRDs), or have a platform team that knows it. Choose ECS when you want the simplest AWS-native option with less to learn and operate. EKS has a per-cluster control-plane fee and a steeper learning curve.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0071","card":"q-aws-009-containers","sec":"aws-s010","q":"What is the difference between the ECS task role and the task execution role?","a":"<p>The task execution role is used by ECS itself to pull the image from ECR and send logs to CloudWatch. The task role is what your application code uses to call AWS services such as S3 or DynamoDB. Keeping them separate gives the app only the permissions it needs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0072","card":"q-aws-010-s3-ebs-and-efs","sec":"aws-s011","q":"S3 vs. EBS vs. EFS?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>S3</strong>: object storage for backups and web content.</li>\n  <li><strong>EBS</strong>: block storage for EC2, for example databases.</li>\n  <li><strong>EFS</strong>: shared file system for multi-instance apps.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0073","card":"q-aws-010-s3-ebs-and-efs","sec":"aws-s011","q":"What is Amazon EBS and how does it integrate with EC2?","a":"<p>Block storage that mounts to EC2 as a root or data volume. Snapshots provide backup.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0074","card":"q-aws-010-s3-ebs-and-efs","sec":"aws-s011","q":"SSD vs. HDD in EBS?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>SSD (gp3, io2)</strong>: fast and IOPS-heavy. Good for databases.</li>\n  <li><strong>HDD (st1, sc1)</strong>: cheap and throughput-focused. Good for big data and cold storage.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0075","card":"q-aws-010-s3-ebs-and-efs","sec":"aws-s011","q":"What is Amazon EFS and when do you choose it over S3?","a":"<p>A shared file system that mounts to multiple EC2 instances. Use it for shared file access, where S3 offers object storage.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0076","card":"q-aws-010-s3-ebs-and-efs","sec":"aws-s011","q":"Why can an EBS volume normally be attached to only one EC2 instance at a time, and what is the exception?","a":"<p>EBS is block storage that behaves like a local disk, and normal file systems on it are not built for concurrent writers from several servers. The exception is Multi-Attach for io1/io2 volumes in the same Availability Zone, which needs a cluster-aware file system. For shared access across instances use EFS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0077","card":"q-aws-010-s3-ebs-and-efs","sec":"aws-s011","q":"Is an EBS volume tied to an Availability Zone, and how do you move it to another one?","a":"<p>Yes, an EBS volume lives in a single AZ and can only attach to instances in that AZ. To move it, take a snapshot (stored in S3 and Regional), then create a new volume from the snapshot in the target AZ.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0078","card":"q-aws-010-s3-ebs-and-efs","sec":"aws-s011","q":"What is the difference between EBS and instance store volumes?","a":"<p>EBS is network-attached persistent storage that survives stop and start. Instance store is disk physically attached to the host, very fast, but the data is lost when the instance stops, terminates or the host fails. Use instance store for temporary data such as caches and buffers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0079","card":"q-aws-010-s3-ebs-and-efs","sec":"aws-s011","q":"How would you pick between S3, EBS and EFS for a given workload?","a":"<p>S3 for objects accessed through an API or HTTP, such as images, backups and data lakes, with virtually unlimited size. EBS for a low-latency disk attached to one instance, such as a database or boot volume. EFS for a POSIX file system shared by many instances or containers across AZs, such as shared content. Also consider cost: S3 is cheapest per GB, EFS is the most expensive.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0080","card":"q-aws-010-s3-ebs-and-efs","sec":"aws-s011","q":"What is the maximum size of a single S3 object, and how do you upload large files?","a":"<p>A single object can be up to 5 TB. A single PUT upload is limited to 5 GB, so for larger or unreliable uploads use multipart upload (recommended from about 100 MB up), which uploads parts in parallel and lets you retry only failed parts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0081","card":"q-aws-010-s3-ebs-and-efs","sec":"aws-s011","q":"What is the consistency model of S3 today?","a":"<p>Since December 2020 S3 provides strong read-after-write consistency for all PUTs and DELETEs, including overwrites and listings. Older material talks about eventual consistency for overwrites and deletes, which no longer applies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0082","card":"q-aws-010-s3-ebs-and-efs","sec":"aws-s011","q":"How do the EBS volume types differ and when do you choose a throughput-optimized HDD over an SSD?","a":"<p>SSD volumes (<code>gp3</code>, <code>io2</code>) serve random I/O: <code>gp3</code> for most workloads, <code>io2</code> for latency-sensitive databases that need high provisioned IOPS and durability. HDD volumes (<code>st1</code> throughput optimized, <code>sc1</code> cold) suit large sequential reads and writes such as logs and data warehousing and are cheaper per GB, but cannot be used as boot volumes. Use HDD only for big sequential workloads.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0083","card":"q-aws-010-s3-ebs-and-efs","sec":"aws-s011","q":"Why are EBS snapshots cheap after the first one, and what must you know before deleting one?","a":"<p>Snapshots are incremental: after the first full copy, each stores only blocks that changed since the previous snapshot. When you delete a snapshot, AWS keeps the blocks that later snapshots still need, so the others stay restorable. Snapshots are stored in S3-managed storage, are Regional, and can be copied across Regions or accounts and encrypted during the copy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0084","card":"q-aws-011-s3-features","sec":"aws-s012","q":"What is an S3 bucket and what are its use cases?","a":"<p>An object container used for web hosting, backups, data lakes and content delivery.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0085","card":"q-aws-011-s3-features","sec":"aws-s012","q":"What is S3 versioning?","a":"<p>It keeps all versions of an object so older ones can be retrieved. It costs more.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0086","card":"q-aws-011-s3-features","sec":"aws-s012","q":"What are the S3 storage classes?","a":"<p>Standard (frequent access), IA (less used), One Zone-IA (cheap, risky), Glacier (archive), Deep Archive (rare access), Intelligent-Tiering (automatic).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0087","card":"q-aws-011-s3-features","sec":"aws-s012","q":"How do you secure S3 data?","a":"<p>IAM and bucket policies, SSE-S3 or KMS encryption, versioning, block public access and access logs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0088","card":"q-aws-011-s3-features","sec":"aws-s012","q":"What are S3 lifecycle rules and what problem do they solve?","a":"<p>Lifecycle rules automatically transition objects to cheaper storage classes after a number of days (Standard to Standard-IA to Glacier) or expire them. They cut storage costs without manual work, and can also delete old object versions and incomplete multipart uploads.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0089","card":"q-aws-011-s3-features","sec":"aws-s012","q":"When using S3 versioning, what happens when you delete an object, and how do you get it back?","a":"<p>A delete without a version ID adds a delete marker, and the earlier versions remain. To restore, remove the delete marker or fetch a specific version ID. Deleting a specific version ID permanently removes that version. Add MFA Delete for protection against accidental permanent deletes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0090","card":"q-aws-011-s3-features","sec":"aws-s012","q":"How do you serve a private S3 bucket's content to users for a limited time without making the bucket public?","a":"<p>Generate a presigned URL with the credentials of an authorized principal. It carries a signature and expiry, so whoever holds it can perform that one operation until it expires. It cannot grant more than the signing identity's own permissions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0091","card":"q-aws-011-s3-features","sec":"aws-s012","q":"What is the difference between an S3 bucket policy and an IAM policy, and when do you use each?","a":"<p>An IAM policy attaches to a user or role and says what that identity can do. A bucket policy attaches to the bucket and says who can access it, including other accounts, the public or specific VPC endpoints. Use bucket policies for cross-account access and bucket-wide rules (force TLS, deny unencrypted uploads). Access is allowed if either allows it and nothing denies it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0092","card":"q-aws-011-s3-features","sec":"aws-s012","q":"How do you replicate S3 data to another Region and what is required?","a":"<p>Use Cross-Region Replication, a replication rule on the source bucket with a destination bucket and an IAM role that S3 assumes. Versioning must be enabled on both buckets. Replication applies to new objects after the rule is set; existing objects need S3 Batch Replication.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0093","card":"q-aws-011-s3-features","sec":"aws-s012","q":"Which S3 storage class would you choose for data you rarely read but need back in milliseconds, and which for archive data that can wait hours?","a":"<p>For rarely read data with millisecond access use Standard-IA (multi-AZ) or One Zone-IA if you accept losing it with an AZ (cheaper). For archives that can wait use Glacier Flexible Retrieval (minutes to hours) or Glacier Deep Archive (about 12 hours, lowest cost). Intelligent-Tiering fits unknown access patterns.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0094","card":"q-aws-011-s3-features","sec":"aws-s012","q":"What does S3 Block Public Access do and why is it the first control to check?","a":"<p>It is a set of account-level and bucket-level settings that override ACLs and bucket policies that would make data public. It stops accidental exposure from a bad policy, which is the most common cause of S3 data leaks. It is enabled by default on new buckets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0095","card":"q-aws-011-s3-features","sec":"aws-s012","q":"How do you react to a new object landing in S3, and which targets can receive the event?","a":"<p>Configure S3 event notifications on the bucket for events such as <code>s3:ObjectCreated:*</code>, with an optional prefix or suffix filter. Targets are Lambda, SQS, SNS, or EventBridge, which adds richer filtering and many more targets. Put an SQS queue in between when processing is slow or needs retries and buffering, since a direct Lambda trigger is asynchronous and bursts can be throttled.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0096","card":"q-aws-011-s3-features","sec":"aws-s012","q":"How do S3 multipart upload and Transfer Acceleration differ?","a":"<p>Multipart upload splits a large object into parts uploaded in parallel and retried individually, which improves throughput and reliability on any network path. Transfer Acceleration sends the upload to the nearest CloudFront edge location and carries it to the bucket over the AWS backbone, which helps users who are far from the bucket's Region. They solve different problems and can be used together.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0097","card":"q-aws-011-s3-features","sec":"aws-s012","q":"What is S3 Object Lock and when do you use governance versus compliance mode?","a":"<p>Object Lock enforces write-once-read-many (WORM) storage: an object version cannot be deleted or overwritten for a retention period or while a legal hold is set. It requires versioning. In governance mode users with a special permission can bypass it. In compliance mode nobody, including the root user, can shorten or remove the retention, which meets regulations such as SEC 17a-4. Use compliance mode only when you really need it, since it cannot be undone.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0098","card":"q-aws-011-s3-features","sec":"aws-s012","q":"What is the correct way to host a static website on S3 for the public, and what is the limitation of the S3 website endpoint?","a":"<p>Keep the bucket private and put CloudFront in front of it with Origin Access Control, plus an ACM certificate for HTTPS and a custom domain from Route 53. The S3 website endpoint serves only HTTP and requires a public bucket, so it fails the security and HTTPS requirements. CloudFront also adds caching and lower latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0099","card":"q-aws-011-s3-features","sec":"aws-s012","q":"How does S3 scale request rates, and how should you design key names for heavy traffic?","a":"<p>S3 supports at least 3,500 write and 5,500 read requests per second per prefix, and there is no limit on the number of prefixes. Since the 2018 performance improvement you no longer need random key prefixes for that rate, but spreading hot objects across several prefixes raises the total. For very hot reads, cache with CloudFront.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0100","card":"q-aws-011-s3-features","sec":"aws-s012","q":"How do you force access to an S3 bucket only through a specific VPC endpoint?","a":"<p>Add a bucket policy statement with <code>Effect: Deny</code> for all principals and a condition <code>StringNotEquals</code> on <code>aws:SourceVpce</code> set to the gateway endpoint ID (or <code>aws:SourceVpc</code> for the whole VPC). Requests that do not come through that endpoint are denied, including from the internet. Be careful: the deny also blocks console access and admins outside the VPC, so test it and keep a way to recover the policy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0101","card":"q-aws-012-encryption-and-migration","sec":"aws-s013","q":"How do you enable encryption for S3, EBS and EFS?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>S3</strong>: SSE-S3 or KMS through bucket settings.</li>\n  <li><strong>EBS</strong>: KMS at creation.</li>\n  <li><strong>EFS</strong>: KMS at setup.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0102","card":"q-aws-012-encryption-and-migration","sec":"aws-s013","q":"How does AWS Snowball help migration?","a":"<p>It is a physical device (80 TB) that ships data to S3 for large, slow transfers.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0103","card":"q-aws-012-encryption-and-migration","sec":"aws-s013","q":"What is the difference between SSE-S3, SSE-KMS and SSE-C?","a":"<p>With SSE-S3, S3 manages the keys. With SSE-KMS, keys live in AWS KMS, which adds key policies, audit through CloudTrail and rotation control, with a small cost and request quota. With SSE-C you supply the key on every request and AWS does not store it. Since January 2023 S3 encrypts all new objects with SSE-S3 by default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0104","card":"q-aws-012-encryption-and-migration","sec":"aws-s013","q":"What is envelope encryption and why does KMS use it?","a":"<p>A data key encrypts the data locally, and a KMS key encrypts that data key. You store the encrypted data key with the data. This avoids sending large data to KMS (it handles only small payloads up to 4 KB) and means the master key never leaves KMS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0105","card":"q-aws-012-encryption-and-migration","sec":"aws-s013","q":"What is the difference between encryption in transit and at rest on AWS?","a":"<p>At rest protects stored data (S3 SSE, EBS and RDS encryption with KMS). In transit protects data moving over the network, with TLS/HTTPS. Enforce TLS on S3 with a bucket policy that denies requests where <code>aws:SecureTransport</code> is false.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0106","card":"q-aws-012-encryption-and-migration","sec":"aws-s013","q":"When would you use Snowball, DataSync or a network transfer to move data to AWS?","a":"<p>Use Snowball for tens of terabytes or more when bandwidth is limited, as it is shipped physically. Use DataSync for online, scheduled transfers from NFS/SMB storage to S3, EFS or FSx. For a smaller data set with decent bandwidth, a direct upload or Direct Connect is enough. Calculate the transfer time at your bandwidth first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0107","card":"q-aws-013-vpc-subnets-and-route-tables","sec":"aws-s014","q":"What is a VPC and how do you set one up?","a":"<p>A private cloud network. Set a CIDR (for example 10.0.0.0/16), subnets, an Internet Gateway and route tables.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0108","card":"q-aws-013-vpc-subnets-and-route-tables","sec":"aws-s014","q":"Public vs. private subnet?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Public</strong>: routes to the Internet Gateway. Web-facing.</li>\n  <li><strong>Private</strong>: no direct internet. Backend.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0109","card":"q-aws-013-vpc-subnets-and-route-tables","sec":"aws-s014","q":"What is a route table in a VPC?","a":"<p>It directs traffic. For example, 0.0.0.0/0 to the Internet Gateway for public subnets.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0110","card":"q-aws-013-vpc-subnets-and-route-tables","sec":"aws-s014","q":"What makes a subnet public in AWS?","a":"<p>Its route table has a route for <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> to an Internet Gateway. In addition, instances need a public or Elastic IP to be reachable from the internet. A subnet is private when it has no such route.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0111","card":"q-aws-013-vpc-subnets-and-route-tables","sec":"aws-s014","q":"A VPC is created with the CIDR 10.0.0.0/16. How many addresses is that, and how many in a /24 subnet are usable?","a":"<p>A /16 has 65,536 addresses. A /24 has 256, but AWS reserves five in each subnet (network address, VPC router, DNS, one for future use, broadcast), so 251 are usable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0112","card":"q-aws-013-vpc-subnets-and-route-tables","sec":"aws-s014","q":"Is a subnet in one Availability Zone or several?","a":"<p>One. A subnet lives in a single AZ. For high availability you create subnets in several AZs and spread resources across them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0113","card":"q-aws-013-vpc-subnets-and-route-tables","sec":"aws-s014","q":"Consider this route table of a subnet. What does the second route do, and what kind of subnet is it?","a":"<p>The first route lets resources inside the VPC reach each other. The second sends all other traffic to an Internet Gateway, so the subnet is public. If the target were a NAT gateway, the subnet would be private with outbound-only internet access.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Text</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Destination      Target\n10.0.0.0/16      local\n0.0.0.0/0        igw-0abc123</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0114","card":"q-aws-013-vpc-subnets-and-route-tables","sec":"aws-s014","q":"What are the main options to connect two VPCs and when would you use each?","a":"<p>VPC peering is a one-to-one private connection, simple but non-transitive and needs non-overlapping CIDRs. Transit Gateway is a hub that connects many VPCs and on-premises networks with transitive routing. PrivateLink exposes a single service privately to other VPCs without opening the whole network.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0115","card":"q-aws-013-vpc-subnets-and-route-tables","sec":"aws-s014","q":"Why plan CIDR ranges carefully before creating VPCs?","a":"<p>A VPC's primary CIDR cannot be changed (you can only add secondary ranges), and peering or Transit Gateway routing needs non-overlapping ranges, as does a VPN to on-premises. Choosing a range that is too small or overlaps later forces a rebuild.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0116","card":"q-aws-014-gateways","sec":"aws-s015","q":"What is a NAT Gateway?","a":"<p>It gives outbound internet access to private subnets. It sits in a public subnet with an Elastic IP.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0117","card":"q-aws-014-gateways","sec":"aws-s015","q":"Internet Gateway vs. NAT Gateway?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Internet Gateway</strong>: two-way public access.</li>\n  <li><strong>NAT</strong>: one-way outbound for private subnets.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0118","card":"q-aws-014-gateways","sec":"aws-s015","q":"What is AWS Direct Connect?","a":"<p>A private link to AWS for high-speed, secure hybrid transfers.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0119","card":"q-aws-014-gateways","sec":"aws-s015","q":"Why is a NAT Gateway placed in a public subnet, and what route does the private subnet need?","a":"<p>The NAT Gateway itself needs a route to the Internet Gateway to reach the internet, so it sits in a public subnet with an Elastic IP. The private subnet's route table sends <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> to the NAT Gateway. The traffic flow is private instance, NAT Gateway, Internet Gateway.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0120","card":"q-aws-014-gateways","sec":"aws-s015","q":"How do you keep NAT Gateway available across Availability Zones, and why is it a cost concern?","a":"<p>A NAT Gateway is resilient inside its AZ only, so for high availability deploy one per AZ and route each private subnet to the gateway in its own AZ. It is billed per hour and per GB processed, so large traffic to S3 or DynamoDB through NAT is costly. Gateway VPC endpoints for S3 and DynamoDB avoid that.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0121","card":"q-aws-014-gateways","sec":"aws-s015","q":"What is a VPC endpoint and what is the difference between a gateway endpoint and an interface endpoint?","a":"<p>A VPC endpoint lets resources reach AWS services privately without going over the internet. A gateway endpoint (S3 and DynamoDB only) is a route-table target and is free. An interface endpoint (PrivateLink) is an elastic network interface with a private IP in your subnet, supports many services and is charged hourly and per GB.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0122","card":"q-aws-014-gateways","sec":"aws-s015","q":"When would you choose Direct Connect over a Site-to-Site VPN?","a":"<p>Direct Connect is a dedicated private network link, giving consistent latency and high bandwidth, but takes weeks to set up and costs more. Site-to-Site VPN runs encrypted over the internet, is set up in minutes and is cheaper but with variable performance. Many companies use Direct Connect as primary with VPN as backup.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0123","card":"q-aws-014-gateways","sec":"aws-s015","q":"Do instances in a private subnet need a NAT Gateway to receive inbound connections from a load balancer?","a":"<p>No. A public load balancer in the public subnets forwards to targets in private subnets using private IPs inside the VPC. NAT is needed only for the private instances' own outbound connections to the internet, such as downloading updates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0124","card":"q-aws-015-load-balancing-dns-and-filtering","sec":"aws-s016","q":"What is an Elastic Load Balancer and what are its types?","a":"<p>It distributes traffic: ALB (HTTP), NLB (TCP/UDP), CLB (legacy).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0125","card":"q-aws-015-load-balancing-dns-and-filtering","sec":"aws-s016","q":"What is Amazon Route 53?","a":"<p>A DNS service that resolves domains and offers failover and latency routing.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0126","card":"q-aws-015-load-balancing-dns-and-filtering","sec":"aws-s016","q":"Security Group vs. Network ACL?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Security Group</strong>: stateful, instance-level.</li>\n  <li><strong>NACL</strong>: stateless, subnet-level.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0127","card":"q-aws-015-load-balancing-dns-and-filtering","sec":"aws-s016","q":"How do you troubleshoot VPC connectivity?","a":"<p>Check routes, Security Groups, NACLs, Flow Logs and IP assignment.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0128","card":"q-aws-015-load-balancing-dns-and-filtering","sec":"aws-s016","q":"When would you use an Application Load Balancer instead of a Network Load Balancer?","a":"<p>Use an ALB for HTTP/HTTPS traffic when you need layer-7 features: path or host based routing, header rules, redirects, WebSockets, authentication with Cognito or OIDC and one load balancer for many services. Use an NLB for TCP/UDP/TLS needing very high throughput, ultra-low latency, static IPs per AZ or PrivateLink. Gateway Load Balancer is for inserting virtual appliances such as firewalls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0129","card":"q-aws-015-load-balancing-dns-and-filtering","sec":"aws-s016","q":"What is the difference between a load balancer listener, a rule and a target group?","a":"<p>A listener checks for connections on a protocol and port. A rule on the listener matches conditions (path, host) and picks an action. A target group is the set of targets (instances, IPs, Lambda) with its own health checks that receive the traffic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0130","card":"q-aws-015-load-balancing-dns-and-filtering","sec":"aws-s016","q":"What is the difference between Route 53 routing policies: simple, weighted, latency, failover and geolocation?","a":"<p>Simple returns one or more values with no health logic. Weighted splits traffic by proportion, useful for canary releases. Latency sends users to the Region with the lowest latency. Failover uses health checks to switch from a primary to a secondary. Geolocation routes by the user's location, for compliance or localized content.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0131","card":"q-aws-015-load-balancing-dns-and-filtering","sec":"aws-s016","q":"Why are Security Groups stateful and NACLs stateless, and what does that mean in practice?","a":"<p>With a stateful security group, return traffic for an allowed request is allowed automatically. With a stateless NACL, return traffic must be explicitly allowed by its own rule, including ephemeral ports (1024-65535). Security groups only have allow rules, while NACLs have allow and deny rules evaluated in number order, which makes NACLs the tool for blocking a specific IP range.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0132","card":"q-aws-015-load-balancing-dns-and-filtering","sec":"aws-s016","q":"A target group instance keeps being marked unhealthy by the ALB although the app works when you SSH in. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check the health check path, port and expected status code match what the app returns, that the instance's security group allows traffic from the load balancer's security group on that port, that the app listens on the right interface, and that the health check timeout and thresholds are reasonable for startup time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0133","card":"q-aws-015-load-balancing-dns-and-filtering","sec":"aws-s016","q":"What is cross-zone load balancing and how does it differ between ALB and NLB?","a":"<p>It lets each load balancer node send traffic to targets in all enabled AZs, not only its own, which evens out load when the number of targets per AZ differs. It is enabled by default for ALB (and can be disabled per target group). For NLB it is off by default and cross-AZ data transfer is charged when enabled.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0134","card":"q-aws-015-load-balancing-dns-and-filtering","sec":"aws-s016","q":"How do sticky sessions work on an Application Load Balancer, and why are they a last resort?","a":"<p>With stickiness enabled on a target group, the ALB sets a cookie (its own duration-based cookie or an application cookie) so the same client keeps going to the same target for the session. It supports apps that keep session state in memory. The cost is uneven load and lost sessions when a target is removed or fails, so storing sessions in ElastiCache or DynamoDB is better.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0135","card":"q-aws-015-load-balancing-dns-and-filtering","sec":"aws-s016","q":"Where should TLS terminate for a web application on AWS, and how do you get the certificate?","a":"<p>Usually on the load balancer or CloudFront. Request a public certificate in AWS Certificate Manager (ACM), validate the domain by DNS and attach it to the HTTPS listener. ACM renews it automatically, and the private key never sits on your instances. Re-encrypt to the targets if compliance needs encryption all the way, and use an NLB TLS listener when you need TCP-level control.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0136","card":"q-aws-015-load-balancing-dns-and-filtering","sec":"aws-s016","q":"A backend behind an ALB needs the original client IP address. How does it get it?","a":"<p>The ALB opens its own connection to the target, so the target sees the ALB's address. The ALB adds the client IP to the <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> header (and the protocol in <code>X-Forwarded-Proto</code>), and the application or web server must read it, trusting only the headers added by the load balancer. An NLB can preserve the source IP for instance targets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0137","card":"q-aws-015-load-balancing-dns-and-filtering","sec":"aws-s016","q":"A client behind an ALB gets 502, 503 or 504. What does each usually mean?","a":"<p>502 Bad Gateway means the target closed the connection or returned an invalid response. 503 Service Unavailable means there are no healthy or registered targets. 504 Gateway Timeout means the target did not reply within the ALB idle timeout (60 seconds by default). Check target health, the application logs, and keep the application's keep-alive timeout longer than the ALB's idle timeout.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0138","card":"q-aws-015-load-balancing-dns-and-filtering","sec":"aws-s016","q":"How would you configure an ALB listener so that <code>/api/*</code> goes to one service and <code>/app/*</code> to another?","a":"<p>Add one target group for each service and, on the HTTPS listener, create two rules with a path-pattern condition each, forwarding to the matching target group, plus a default action for everything else. Rules are evaluated in priority order, so the lowest number matches first. Host-header conditions work the same way for different domains.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0139","card":"q-aws-015-load-balancing-dns-and-filtering","sec":"aws-s016","q":"In Route 53, when would you use an alias record instead of a CNAME to point a name at an ALB or CloudFront distribution?","a":"<p>Use an alias record. A CNAME is not allowed at the zone apex (for example <code>example.com</code>), but an alias record is. An alias is a Route 53 extension that points to AWS resources such as ALB, CloudFront, S3 website endpoints or another record in the zone. Queries to alias records pointing at AWS resources are free, it can follow the target's IP changes automatically, and it can evaluate the target's health. Use a CNAME only for names outside AWS or for non-apex names where an alias is not available.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0140","card":"q-aws-016-rds-and-aurora","sec":"aws-s017","q":"What is Amazon RDS and which databases does it support?","a":"<p>Managed relational databases: MySQL, PostgreSQL, MariaDB, Oracle, SQL Server and Aurora.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0141","card":"q-aws-016-rds-and-aurora","sec":"aws-s017","q":"What is Multi-AZ in RDS?","a":"<p>Enable Multi-AZ and RDS syncs the primary to a standby in another AZ for failover.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0142","card":"q-aws-016-rds-and-aurora","sec":"aws-s017","q":"Aurora vs. RDS?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Aurora</strong>: faster, scalable, six-copy storage.</li>\n  <li><strong>RDS</strong>: standard engines, simpler.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0143","card":"q-aws-016-rds-and-aurora","sec":"aws-s017","q":"What is the difference between RDS Multi-AZ and a read replica?","a":"<p>Multi-AZ is for high availability: a standby in another AZ receives synchronous replication and takes over on failure, but it does not serve reads in the classic single-standby setup. A read replica is for scaling reads: it replicates asynchronously, serves read traffic, can be in another Region and can be promoted to a standalone database. Replica lag means reads can be slightly stale.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0144","card":"q-aws-016-rds-and-aurora","sec":"aws-s017","q":"How do automated backups differ from manual snapshots in RDS?","a":"<p>Automated backups run daily within a window, include transaction logs for point-in-time restore, and are deleted after the retention period (up to 35 days) or when you delete the instance, unless you keep them. Manual snapshots stay until you delete them. Restoring always creates a new DB instance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0145","card":"q-aws-016-rds-and-aurora","sec":"aws-s017","q":"Why does Aurora usually give better availability than standard RDS?","a":"<p>Aurora storage is shared across the cluster and keeps six copies of the data across three AZs, self-heals, and replicas read the same storage, so failover to a replica typically takes tens of seconds. Aurora also scales storage automatically up to 128 TiB. The trade-off is a different cost model and less engine choice (MySQL and PostgreSQL compatible).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0146","card":"q-aws-016-rds-and-aurora","sec":"aws-s017","q":"How does an application connect to RDS or Aurora so that a failover does not require a code change?","a":"<p>Use the DNS endpoint, not an IP. RDS Multi-AZ failover updates the DNS name to the new primary, and Aurora provides a cluster endpoint (writer), a reader endpoint (load-balanced across replicas) and instance endpoints. The application must reconnect and retry after the connection drops.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0147","card":"q-aws-016-rds-and-aurora","sec":"aws-s017","q":"What is RDS Proxy and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>RDS Proxy pools and shares database connections. It helps Lambda functions and bursty apps that open many short connections and exhaust the database's connection limit, and it speeds up failover by keeping connections alive. It sits between the app and the database and works with IAM auth and Secrets Manager.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0148","card":"q-aws-016-rds-and-aurora","sec":"aws-s017","q":"Can you make an unencrypted RDS instance encrypted later?","a":"<p>Not in place. Take a snapshot, copy it with encryption enabled using a KMS key, and restore a new instance from the encrypted copy, then switch the app to it. Decide on encryption at creation time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0149","card":"q-aws-016-rds-and-aurora","sec":"aws-s017","q":"What is an RDS parameter group and what is the catch with changing parameters?","a":"<p>A parameter group is a named set of engine settings (for example <code>max_connections</code> or the character set) applied to one or more DB instances. Dynamic parameters apply immediately, while static parameters take effect only after a reboot, so you must plan a maintenance window. The default parameter group cannot be edited, so create a custom one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0150","card":"q-aws-016-rds-and-aurora","sec":"aws-s017","q":"What does RDS storage autoscaling do, and what must you set?","a":"<p>It grows allocated storage automatically when free space runs low, so the database does not stop for lack of space. You set a maximum storage threshold. It only grows, never shrinks, and there is a cooldown between increases, so monitoring free storage is still needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0151","card":"q-aws-016-rds-and-aurora","sec":"aws-s017","q":"When would you use Aurora Serverless v2 and when a provisioned Aurora cluster?","a":"<p>Serverless v2 scales compute in fine-grained Aurora Capacity Units within a minimum and maximum range, so it fits variable, unpredictable or development workloads and avoids over-provisioning. A provisioned cluster with reserved instances is cheaper for a steady, high load. Serverless v2 instances can be mixed with provisioned ones in one cluster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0152","card":"q-aws-016-rds-and-aurora","sec":"aws-s017","q":"What is an Aurora Global Database and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>It spans several Regions: one primary Region accepts writes and up to five secondary Regions get storage-level replication, typically with under a second of lag. It gives low-latency local reads for users around the world and a disaster-recovery option with an RPO of around a second and an RTO of minutes through promotion of a secondary Region. Secondary Regions are read-only unless you enable write forwarding, which sends writes to the primary.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0153","card":"q-aws-016-rds-and-aurora","sec":"aws-s017","q":"Why do RDS backups and restores matter differently, and how do you test them?","a":"<p>A backup only counts if a restore works within your recovery time. Restoring a snapshot or point-in-time creates a new instance and can take from minutes to hours depending on size, so measure it. Run restore drills, check that the application works against the restored copy and compare the measured time with the RTO you promised.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0154","card":"q-aws-016-rds-and-aurora","sec":"aws-s017","q":"In Amazon RDS, how do you find out why a database is slow, and what do Performance Insights and Enhanced Monitoring each show?","a":"<p>Performance Insights shows database load (average active sessions) broken down by SQL statement, wait event, host and user, so it tells you which queries and waits cause the load. Enhanced Monitoring shows operating-system metrics of the instance (CPU per process, memory, file system, disk I/O) at intervals as low as one second. Use Performance Insights to find the slow query, then Enhanced Monitoring to see whether the host is short on CPU, memory or I/O. Standard CloudWatch metrics give the overview and alarms.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0155","card":"q-aws-017-dynamodb-and-redshift","sec":"aws-s018","q":"What is DynamoDB and when do you use it over RDS?","a":"<p>A NoSQL database that scales fast with low latency. Use it for unstructured, high-traffic data, where RDS is relational.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0156","card":"q-aws-017-dynamodb-and-redshift","sec":"aws-s018","q":"How does DynamoDB scale and partition?","a":"<p>On-demand or provisioned scaling. Partitions are derived from the key and split automatically for load.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0157","card":"q-aws-017-dynamodb-and-redshift","sec":"aws-s018","q":"What is Amazon Redshift and its use cases?","a":"<p>A data warehouse for analytics and BI on large datasets.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0158","card":"q-aws-017-dynamodb-and-redshift","sec":"aws-s018","q":"SQL vs. NoSQL in AWS?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>SQL (RDS)</strong>: structured data, joins.</li>\n  <li><strong>NoSQL (DynamoDB)</strong>: flexible schema, scales.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0159","card":"q-aws-017-dynamodb-and-redshift","sec":"aws-s018","q":"What is a partition key and a sort key in DynamoDB, and how do they affect data modeling?","a":"<p>The partition key decides which partition stores an item, and the key plus the sort key form the primary key. The sort key keeps items with the same partition key ordered and allows range queries (<code>begins_with</code>, <code>between</code>). You design the keys around your access patterns up front because DynamoDB lacks joins and ad hoc queries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0160","card":"q-aws-017-dynamodb-and-redshift","sec":"aws-s018","q":"What is a hot partition in DynamoDB and how do you avoid it?","a":"<p>A hot partition is one key that gets far more traffic than others and is throttled even though total capacity is available. Avoid it with a high-cardinality partition key that spreads requests evenly, write sharding (adding a suffix to the key), and caching for hot reads with DAX. Adaptive capacity helps but does not remove the problem.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0161","card":"q-aws-017-dynamodb-and-redshift","sec":"aws-s018","q":"What is the difference between Query and Scan in DynamoDB?","a":"<p>Query reads items with a given partition key (and optional sort key condition), so it reads only the matching items and is efficient. Scan reads the whole table and filters afterwards, using capacity for every item read, so it is slow and costly on large tables. Design the keys or an index so you can use Query.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0162","card":"q-aws-017-dynamodb-and-redshift","sec":"aws-s018","q":"What is the difference between a local and a global secondary index in DynamoDB?","a":"<p>A local secondary index uses the same partition key with a different sort key, must be created with the table, and shares its capacity. A global secondary index has a different partition and sort key, can be added any time, has its own capacity, and is eventually consistent. Use a GSI for a new access pattern.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0163","card":"q-aws-017-dynamodb-and-redshift","sec":"aws-s018","q":"When do you choose DynamoDB on-demand over provisioned capacity?","a":"<p>On-demand suits new or unpredictable workloads, since you pay per request and need no capacity planning. Provisioned (with auto scaling or reserved capacity) is cheaper for steady, predictable traffic. You can switch between modes with limits on how often.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0164","card":"q-aws-017-dynamodb-and-redshift","sec":"aws-s018","q":"Why is Redshift not a replacement for RDS in an OLTP application?","a":"<p>Redshift is a columnar data warehouse built for OLAP: large analytical queries that scan many rows and a few columns. It is slow for single-row inserts and updates and many small concurrent transactions. Use RDS or Aurora for transactional workloads and move data to Redshift for reporting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0165","card":"q-aws-017-dynamodb-and-redshift","sec":"aws-s018","q":"What is DAX and when is it useful?","a":"<p>DAX is an in-memory cache for DynamoDB that serves reads in microseconds without changing the application's data model. It helps read-heavy, repeated-key access. It does not help write-heavy workloads or strongly consistent reads, which go to the table.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0166","card":"q-aws-017-dynamodb-and-redshift","sec":"aws-s018","q":"How do eventually consistent and strongly consistent reads differ in DynamoDB?","a":"<p>An eventually consistent read (the default) may return data a moment out of date because replicas of the item are updated slightly after the write, and it costs half the read capacity. A strongly consistent read returns the latest committed write, costs twice as much, can have higher latency, and is not available on global secondary indexes or across Regions in global tables. Use strong consistency only when stale data is harmful.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0167","card":"q-aws-017-dynamodb-and-redshift","sec":"aws-s018","q":"How do you calculate DynamoDB read and write capacity units?","a":"<p>One write capacity unit is one write per second of an item up to 1 KB. One read capacity unit is one strongly consistent read per second of up to 4 KB, or two eventually consistent reads. Larger items round up to the next unit. For example, 100 writes per second of 3 KB items need 300 WCU, and 80 eventually consistent reads per second of 8 KB items need 80 RCU.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0168","card":"q-aws-017-dynamodb-and-redshift","sec":"aws-s018","q":"What are DynamoDB Streams and what are they used for?","a":"<p>A stream is an ordered, 24-hour log of item-level changes (new and old images) for a table. It lets Lambda react to changes, replicate data to other stores, build search indexes or audit trails and maintain aggregates. Records appear in order per item, but a retried Lambda batch can process records again, so consumers should be idempotent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0169","card":"q-aws-017-dynamodb-and-redshift","sec":"aws-s018","q":"How does DynamoDB TTL work and when is it useful?","a":"<p>You designate an attribute holding an expiry time in epoch seconds, and DynamoDB deletes expired items in the background at no write cost, typically within a few days of expiry rather than at the exact second. It suits sessions, temporary tokens and old events. Filter out expired items in queries if you must not read them before deletion.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0170","card":"q-aws-017-dynamodb-and-redshift","sec":"aws-s018","q":"What are DynamoDB transactions and conditional writes, and when would you use them?","a":"<p>A conditional write (<code>ConditionExpression</code>) succeeds only if a condition on the item holds, which gives optimistic locking with a version attribute or prevents overwriting an existing key. <code>TransactWriteItems</code> and <code>TransactGetItems</code> group up to 100 operations across tables into an all-or-nothing, isolated action, at about twice the capacity cost. Use them for things like moving a balance between two items.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0171","card":"q-aws-017-dynamodb-and-redshift","sec":"aws-s018","q":"What are DynamoDB Global Tables and what are their conflict rules?","a":"<p>A global table replicates a table across chosen Regions, each one accepting reads and writes (multi-active), with typically sub-second replication. It gives low-latency local access and Regional disaster recovery. If the same item is written in two Regions at the same time, the last writer wins by timestamp, so design for that or route each key's writes to one Region.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0172","card":"q-aws-017-dynamodb-and-redshift","sec":"aws-s018","q":"What is the maximum size of a DynamoDB item and how do you store larger data?","a":"<p>An item can be at most 400 KB, including attribute names. Store large payloads in S3 and keep the S3 key plus metadata in the item, or split the data across items that share a partition key.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0173","card":"q-aws-017-dynamodb-and-redshift","sec":"aws-s018","q":"What is the difference between DynamoDB point-in-time recovery and on-demand backups?","a":"<p>Point-in-time recovery (PITR) continuously backs up the table and lets you restore to any second within the last 35 days (you choose the window up to 35 days). On-demand backups are full snapshots you take when you want and keep until you delete them, so they suit long-term retention and milestones. Both restore into a new table, never over the existing one, and you must re-create some settings such as autoscaling policies and alarms. PITR is off by default and must be enabled.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0174","card":"q-aws-018-caching-migration-and-security","sec":"aws-s019","q":"What is Amazon ElastiCache?","a":"<p>An in-memory cache (Redis or Memcached) that speeds up apps by reducing database load.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0175","card":"q-aws-018-caching-migration-and-security","sec":"aws-s019","q":"How do you migrate an on-prem database to AWS?","a":"<p>Use DMS: bulk move, then sync changes, then switch the app.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0176","card":"q-aws-018-caching-migration-and-security","sec":"aws-s019","q":"How do you secure an AWS database?","a":"<p>IAM, encryption (KMS and SSL), private subnet, logs and backups.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0177","card":"q-aws-018-caching-migration-and-security","sec":"aws-s019","q":"What is the difference between ElastiCache for Redis and for Memcached?","a":"<p>Redis supports rich data types (lists, sets, sorted sets), persistence, replication with failover and pub/sub, and is the default choice. Memcached is a simple multi-threaded key-value cache with no replication or persistence, used for simple horizontal caching. If you need durability, failover or data structures, choose Redis.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0178","card":"q-aws-018-caching-migration-and-security","sec":"aws-s019","q":"Explain the cache-aside (lazy loading) pattern and one downside.","a":"<p>The app checks the cache first. On a hit it returns the value. On a miss it reads the database, writes the result to the cache with a TTL and returns it. The downside is that the first request is slow and cached data can be stale until the TTL expires or you invalidate it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0179","card":"q-aws-018-caching-migration-and-security","sec":"aws-s019","q":"What is the difference between a cache-aside and a write-through cache strategy?","a":"<p>In cache-aside the cache fills on read misses, so only requested data is cached but it can be stale. In write-through every write goes to both the cache and the database, so the cache is always fresh but you cache data that may never be read and each write is slower.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0180","card":"q-aws-018-caching-migration-and-security","sec":"aws-s019","q":"What does the AWS Database Migration Service do, and what is the Schema Conversion Tool for?","a":"<p>DMS moves data from a source database to a target, with full load and ongoing change data capture so the source can stay online during migration. The Schema Conversion Tool converts schema and code when engines differ (for example Oracle to PostgreSQL). For same-engine migrations DMS alone may be enough.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0181","card":"q-aws-018-caching-migration-and-security","sec":"aws-s019","q":"What would you put in place to secure a database in a private subnet that the application tier reaches?","a":"<p>Put the database in private subnets with a security group that allows the DB port only from the application's security group, encrypt storage with KMS and connections with TLS, use IAM or Secrets Manager for credentials, turn on automated backups and audit logging, and deny public accessibility.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0182","card":"q-aws-018-caching-migration-and-security","sec":"aws-s019","q":"What is a cache stampede and how do you reduce it?","a":"<p>When a popular key expires, many requests miss at once and all hit the database to rebuild it, which can overload it. Reduce it by adding random jitter to TTLs, using a lock or single-flight so only one request refills the key, refreshing hot keys in the background before expiry, or serving the stale value briefly while one request refreshes it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0183","card":"q-aws-019-iam","sec":"aws-s020","q":"What is AWS IAM and how do permissions work?","a":"<p>It controls access. Users, roles and policies define the allowed actions.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0184","card":"q-aws-019-iam","sec":"aws-s020","q":"IAM policies, roles and users?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Policies</strong>: JSON rules.</li>\n  <li><strong>Users</strong>: individual identities.</li>\n  <li><strong>Roles</strong>: temporary access for services.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0185","card":"q-aws-019-iam","sec":"aws-s020","q":"How do you apply least privilege?","a":"<p>Limit permissions to the essentials with specific policies, and review them with Access Analyzer.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0186","card":"q-aws-019-iam","sec":"aws-s020","q":"How does MFA work in AWS?","a":"<p>It adds a second factor. Enable it in IAM and scan the QR code in an authenticator app.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0187","card":"q-aws-019-iam","sec":"aws-s020","q":"How does IAM policy evaluation work when one policy allows an action and another denies it?","a":"<p>Requests are denied by default. An explicit allow grants access, but an explicit deny always wins over any allow. Within one account, access is granted when an identity-based or resource-based policy allows and nothing denies. Permission boundaries and service control policies can limit what is possible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0188","card":"q-aws-019-iam","sec":"aws-s020","q":"Why use an IAM role for an EC2 instance rather than storing access keys on it?","a":"<p>A role gives the instance temporary credentials delivered through the instance metadata service and rotated automatically, so there is no long-lived secret on disk to leak or rotate. Keys in files, images or source control are a common cause of breaches.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0189","card":"q-aws-019-iam","sec":"aws-s020","q":"What is the following policy statement doing, and is it a good practice?","a":"<p>It allows every S3 action on every bucket and object in the account. That breaks least privilege. Narrow the actions (for example <code>s3:GetObject</code>) and the resource to the specific bucket ARN and prefix the workload needs.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;,\n  &quot;Action&quot;: &quot;s3:*&quot;,\n  &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;*&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0190","card":"q-aws-019-iam","sec":"aws-s020","q":"What is the difference between an identity-based policy and a resource-based policy in IAM?","a":"<p>An identity-based policy is attached to a user, group or role and defines what it can do. A resource-based policy is attached to a resource such as an S3 bucket, SQS queue or KMS key and defines who can access it, including principals in other accounts. For cross-account access, a resource policy can grant access directly, while the role approach requires the caller to assume a role.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0191","card":"q-aws-019-iam","sec":"aws-s020","q":"How does cross-account access with an IAM role work?","a":"<p>The target account creates a role with a trust policy naming the other account (or a principal in it) and a permissions policy for what the role can do. A principal in the other account calls <code>sts:AssumeRole</code> and receives temporary credentials. The caller also needs permission to call <code>AssumeRole</code> on that role.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0192","card":"q-aws-019-iam","sec":"aws-s020","q":"What is a permission boundary and how is it different from a service control policy?","a":"<p>A permission boundary is a managed policy that sets the maximum permissions an IAM user or role can have, even if its own policies give more. It is used to delegate role creation safely. A service control policy applies to accounts in AWS Organizations and sets the maximum for everything in the account. Neither grants permissions by itself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0193","card":"q-aws-019-iam","sec":"aws-s020","q":"Why should you avoid using the root user for daily work?","a":"<p>The root user has unrestricted access that cannot be limited by IAM policies. Protect it with MFA, no access keys, and use it only for the few tasks that require it (such as changing the account's support plan or closing the account). Use IAM Identity Center or IAM roles for daily work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0194","card":"q-aws-019-iam","sec":"aws-s020","q":"What is the difference between IAM users, groups and roles, and when do you use each?","a":"<p>A user is a long-lived identity for one person or program with its own credentials. A group is a collection of users used to attach permissions once, and it cannot be a principal. A role has no credentials of its own and is assumed to receive temporary ones, for AWS services, other accounts or federated users. Prefer roles and Identity Center over IAM users with access keys.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0195","card":"q-aws-019-iam","sec":"aws-s020","q":"How do you enforce MFA for sensitive actions with an IAM policy?","a":"<p>Add a condition such as <code>\"Bool\": {\"aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent\": \"true\"}</code> to an allow statement, or a deny statement with <code>BoolIfExists</code> set to false for the dangerous actions, so a request without MFA is refused. Users sign in with an MFA device (a TOTP app, a passkey or a hardware key), and for CLI use they get temporary credentials through <code>sts:GetSessionToken</code> or a role session with the MFA code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0196","card":"q-aws-019-iam","sec":"aws-s020","q":"What can IAM policy conditions restrict, and can you give examples?","a":"<p>A condition limits when a statement applies, using keys such as <code>aws:SourceIp</code> (only from the corporate range), <code>aws:SecureTransport</code> (HTTPS only), <code>aws:RequestedRegion</code> (approved Regions), <code>aws:PrincipalOrgID</code> (only principals in your organization), <code>aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent</code> and resource tags. Conditions add least privilege without creating many policies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0197","card":"q-aws-019-iam","sec":"aws-s020","q":"What is IAM Access Analyzer and what does it find?","a":"<p>It uses automated reasoning to find resources shared with an external principal outside your account or organization, such as S3 buckets, IAM roles, KMS keys and SQS queues, and it can report unused access and validate policies before you deploy them. Use it to catch accidental public or cross-account exposure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0198","card":"q-aws-019-iam","sec":"aws-s020","q":"What is the confused deputy problem in cross-account roles and how does an external ID help?","a":"<p>A third-party service that assumes roles in many customers' accounts could be tricked into using one customer's role on behalf of another. The role's trust policy requires a unique <code>sts:ExternalId</code> that only the real customer and the service know, so a different customer cannot make the service assume your role. For AWS services use <code>aws:SourceArn</code> and <code>aws:SourceAccount</code> conditions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0199","card":"q-aws-019-iam","sec":"aws-s020","q":"What is the difference between an AWS managed policy, a customer managed policy and an inline policy?","a":"<p>AWS managed policies are created and updated by AWS and are reusable but broad. Customer managed policies are yours, reusable, versioned (up to five versions) and can be tightened. An inline policy is embedded in a single user, group or role and deleted with it, so it suits strict one-to-one relationships. Prefer customer managed policies for production roles.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0200","card":"q-aws-019-iam","sec":"aws-s020","q":"Why is IAM Identity Center or SAML/OIDC federation preferred over creating an IAM user for each employee?","a":"<p>Employees sign in with the company identity provider and receive temporary credentials by assuming roles. There are no long-lived access keys to leak or rotate, offboarding happens in one place, and MFA and group membership are managed centrally. IAM Identity Center adds single sign-on across many accounts with permission sets. IAM users are kept for few cases, such as break-glass access or systems that cannot use roles.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0201","card":"q-aws-020-keys-secrets-and-protection","sec":"aws-s021","q":"What is AWS KMS?","a":"<p>It manages encryption keys and encrypts data in S3, RDS and more.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0202","card":"q-aws-020-keys-secrets-and-protection","sec":"aws-s021","q":"How do you rotate keys in AWS?","a":"<p>KMS rotates yearly (automatic) or you rotate manually: create a new key and update apps.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0203","card":"q-aws-020-keys-secrets-and-protection","sec":"aws-s021","q":"AWS Secrets Manager vs. KMS?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Secrets Manager</strong>: stores and rotates secrets.</li>\n  <li><strong>KMS</strong>: manages keys.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0204","card":"q-aws-020-keys-secrets-and-protection","sec":"aws-s021","q":"What is AWS Shield?","a":"<p>DDoS protection. Standard is free; Advanced is paid and more robust.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0205","card":"q-aws-020-keys-secrets-and-protection","sec":"aws-s021","q":"AWS WAF vs. a traditional firewall?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>WAF</strong>: Layer 7 (HTTP) filtering.</li>\n  <li><strong>Traditional</strong>: network-level control.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0206","card":"q-aws-020-keys-secrets-and-protection","sec":"aws-s021","q":"How do you audit and monitor security?","a":"<p>CloudTrail (API logs), CloudWatch (alerts) and Config (compliance).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0207","card":"q-aws-020-keys-secrets-and-protection","sec":"aws-s021","q":"What is the difference between an AWS managed key and a customer managed key in KMS?","a":"<p>AWS managed keys are created and rotated by AWS for a service (such as <code>aws/s3</code>), you cannot edit their policy and they rotate every year. Customer managed keys are created by you, with a key policy you control, optional rotation, and the ability to disable or schedule deletion. Choose customer managed keys when you need control, cross-account use or audit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0208","card":"q-aws-020-keys-secrets-and-protection","sec":"aws-s021","q":"Why use Secrets Manager rather than environment variables or a config file for database passwords?","a":"<p>Secrets Manager stores secrets encrypted with KMS, controls who can read them with IAM, logs access in CloudTrail and can rotate secrets automatically (with Lambda for RDS). Environment variables and files are stored in plain text in task definitions, images or repos and do not rotate. The trade-off is a per-secret cost and an API call to fetch the value.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0209","card":"q-aws-020-keys-secrets-and-protection","sec":"aws-s021","q":"What is the difference between Secrets Manager and Systems Manager Parameter Store?","a":"<p>Parameter Store holds configuration and secrets as strings (<code>SecureString</code> for encrypted values), is cheaper (standard parameters are free), and has no built-in rotation. Secrets Manager costs per secret but adds automatic rotation and cross-Region replication. Use Parameter Store for configuration and simple secrets, Secrets Manager when rotation matters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0210","card":"q-aws-020-keys-secrets-and-protection","sec":"aws-s021","q":"What does AWS Shield Advanced add over Shield Standard?","a":"<p>Standard is automatic and free and protects against common layer 3 and 4 DDoS attacks. Advanced adds more detection and mitigation for larger attacks on specific resources (CloudFront, Route 53, ELB, Elastic IP), access to the Shield Response Team, cost protection for scaling charges during attacks and integration with WAF.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0211","card":"q-aws-020-keys-secrets-and-protection","sec":"aws-s021","q":"What kinds of threats do you block with AWS WAF rules?","a":"<p>WAF works at layer 7 on CloudFront, ALB, API Gateway and AppSync. Use managed rule groups and custom rules for SQL injection, cross-site scripting, bad bots, rate limiting per IP, geo restrictions and IP allow or block lists. It does not stop volumetric network floods, which is what Shield covers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0212","card":"q-aws-020-keys-secrets-and-protection","sec":"aws-s021","q":"What is GuardDuty and how does it differ from Inspector and Macie?","a":"<p>GuardDuty detects threats by analyzing CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs and DNS logs (for example unusual API calls or crypto-mining traffic). Inspector scans EC2 instances, container images and Lambda for software vulnerabilities. Macie finds sensitive data such as PII in S3.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0213","card":"q-aws-020-keys-secrets-and-protection","sec":"aws-s021","q":"How does KMS key rotation work and does it affect existing data?","a":"<p>When rotation is on, KMS creates new key material for the same key (same key ID and ARN) and keeps the old material to decrypt data encrypted earlier, so no re-encryption or application change is needed. For customer managed symmetric keys, automatic rotation is optional and defaults to yearly. Rotation does not help if a key is compromised. In that case create a new key and re-encrypt the data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0214","card":"q-aws-020-keys-secrets-and-protection","sec":"aws-s021","q":"How do KMS key policies and IAM policies work together?","a":"<p>Every KMS key has a key policy, and it is the main control. IAM policies grant access to a key only if the key policy allows the account to use IAM policies, which the default key policy does by trusting the account root. For another account, both the key policy and an IAM policy in the calling account must allow the action. Using a key requires <code>kms:Decrypt</code> or <code>kms:GenerateDataKey</code> in addition to the permissions of the service, such as S3.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0215","card":"q-aws-020-keys-secrets-and-protection","sec":"aws-s021","q":"What is a KMS grant and when is it used?","a":"<p>A grant gives a principal temporary and narrowly scoped permission to use a key, without editing the key policy. AWS services use grants, for example EBS asking KMS to decrypt a volume key for an instance, and you can create them programmatically and revoke them. Grants suit delegated or short-lived access that is created and removed by code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0216","card":"q-aws-020-keys-secrets-and-protection","sec":"aws-s021","q":"How does automatic rotation of a secret in Secrets Manager work?","a":"<p>Secrets Manager invokes a rotation Lambda function on a schedule, and the function runs four steps: create a new secret version, set the new password in the database, test it and finish by moving the <code>AWSCURRENT</code> label to the new version. Clients that read <code>AWSCURRENT</code> get the new value. Applications should fetch the secret at runtime or cache it with a short TTL, and the function needs network access to the database.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0217","card":"q-aws-021-cloudwatch-and-cloudtrail","sec":"aws-s022","q":"What is Amazon CloudWatch?","a":"<p>It monitors metrics and logs for EC2, RDS, Lambda and more.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0218","card":"q-aws-021-cloudwatch-and-cloudtrail","sec":"aws-s022","q":"How do you set up a CloudWatch alarm?","a":"<p>Pick a metric (for example CPU), set a threshold and an action (for example SNS).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0219","card":"q-aws-021-cloudwatch-and-cloudtrail","sec":"aws-s022","q":"CloudTrail vs. CloudWatch?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>CloudTrail</strong>: API activity logs.</li>\n  <li><strong>CloudWatch</strong>: performance metrics.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0220","card":"q-aws-021-cloudwatch-and-cloudtrail","sec":"aws-s022","q":"Metric vs. log in CloudWatch?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Metric</strong>: numbers (CPU).</li>\n  <li><strong>Log</strong>: text (errors).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0221","card":"q-aws-021-cloudwatch-and-cloudtrail","sec":"aws-s022","q":"How do you set up centralized logging?","a":"<p>A CloudWatch Logs group, streamed through the agent, with S3 or Kinesis for scale.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0222","card":"q-aws-021-cloudwatch-and-cloudtrail","sec":"aws-s022","q":"What are the three types of data CloudWatch collects and what is each used for?","a":"<p>Metrics are numeric time series (CPU, request count) used for graphs and alarms. Logs are text events from applications and services, searched with Logs Insights. Events (now EventBridge) are state changes that trigger actions. Alarms act on metrics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0223","card":"q-aws-021-cloudwatch-and-cloudtrail","sec":"aws-s022","q":"A team needs to know who deleted a security group yesterday. Which service answers that and how?","a":"<p>CloudTrail. It records management API calls with the identity, time, source IP and request parameters. Search the event history (the last 90 days) for <code>DeleteSecurityGroup</code>, or query a trail stored in S3 with Athena for longer retention. CloudWatch does not record who made an API call.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0224","card":"q-aws-021-cloudwatch-and-cloudtrail","sec":"aws-s022","q":"How do you keep CloudTrail logs trustworthy for an audit?","a":"<p>Create a trail that delivers to an S3 bucket in a protected account, enable log file validation (digest files to detect tampering), encrypt with KMS, restrict the bucket policy, and make the trail multi-Region. Event history alone keeps only 90 days of management events.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0225","card":"q-aws-021-cloudwatch-and-cloudtrail","sec":"aws-s022","q":"What is the difference between standard and high-resolution CloudWatch metrics, and what is a custom metric?","a":"<p>Standard metrics have a resolution of one minute (EC2 basic monitoring is five minutes, detailed is one minute), and high-resolution custom metrics can be down to one second. Custom metrics are values you publish yourself with <code>PutMetricData</code> or the agent, such as memory usage or business counts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0226","card":"q-aws-021-cloudwatch-and-cloudtrail","sec":"aws-s022","q":"What is a metric filter in CloudWatch Logs?","a":"<p>A metric filter scans log events for a pattern (such as <code>ERROR</code>) and turns matches into a CloudWatch metric, which can then drive an alarm. It lets you alert on application errors without changing the application code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0227","card":"q-aws-021-cloudwatch-and-cloudtrail","sec":"aws-s022","q":"Why should you set a retention period on CloudWatch Logs log groups?","a":"<p>Log groups keep events forever by default and storage is billed per GB, so cost grows unnoticed. Set a retention period (for example 30 days) that matches your needs, and export older logs to S3 for cheaper long-term storage. Retention is set per log group, so enforce it with infrastructure as code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0228","card":"q-aws-021-cloudwatch-and-cloudtrail","sec":"aws-s022","q":"What is a CloudWatch composite alarm and why use one?","a":"<p>A composite alarm combines the states of other alarms with <code>AND</code>, <code>OR</code> and <code>NOT</code> rules. It reduces noise: for example alert only when high CPU and high latency fire together, or mute child alarms during maintenance. Page people from the composite alarm and keep the child alarms for dashboards.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0229","card":"q-aws-021-cloudwatch-and-cloudtrail","sec":"aws-s022","q":"How would you set up alerting when an application writes an error pattern to CloudWatch Logs?","a":"<p>Create a metric filter on the log group that counts matching events and publishes a metric, then create an alarm on that metric with a threshold and period, and send the action to an SNS topic that notifies email, chat or a paging tool. Treat missing data deliberately, because a metric with no events may report no data instead of zero.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0230","card":"q-aws-022-management-tools","sec":"aws-s023","q":"How does AWS Systems Manager help with EC2?","a":"<p>The SSM Agent runs commands, applies patches and opens sessions remotely.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0231","card":"q-aws-022-management-tools","sec":"aws-s023","q":"What is AWS Trusted Advisor?","a":"<p>It recommends cost, performance and security fixes.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0232","card":"q-aws-022-management-tools","sec":"aws-s023","q":"What is AWS Config?","a":"<p>It tracks resource configurations and flags compliance drift.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0233","card":"q-aws-022-management-tools","sec":"aws-s023","q":"How do you automate with the AWS CLI and SDKs?","a":"<p>Script the tasks, for example <code>aws ec2 start-instances</code>, or Boto3 code.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0234","card":"q-aws-022-management-tools","sec":"aws-s023","q":"How do you troubleshoot a failed deployment?","a":"<p>Check CloudWatch logs and CloudTrail, then roll back with the deployment tool.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0235","card":"q-aws-022-management-tools","sec":"aws-s023","q":"What is the benefit of Systems Manager Session Manager over SSH?","a":"<p>It gives a shell on an instance with no open inbound port, no bastion host and no SSH keys. Access is controlled by IAM, and sessions can be logged to CloudTrail, S3 or CloudWatch Logs. The instance needs the SSM Agent and an instance profile with SSM permissions, plus a route to SSM endpoints.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0236","card":"q-aws-022-management-tools","sec":"aws-s023","q":"What is Systems Manager Patch Manager used for?","a":"<p>It defines patch baselines and schedules, and applies OS patches across fleets of instances in maintenance windows, reporting compliance. It replaces logging in to each server and keeps instance patching consistent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0237","card":"q-aws-022-management-tools","sec":"aws-s023","q":"What does AWS Config do that CloudTrail does not?","a":"<p>Config records the configuration state of resources over time and evaluates it against rules (for example, \"S3 buckets must not be public\", \"EBS volumes must be encrypted\"), flagging non-compliant resources. CloudTrail records who made which API call. Together they answer what the configuration is and who changed it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0238","card":"q-aws-022-management-tools","sec":"aws-s023","q":"Which Trusted Advisor checks are available on every support plan?","a":"<p>Basic and Developer support plans get a limited set of core checks (such as service limits and some security checks, like open ports and S3 permissions). The full set of cost, performance, fault tolerance and security checks needs Business or Enterprise support.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0239","card":"q-aws-022-management-tools","sec":"aws-s023","q":"What is the difference between the AWS CLI and an SDK like Boto3, and when do you choose each?","a":"<p>The CLI is for one-off commands and shell scripts. An SDK such as Boto3 (Python) is for application code and complex logic, with exceptions, pagination helpers, waiters and easier testing. Both use the same credential chain and call the same APIs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0240","card":"q-aws-023-availability-and-scaling","sec":"aws-s024","q":"How do you build a highly available app on AWS?","a":"<p>Multi-AZ ELB, Auto Scaling, RDS Multi-AZ and Route 53.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0241","card":"q-aws-023-availability-and-scaling","sec":"aws-s024","q":"Horizontal vs. vertical scaling?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Horizontal</strong>: more instances.</li>\n  <li><strong>Vertical</strong>: a bigger instance.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0242","card":"q-aws-023-availability-and-scaling","sec":"aws-s024","q":"Stateless vs. stateful apps?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Stateless</strong>: no stored memory (Lambda).</li>\n  <li><strong>Stateful</strong>: retains data (RDS).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0243","card":"q-aws-023-availability-and-scaling","sec":"aws-s024","q":"What is AWS Global Accelerator?","a":"<p>It routes users to the nearest edge for low latency and failover.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0244","card":"q-aws-023-availability-and-scaling","sec":"aws-s024","q":"What does a highly available three-tier web application on AWS look like?","a":"<p>Route 53 for DNS, optionally CloudFront and WAF in front, an ALB in public subnets across at least two AZs, web or app instances in an Auto Scaling group in private subnets, and a Multi-AZ RDS or Aurora database in private subnets. Add ElastiCache for read load and S3 for static content.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0245","card":"q-aws-023-availability-and-scaling","sec":"aws-s024","q":"When do you scale horizontally rather than vertically, and what is the limit of vertical scaling?","a":"<p>Scale horizontally (more instances) when the application is stateless and can be load balanced, as it gives more availability and almost no ceiling. Vertical scaling (a bigger instance) is simple and works for databases or legacy apps, but it needs a restart, has an instance-size limit and remains a single point of failure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0246","card":"q-aws-023-availability-and-scaling","sec":"aws-s024","q":"How does a stateless application store user sessions so that instances can be added or removed?","a":"<p>Move sessions out of the instance memory into a shared store such as ElastiCache (Redis) or DynamoDB, or use signed tokens (JWT) kept by the client. Sticky sessions on a load balancer are a weaker option because instances that hold the session cannot be removed freely.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0247","card":"q-aws-023-availability-and-scaling","sec":"aws-s024","q":"How is AWS Global Accelerator different from CloudFront?","a":"<p>CloudFront caches content at edge locations, so it speeds up cacheable HTTP content. Global Accelerator gives two static anycast IPs and routes TCP/UDP traffic over the AWS backbone to the nearest healthy endpoint (ALB, NLB, EC2), with no caching. Use it for non-cacheable traffic, gaming or fast regional failover.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0248","card":"q-aws-023-availability-and-scaling","sec":"aws-s024","q":"What is a single point of failure and how do you find them in an AWS design?","a":"<p>A single component whose failure takes the system down, such as one instance, one AZ, a single NAT gateway, or a non-replicated database. Review each tier and ask \"what if this fails?\", then add redundancy across AZs, health checks and automated failover.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0249","card":"q-aws-023-availability-and-scaling","sec":"aws-s024","q":"How does CloudFront improve performance and cost for a web application, and what are the limits?","a":"<p>It caches content at edge locations close to users, so responses are faster and fewer requests reach the origin, and it uses the AWS backbone to the origin for non-cacheable requests. It can also terminate TLS, apply WAF rules and shield S3 behind Origin Access Control. Dynamic, personalized content gains less from caching, so use cache keys, TTLs and cache-control headers deliberately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0250","card":"q-aws-024-cost-and-anti-patterns","sec":"aws-s025","q":"What does a cost-effective architecture look like?","a":"<p>Right-size, use Reserved or Spot capacity, go serverless and tier storage.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0251","card":"q-aws-024-cost-and-anti-patterns","sec":"aws-s025","q":"What are common AWS architecture anti-patterns?","a":"<p>Overprovisioning, no elasticity, weak security and no IaC.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0252","card":"q-aws-024-cost-and-anti-patterns","sec":"aws-s025","q":"What are the main levers for reducing an AWS bill without hurting the service?","a":"<p>Right-size instances using utilization data, use Savings Plans or Reserved Instances for steady load, Spot for flexible work, turn off idle resources on a schedule, move cold data to cheaper S3 classes, delete unattached volumes and old snapshots, and reduce data transfer (for example with VPC endpoints and CloudFront).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0253","card":"q-aws-024-cost-and-anti-patterns","sec":"aws-s025","q":"Which hidden costs often surprise teams on AWS?","a":"<p>Data transfer between AZs and out to the internet, NAT Gateway processing charges, idle load balancers and unattached Elastic IPs or EBS volumes, over-retained logs and snapshots, and request-heavy S3 or DynamoDB usage. Cost Explorer and tags show where the spend goes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0254","card":"q-aws-024-cost-and-anti-patterns","sec":"aws-s025","q":"Why is \"lift and shift with oversized instances and no Auto Scaling\" considered an anti-pattern?","a":"<p>It copies the on-premises habit of provisioning for peak load, so you pay for idle capacity and gain none of the elasticity and managed-service benefits of the cloud. Right-size, add Auto Scaling and adopt managed services where it makes sense.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0255","card":"q-aws-024-cost-and-anti-patterns","sec":"aws-s025","q":"Why is manually configuring resources in the console considered an anti-pattern for production?","a":"<p>Manual changes are not repeatable, not reviewed, cause drift between environments and are hard to audit or roll back. Use infrastructure as code (CloudFormation, Terraform) in version control with review and a pipeline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0256","card":"q-aws-025-microservices-serverless-migration-and-d","sec":"aws-s026","q":"How do you run microservices on AWS?","a":"<p>ECS or EKS, Lambda, API Gateway and SQS. Scalable and independent.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0257","card":"q-aws-025-microservices-serverless-migration-and-d","sec":"aws-s026","q":"What is a serverless architecture on AWS?","a":"<p>Lambda, API Gateway, DynamoDB, S3 and Step Functions.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0258","card":"q-aws-025-microservices-serverless-migration-and-d","sec":"aws-s026","q":"How do you migrate a monolith to AWS?","a":"<p>Lift-and-shift to EC2, then refactor into microservices (ECS, Lambda).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0259","card":"q-aws-025-microservices-serverless-migration-and-d","sec":"aws-s026","q":"What is the disaster recovery approach in AWS?","a":"<p>Multi-region deployment, S3 replication, RDS replicas and Route 53 failover.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0260","card":"q-aws-025-microservices-serverless-migration-and-d","sec":"aws-s026","q":"What are the benefits and costs of microservices compared with a monolith?","a":"<p>Benefits: independent deployment and scaling, a small blast radius, and teams that can own services and choose technology. Costs: network calls instead of function calls, distributed tracing and debugging, data consistency across services and operational overhead. A small team with a simple domain is often better off with a monolith first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0261","card":"q-aws-025-microservices-serverless-migration-and-d","sec":"aws-s026","q":"Why put a queue like SQS between two services instead of calling one from the other directly?","a":"<p>A queue decouples them: the producer is not blocked, spikes are absorbed, the consumer scales on queue depth, and messages are retried if the consumer fails. The trade-offs are asynchronous behavior, possible duplicate delivery (standard queues are at-least-once) and the need for idempotent consumers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0262","card":"q-aws-025-microservices-serverless-migration-and-d","sec":"aws-s026","q":"What is the difference between SQS, SNS and EventBridge?","a":"<p>SQS is a queue where consumers pull messages, one consumer processes each message. SNS is pub/sub that pushes one message to many subscribers (including SQS queues, Lambda, email). EventBridge is an event bus with content-based routing rules, SaaS and AWS service events and schemas. A common pattern is SNS fan-out to several SQS queues.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0263","card":"q-aws-025-microservices-serverless-migration-and-d","sec":"aws-s026","q":"Name the four disaster recovery strategies on AWS from cheapest to fastest recovery.","a":"<p>Backup and restore (hours of recovery, lowest cost), pilot light (core data replicated, compute kept off until needed), warm standby (a scaled-down copy running) and multi-site active-active (full capacity in two Regions, near-zero downtime, highest cost). Choose by the RTO and RPO you need.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0264","card":"q-aws-025-microservices-serverless-migration-and-d","sec":"aws-s026","q":"What do RTO and RPO mean?","a":"<p>RTO (recovery time objective) is how long you can be down. RPO (recovery point objective) is how much data loss, measured in time, you can accept. Tighter targets need more replication and standby capacity, so cost rises.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0265","card":"q-aws-025-microservices-serverless-migration-and-d","sec":"aws-s026","q":"What are the \"6 Rs\" of migration to AWS?","a":"<p>Rehost (lift and shift), replatform (small optimizations, such as moving to RDS), repurchase (move to SaaS), refactor (re-architect to cloud-native), retire (turn off), and retain (keep on-premises for now). The choice per application balances effort, risk and benefit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0266","card":"q-aws-025-microservices-serverless-migration-and-d","sec":"aws-s026","q":"What is the difference between SQS standard and FIFO queues, and when do you pick FIFO?","a":"<p>Standard queues offer nearly unlimited throughput with at-least-once delivery and best-effort ordering, so messages can be duplicated or reordered. FIFO queues keep order within a message group and deduplicate within a five-minute window (exactly-once processing), at lower throughput limits (higher with batching and high-throughput mode). Pick FIFO when order or duplicates matter, such as account transactions, and standard for everything else.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0267","card":"q-aws-025-microservices-serverless-migration-and-d","sec":"aws-s026","q":"What is a dead-letter queue in SQS and how do you configure it?","a":"<p>A dead-letter queue (DLQ) is a separate queue that receives messages the consumer failed to process after a number of attempts. On the source queue set a redrive policy with the DLQ ARN and <code>maxReceiveCount</code>. It stops a poison message from looping forever, and you set an alarm on the DLQ depth, inspect messages, fix the bug and redrive them. A FIFO queue needs a FIFO DLQ.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0268","card":"q-aws-025-microservices-serverless-migration-and-d","sec":"aws-s026","q":"How do you choose the visibility timeout of an SQS queue?","a":"<p>The visibility timeout hides a received message from other consumers while one works on it. Set it longer than the processing time of a message (Lambda recommends at least six times the function timeout). If it is too short, another consumer receives the same message and work is duplicated. If it is too long, a crashed consumer's message waits longer before a retry.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0269","card":"q-aws-025-microservices-serverless-migration-and-d","sec":"aws-s026","q":"When would you choose Kinesis Data Streams over SQS?","a":"<p>Choose Kinesis Data Streams when you need ordered records per shard (partition key), replay of data within the retention period (24 hours by default, up to 365 days), or several independent consumers reading the same stream. SQS fits simple work queues: each message is processed by one consumer and then deleted, with no replay, and it scales with no shard management. Kinesis needs shard capacity planning unless you use on-demand mode.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0270","card":"q-aws-026-code-services","sec":"aws-s027","q":"What is AWS CodePipeline?","a":"<p>A CI/CD service that integrates CodeCommit, CodeBuild and CodeDeploy.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0271","card":"q-aws-026-code-services","sec":"aws-s027","q":"How do you build CI/CD with CodeBuild and CodeDeploy?","a":"<p>Pipeline stages: Source (CodeCommit), Build (CodeBuild), Deploy (CodeDeploy).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0272","card":"q-aws-026-code-services","sec":"aws-s027","q":"What is AWS CodeStar?","a":"<p>It unifies pipeline setup: CodeCommit, CodeBuild and hosting.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0273","card":"q-aws-026-code-services","sec":"aws-s027","q":"What is the role of CodeBuild in a pipeline and how is a build defined?","a":"<p>CodeBuild is a managed build service that runs commands in a container to compile, test and package code, with no build servers to manage and billing per build minute. Commands are defined in a <code>buildspec.yml</code> with phases (<code>install</code>, <code>pre_build</code>, <code>build</code>, <code>post_build</code>) and artifacts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0274","card":"q-aws-026-code-services","sec":"aws-s027","q":"What does CodeDeploy need to deploy to EC2, and what defines its steps?","a":"<p>Instances need the CodeDeploy agent and an IAM instance profile. An <code>appspec.yml</code> file defines which files to copy and the lifecycle hooks (such as <code>BeforeInstall</code>, <code>AfterInstall</code>, <code>ApplicationStart</code>, <code>ValidateService</code>) to run scripts. The deployment can be in-place or blue/green.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0275","card":"q-aws-026-code-services","sec":"aws-s027","q":"In CodePipeline, what is the difference between a stage, an action and an artifact?","a":"<p>A stage is a group of actions such as Source, Build or Deploy. An action is a single task in a stage (build with CodeBuild, deploy with CodeDeploy). Artifacts are the files passed between actions, stored in S3. A manual approval action can gate a stage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0276","card":"q-aws-027-deployment-and-rollback","sec":"aws-s028","q":"Blue/green vs. rolling deployment?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Blue/green</strong>: full switch, no downtime.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rolling</strong>: gradual, partial risk.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0277","card":"q-aws-027-deployment-and-rollback","sec":"aws-s028","q":"How do you roll back a failed deployment?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>CodeDeploy</strong>: switch back (blue/green).</li>\n  <li><strong>CloudFormation</strong>: revert the stack.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0278","card":"q-aws-027-deployment-and-rollback","sec":"aws-s028","q":"Compare in-place, rolling, blue/green and canary deployments.","a":"<p>In-place updates existing instances, which is simple but has downtime or reduced capacity. Rolling updates in batches, so capacity drops during deploy. Blue/green starts a full new environment and switches traffic at once, giving fast rollback by switching back, at the cost of double capacity for a while. Canary sends a small share of traffic to the new version first, then increases it, limiting the blast radius.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0279","card":"q-aws-027-deployment-and-rollback","sec":"aws-s028","q":"How does blue/green deployment on AWS usually switch traffic?","a":"<p>By changing the target: moving ALB listener rules or weights between two target groups, changing weighted Route 53 records, or letting CodeDeploy shift traffic on an ECS service or Lambda alias. Keep the old environment for a while for instant rollback.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0280","card":"q-aws-027-deployment-and-rollback","sec":"aws-s028","q":"What is a database change challenge in blue/green and how do you handle it?","a":"<p>Both versions of the application may share one database, so schema changes must be backward compatible. Use the expand and contract approach: add new columns or tables first, deploy code that works with both, then remove old structures later. This keeps rollback possible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0281","card":"q-aws-027-deployment-and-rollback","sec":"aws-s028","q":"What triggers an automatic rollback in CodeDeploy?","a":"<p>You configure rollback on deployment failure or on CloudWatch alarm thresholds, such as an error rate alarm. CodeDeploy then redeploys the last known good revision, or for blue/green shifts traffic back to the original environment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0282","card":"q-aws-028-iac-and-containers","sec":"aws-s029","q":"How is CloudFormation used for IaC?","a":"<p>Templates define resources. It provisions, updates and rolls back.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0283","card":"q-aws-028-iac-and-containers","sec":"aws-s029","q":"What are the benefits of AWS OpsWorks?","a":"<p>Managed Chef or Puppet that automates deployment and scaling.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0284","card":"q-aws-028-iac-and-containers","sec":"aws-s029","q":"How do Docker and ECS work together on AWS?","a":"<p>Containers package apps, ECS runs them and scales them from ECR images.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0285","card":"q-aws-028-iac-and-containers","sec":"aws-s029","q":"How do you automate infrastructure provisioning?","a":"<p>CloudFormation or Terraform, CLI scripts and Auto Scaling.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0286","card":"q-aws-028-iac-and-containers","sec":"aws-s029","q":"How do you handle secrets in CI/CD?","a":"<p>Secrets Manager, IAM roles and KMS encryption.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0287","card":"q-aws-028-iac-and-containers","sec":"aws-s029","q":"What are the benefits of infrastructure as code compared with console changes?","a":"<p>Repeatability across environments, version control and peer review, automated testing, quick recovery or rebuild, documentation by code and fewer manual errors. It also lets you use the same pipeline discipline as for application code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0288","card":"q-aws-028-iac-and-containers","sec":"aws-s029","q":"How do CloudFormation and Terraform compare, and how do you choose?","a":"<p>CloudFormation is AWS-native, free, with stack-level rollback and no state file to manage, but only covers AWS. Terraform is multi-cloud with a large provider ecosystem, a plan step and modules, but you must manage and secure its state file (usually in S3 with locking). Choose CloudFormation for AWS-only shops that want a managed service, Terraform for multi-cloud or an existing Terraform skill set.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0289","card":"q-aws-028-iac-and-containers","sec":"aws-s029","q":"What is a CloudFormation change set and why use it?","a":"<p>A change set previews what an update will create, modify or replace before you apply it. It helps you notice that a property change forces a replacement, which could delete a database, before running the update.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0290","card":"q-aws-028-iac-and-containers","sec":"aws-s029","q":"What is CloudFormation drift detection?","a":"<p>It compares the actual configuration of the stack's resources with the template and reports resources changed outside CloudFormation, such as by hand in the console. You then either update the template to match or fix the resource.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0291","card":"q-aws-028-iac-and-containers","sec":"aws-s029","q":"Why would you build a custom Docker image and push to ECR instead of installing software on instances at boot?","a":"<p>An image is built and tested once and runs the same everywhere, starts faster and avoids boot-time failures from package downloads. Versioned image tags also make rollbacks simple. The same idea applies to baked AMIs for EC2.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0292","card":"q-aws-028-iac-and-containers","sec":"aws-s029","q":"What is the AWS CDK and how does it relate to CloudFormation?","a":"<p>The CDK lets you define infrastructure in a programming language such as TypeScript or Python with reusable constructs. It synthesizes to a CloudFormation template that CloudFormation then deploys, so you get CloudFormation's rollback with loops, functions and types.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0293","card":"q-aws-029-migration-and-scale","sec":"aws-s030","q":"How do you migrate a 10 TB database with minimal downtime?","a":"<p>Snowball for the bulk, DMS for sync, then switch in a maintenance window.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0294","card":"q-aws-029-migration-and-scale","sec":"aws-s030","q":"Design a web app for 1M users daily.","a":"<p>ELB, Auto Scaling EC2, RDS Multi-AZ, ElastiCache and S3 with CloudFront.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0295","card":"q-aws-029-migration-and-scale","sec":"aws-s030","q":"How do you handle a traffic spike?","a":"<p>Auto Scaling, RDS replicas, CloudFront and Lambda.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0296","card":"q-aws-029-migration-and-scale","sec":"aws-s030","q":"How would you approach migrating a large on-premises database with minimal downtime?","a":"<p>Assess the source, choose a target engine, bulk-load the data (Snowball if bandwidth is limited, or DMS full load), keep it in sync with DMS change data capture, test the application against the target, and cut over during a short window by stopping writes, letting replication drain and pointing the app to the new endpoint. Keep a rollback plan.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0297","card":"q-aws-029-migration-and-scale","sec":"aws-s030","q":"How would you design a web application for about one million daily users?","a":"<p>Use Route 53 and CloudFront with static assets in S3, an ALB across multiple AZs, a stateless app tier in an Auto Scaling group, a Multi-AZ Aurora or RDS database with read replicas, ElastiCache for hot data and a queue for background work. Estimate the peak requests per second from daily users, not only the daily total, and load test.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0298","card":"q-aws-029-migration-and-scale","sec":"aws-s030","q":"A product launch will multiply traffic tenfold in minutes. What do you do before and during?","a":"<p>Before: load test, raise service quotas, pre-warm or pre-scale (scheduled scaling, ALB pre-warming through AWS Support if needed), add caching and CDN, and tune the database (read replicas, connection pooling). During: watch CloudWatch dashboards and alarms and keep a runbook to scale or shed load. Auto Scaling reacts in minutes, so a sudden spike needs pre-provisioned capacity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0299","card":"q-aws-029-migration-and-scale","sec":"aws-s030","q":"Why does the database often become the bottleneck as an application scales out, and what are your options?","a":"<p>Application servers scale horizontally, but a relational database has a single writer. Options include read replicas for reads, caching, connection pooling with RDS Proxy, query and index tuning, sharding or partitioning, a larger instance, and moving suitable data to DynamoDB.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0300","card":"q-aws-030-troubleshooting-and-security","sec":"aws-s031","q":"How do you troubleshoot an unreachable EC2 instance?","a":"<p>Check status, Security Groups, routes, IP and logs. Restart if needed.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0301","card":"q-aws-030-troubleshooting-and-security","sec":"aws-s031","q":"How do you fix slow application performance?","a":"<p>Use CloudWatch and X-Ray, then scale EC2, add caching and optimize the database.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0302","card":"q-aws-030-troubleshooting-and-security","sec":"aws-s031","q":"How do you secure an S3 plus RDS app?","a":"<p>IAM, encryption, private subnets and restricted access.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0303","card":"q-aws-030-troubleshooting-and-security","sec":"aws-s031","q":"An S3 bucket was made public. What do you do?","a":"<p>Remove public access, turn on the block-public-access settings and audit with CloudTrail.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0304","card":"q-aws-030-troubleshooting-and-security","sec":"aws-s031","q":"An EC2 instance in a public subnet cannot be reached over SSH. In what order do you check?","a":"<p>Check the instance's state and status checks, the security group (inbound port 22 from your IP), the NACL (inbound and outbound ephemeral ports), the subnet route table to the Internet Gateway, the instance having a public or Elastic IP, the key pair and user name, and finally the console or system log. VPC Flow Logs show whether traffic is accepted or rejected.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0305","card":"q-aws-030-troubleshooting-and-security","sec":"aws-s031","q":"An EC2 instance in a private subnet cannot reach the internet to download packages. What do you check?","a":"<p>Whether the private subnet's route table sends <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> to a NAT Gateway, that the NAT Gateway is in a public subnet whose route table points to an Internet Gateway and that it has an Elastic IP, security group outbound rules, and NACL rules. For AWS services, a VPC endpoint is an alternative.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0306","card":"q-aws-030-troubleshooting-and-security","sec":"aws-s031","q":"A web application becomes slow under load. How do you find the cause?","a":"<p>Look at metrics for each tier: CPU and memory on the instances, load balancer latency and 5xx counts, database CPU, connections and slow queries, and cache hit rate. Use X-Ray or application tracing to see which call is slow. Then fix the bottleneck found: scale out, add caching or optimize queries and indexes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0307","card":"q-aws-030-troubleshooting-and-security","sec":"aws-s031","q":"An S3 bucket was found to be public. What steps do you take?","a":"<p>Turn on S3 Block Public Access, remove the offending policy or ACL, check CloudTrail data events and S3 access logs to see what was accessed, rotate any exposed credentials or data, and add AWS Config rules or Access Analyzer to detect public buckets in the future. Report the incident according to company process.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0308","card":"q-aws-030-troubleshooting-and-security","sec":"aws-s031","q":"Why does an application on EC2 get \"Access Denied\" calling S3 even though the bucket policy looks right?","a":"<p>Check the identity side: the instance role's policy must allow the action on that bucket ARN and the object path (<code>arn:aws:s3:::bucket</code> for list, <code>arn:aws:s3:::bucket/*</code> for objects). Also check for explicit denies (a bucket policy, an SCP, a permission boundary), the VPC endpoint policy, KMS key permissions for encrypted objects and the object owner (ACL).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0309","card":"q-aws-031-resilience-and-cost","sec":"aws-s032","q":"What is a backup and recovery strategy?","a":"<p>EBS snapshots, RDS backups, S3 versioning and Glacier.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0310","card":"q-aws-031-resilience-and-cost","sec":"aws-s032","q":"How do you reduce costs from overprovisioning?","a":"<p>Cost Explorer, right-sizing, Spot, stopping idle resources and tiering S3.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0311","card":"q-aws-031-resilience-and-cost","sec":"aws-s032","q":"How do you design multi-region DR?","a":"<p>Route 53 failover, S3 replication, RDS replicas and AMIs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0312","card":"q-aws-031-resilience-and-cost","sec":"aws-s032","q":"What is the difference between backup and replication for protecting data?","a":"<p>Replication copies data continuously to another place for availability, but it also copies mistakes such as deletes or corruption. A backup is a point-in-time copy you can restore from after such events. You normally need both, with backups kept separate (another account or Region, with immutability such as S3 Object Lock or Backup Vault Lock).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0313","card":"q-aws-031-resilience-and-cost","sec":"aws-s032","q":"What is AWS Backup and why use it?","a":"<p>AWS Backup is a central service that defines backup plans (schedule, retention, lifecycle) and applies them across services such as EBS, RDS, DynamoDB, EFS and S3, with cross-Region and cross-account copies and compliance reporting. It replaces many separate scripts and per-service settings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0314","card":"q-aws-031-resilience-and-cost","sec":"aws-s032","q":"How do you find and remove wasted resources in an AWS account?","a":"<p>Use Cost Explorer and Trusted Advisor or Compute Optimizer to find idle or oversized instances, list unattached EBS volumes, old snapshots and AMIs, unused Elastic IPs and idle load balancers, and enforce tags so each resource has an owner. Schedule non-production environments to stop at night.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0315","card":"q-aws-031-resilience-and-cost","sec":"aws-s032","q":"How does S3 Intelligent-Tiering reduce cost and what is the catch?","a":"<p>It moves each object automatically between access tiers based on access patterns, with no retrieval fee, so you save on data that goes cold without guessing. The catch is a small per-object monitoring charge and, for very small objects (under 128 KB), they are not monitored or moved, so it is a poor fit for many tiny objects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0316","card":"q-aws-032-ownership-and-deep-dives","sec":"aws-s033","q":"Tell me about a time you took ownership of a project.","a":"<p>Led an app migration: planned it, executed it and beat the deadline.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0317","card":"q-aws-032-ownership-and-deep-dives","sec":"aws-s033","q":"Tell me about a time you dove deep into a problem.","a":"<p>Fixed crashes by tracing a CPU spike and optimizing a query.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0318","card":"q-aws-032-ownership-and-deep-dives","sec":"aws-s033","q":"What is the STAR method and why is it useful when answering behavioral questions?","a":"<p>STAR stands for Situation, Task, Action and Result. It keeps the answer structured: set the context briefly, say what you were responsible for, describe the specific steps you took (use \"I\", not only \"we\"), and end with a measurable result and what you learned. It stops answers from rambling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0319","card":"q-aws-032-ownership-and-deep-dives","sec":"aws-s033","q":"In an Amazon-style interview, what does the Ownership leadership principle look for in an answer?","a":"<p>Owners act for the long term beyond their own role, do not say \"that is not my job\" and take responsibility for results, including fixing problems outside their own team when needed. A good story shows you noticed a problem, took initiative and followed it through to the outcome.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0320","card":"q-aws-032-ownership-and-deep-dives","sec":"aws-s033","q":"What does the Dive Deep leadership principle look for, and what makes a strong example?","a":"<p>It looks for staying close to the details, checking metrics and anecdotes against each other, and asking questions until the root cause is found. A strong example names the symptom, the data you inspected (logs, metrics, traces), the root cause you found and the lasting fix and prevention.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0321","card":"q-aws-033-disagreement-speed-and-customers","sec":"aws-s034","q":"How did you handle a disagreement?","a":"<p>Debated EC2 vs. Lambda, committed to the decision, optimized it and the team aligned later.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0322","card":"q-aws-033-disagreement-speed-and-customers","sec":"aws-s034","q":"Tell me about delivering a fast solution.","a":"<p>Built a dashboard in 5 days instead of 2 weeks using Glue, Athena and QuickSight.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0323","card":"q-aws-033-disagreement-speed-and-customers","sec":"aws-s034","q":"How did you ensure customer satisfaction?","a":"<p>Migrated an app with zero downtime and cost savings, with constant updates to the customer.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0324","card":"q-aws-033-disagreement-speed-and-customers","sec":"aws-s034","q":"In an Amazon-style behavioral interview, how do you answer \"Tell me about a time you disagreed with a teammate or manager\"?","a":"<p>Pick a real technical disagreement and show you argued with data, not opinion. Say what each side proposed (for instance EC2 versus Lambda), what evidence you brought (cost, latency, operational load), who decided, and that you committed fully once the decision was made. Close with the measured result and what you learned. This maps to the leadership principle \"Have Backbone; Disagree and Commit\". Do not make the other person the villain.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0325","card":"q-aws-033-disagreement-speed-and-customers","sec":"aws-s034","q":"When an interviewer asks for a time you delivered a solution fast, how do you show speed without sounding careless?","a":"<p>Show a deliberate trade-off. Name what you cut or deferred (scope, polish, scale), the reversible decisions you made quickly, and the managed services you reused instead of building (for instance Athena and QuickSight instead of a custom reporting stack). Then state the time saved and that quality held. This maps to \"Bias for Action\". The weak version is \"I worked long hours\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0326","card":"q-aws-033-disagreement-speed-and-customers","sec":"aws-s034","q":"How do you structure an answer about customer satisfaction on a migration project?","a":"<p>Start from the customer's goal and constraint (for instance zero downtime and lower cost), then describe what you did to meet it (planning, rehearsals, cutover plan, rollback), how you kept the customer informed, and the measurable outcome. This maps to \"Customer Obsession\". Interviewers look for the customer's problem driving your decisions, not your tooling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0327","card":"q-aws-034-create-an-ec2-instance","sec":"aws-s035","q":"How do you create an EC2 instance with the AWS CLI?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 run-instances \\\n    --image-id ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0 \\\n    --instance-type t2.micro \\\n    --key-name my-key-pair \\\n    --security-group-ids sg-12345678 \\\n    --subnet-id subnet-12345678 \\\n    --region us-east-1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0328","card":"q-aws-034-create-an-ec2-instance","sec":"aws-s035","q":"In the AWS CLI, why must the <code>--image-id</code> passed to <code>aws ec2 run-instances</code> be checked per Region?","a":"<p>AMI IDs are Region-specific. The same operating system image has a different ID in each Region, so an ID copied from another Region fails with an invalid AMI error. To reuse your own image elsewhere, copy it to the target Region, which creates a new AMI ID.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0329","card":"q-aws-034-create-an-ec2-instance","sec":"aws-s035","q":"What does <code>--subnet-id</code> decide when you run <code>aws ec2 run-instances</code>, and what happens if you leave it out?","a":"<p>The subnet decides the VPC and the Availability Zone of the instance, and the private IP range it gets its address from. If you leave it out, the instance is placed in a default subnet of the default VPC of that Region (if the account still has one), which is usually public. Production workloads should name the subnet explicitly.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 run-instances \\\n    --image-id ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0 \\\n    --instance-type t2.micro \\\n    --subnet-id subnet-12345678 \\\n    --region us-east-1</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0330","card":"q-aws-034-create-an-ec2-instance","sec":"aws-s035","q":"What is <code>--key-name</code> in <code>aws ec2 run-instances</code>, and what are the consequences of omitting it?","a":"<p>It names an existing EC2 key pair whose public key is injected into the instance for SSH login. Without it (and without another access method) you cannot SSH in with a key. Modern alternatives are Systems Manager Session Manager, which needs no key or open port 22, and EC2 Instance Connect.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0331","card":"q-aws-034-create-an-ec2-instance","sec":"aws-s035","q":"You ran <code>aws ec2 run-instances</code> and the command returned an instance ID, but the instance is not yet usable. What state does it move through, and how do you wait for it in a script?","a":"<p>It starts in <code>pending</code> and moves to <code>running</code>. Status checks then take a few more minutes to pass. In a script use <code>aws ec2 wait instance-running --instance-ids &lt;id&gt;</code> (and <code>wait instance-status-ok</code> for the checks) instead of sleeping for a fixed time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0332","card":"q-aws-035-create-an-rds-instance-and-a-read-replic","sec":"aws-s036","q":"How do you create an RDS instance with the AWS CLI?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws rds create-db-instance \\\n    --db-instance-identifier my-rds-instance \\\n    --db-instance-class db.t3.micro \\\n    --engine mysql \\\n    --master-username admin \\\n    --master-user-password &lt;master-password&gt; \\\n    --allocated-storage 20 \\\n    --vpc-security-group-ids sg-12345678 \\\n    --db-subnet-group-name my-subnet-group \\\n    --region us-east-1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0333","card":"q-aws-035-create-an-rds-instance-and-a-read-replic","sec":"aws-s036","q":"How do you create an RDS read replica for an existing instance?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws rds create-db-instance-read-replica \\\n    --db-instance-identifier my-rds-replica \\\n    --source-db-instance-identifier my-rds-instance \\\n    --db-instance-class db.t3.micro \\\n    --region us-east-1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0334","card":"q-aws-035-create-an-rds-instance-and-a-read-replic","sec":"aws-s036","q":"How do you attach a security group to an RDS instance?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws rds modify-db-instance \\\n    --db-instance-identifier my-rds-instance \\\n    --vpc-security-group-ids sg-12345678 \\\n    --apply-immediately \\\n    --region us-east-1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0335","card":"q-aws-035-create-an-rds-instance-and-a-read-replic","sec":"aws-s036","q":"In <code>aws rds create-db-instance</code>, what is <code>--db-subnet-group-name</code> and why is it required for a VPC database?","a":"<p>A DB subnet group is a set of subnets, covering at least two Availability Zones, that RDS may place the database in. RDS needs it to choose where to launch the instance and where to put the standby for Multi-AZ. Use private subnets so the database is not reachable from the internet.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0336","card":"q-aws-035-create-an-rds-instance-and-a-read-replic","sec":"aws-s036","q":"How should the master password be handled when creating an RDS instance from the CLI, instead of typing <code>--master-user-password</code> in a script?","a":"<p>Avoid putting a plaintext password in scripts and shell history. Either pass it from a secret store at run time, or use <code>--manage-master-user-password</code> so RDS creates and rotates the master secret in AWS Secrets Manager. Applications then read the secret at run time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0337","card":"q-aws-035-create-an-rds-instance-and-a-read-replic","sec":"aws-s036","q":"What must be true of the source database before you can create a read replica for an RDS MySQL instance?","a":"<p>Automated backups must be enabled on the source (backup retention period greater than 0), because replication uses the binary log. Replicas are asynchronous, so they can lag behind the source, and they can be promoted to standalone databases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0338","card":"q-aws-035-create-an-rds-instance-and-a-read-replic","sec":"aws-s036","q":"What does <code>--apply-immediately</code> do in <code>aws rds modify-db-instance</code>, and what is the default behavior without it?","a":"<p>Without the flag, modifications wait for the next maintenance window. With it, RDS applies the change now. Some changes can still cause a brief outage or reboot (for example an instance class change), so use it deliberately on production.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws rds modify-db-instance \\\n    --db-instance-identifier my-rds-instance \\\n    --vpc-security-group-ids sg-12345678 \\\n    --apply-immediately</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0339","card":"q-aws-035-create-an-rds-instance-and-a-read-replic","sec":"aws-s036","q":"How should an RDS read replica be sized relative to its source, and why can it lag?","a":"<p>Replication to a replica is asynchronous, so the replica can fall behind if it is smaller than the source or if the source takes heavy writes. Size it at least as large as the source for write-heavy workloads, and watch the <code>ReplicaLag</code> CloudWatch metric. Applications reading from a replica must tolerate slightly stale data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0340","card":"q-aws-036-create-an-s3-bucket","sec":"aws-s037","q":"How do you create an S3 bucket with the AWS CLI?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws s3api create-bucket \\\n    --bucket my-unique-bucket-name \\\n    --region us-east-1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0341","card":"q-aws-036-create-an-s3-bucket","sec":"aws-s037","q":"Why does <code>aws s3api create-bucket</code> need <code>--create-bucket-configuration LocationConstraint=&lt;region&gt;</code> outside <code>us-east-1</code>?","a":"<p>us-east-1 is the default Region for the API, so no constraint is needed there. For every other Region the bucket must be told where to be created, otherwise the call fails with an <code>IllegalLocationConstraintException</code>. The <code>--region</code> flag must match the constraint.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0342","card":"q-aws-036-create-an-s3-bucket","sec":"aws-s037","q":"What is the difference between <code>aws s3</code> and <code>aws s3api</code> in the AWS CLI?","a":"<p><code>aws s3</code> is the high-level layer with convenient commands (<code>cp</code>, <code>sync</code>, <code>rm</code>, <code>ls</code>) that handle multipart uploads and recursion. <code>aws s3api</code> maps one-to-one to S3 API operations (<code>create-bucket</code>, <code>put-bucket-policy</code>, <code>get-bucket-versioning</code>) and exposes every parameter. Use <code>s3</code> for moving data and <code>s3api</code> for configuration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0343","card":"q-aws-036-create-an-s3-bucket","sec":"aws-s037","q":"Which security settings does a bucket have by default when created today?","a":"<p>Since April 2023, new buckets have S3 Block Public Access enabled and ACLs disabled (bucket owner enforced). Since January 2023, all new objects are encrypted at rest with SSE-S3 by default. You still need to set the bucket policy and any versioning yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0344","card":"q-aws-036-create-an-s3-bucket","sec":"aws-s037","q":"What rule must a bucket name follow, and why does a creation call sometimes fail even with a valid name?","a":"<p>Bucket names must be globally unique across all AWS accounts in a partition, 3 to 63 characters, lower case letters, numbers and hyphens. The call fails if someone else already owns the name, so teams usually add an account ID or Region as a suffix.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0345","card":"q-aws-037-create-an-iam-role-and-attach-it-to-an-e","sec":"aws-s038","q":"How do you create an IAM role and attach it to an EC2 instance?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Create IAM role\naws iam create-role \\\n    --role-name ec2-role \\\n    --assume-role-policy-document &#x27;{&quot;Version&quot;:&quot;2012-10-17&quot;,&quot;Statement&quot;:[{&quot;Effect&quot;:&quot;Allow&quot;,&quot;Principal&quot;:{&quot;Service&quot;:&quot;ec2.amazonaws.com&quot;},&quot;Action&quot;:&quot;sts:AssumeRole&quot;}]}&#x27;\n# Attach policy to role\naws iam attach-role-policy \\\n    --role-name ec2-role \\\n    --policy-arn arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess\n# Create instance profile\naws iam create-instance-profile \\\n    --instance-profile-name ec2-instance-profile\n# Add role to instance profile\naws iam add-role-to-instance-profile \\\n    --instance-profile-name ec2-instance-profile \\\n    --role-name ec2-role\n# Attach to EC2 instance\naws ec2 associate-iam-instance-profile \\\n    --instance-id i-1234567890abcdef0 \\\n    --iam-instance-profile Name=ec2-instance-profile</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0346","card":"q-aws-037-create-an-iam-role-and-attach-it-to-an-e","sec":"aws-s038","q":"In <code>aws iam create-role</code>, what is the <code>--assume-role-policy-document</code> and how does it differ from the policy attached afterwards?","a":"<p>The assume-role (trust) policy says who may become the role: here the EC2 service. The permission policy attached with <code>attach-role-policy</code> says what the role may do once assumed, for instance read S3. Both are needed: trust without permissions can do nothing, and permissions without trust can never be used.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws iam create-role \\\n    --role-name ec2-role \\\n    --assume-role-policy-document &#x27;{&quot;Version&quot;:&quot;2012-10-17&quot;,&quot;Statement&quot;:[{&quot;Effect&quot;:&quot;Allow&quot;,&quot;Principal&quot;:{&quot;Service&quot;:&quot;ec2.amazonaws.com&quot;},&quot;Action&quot;:&quot;sts:AssumeRole&quot;}]}&#x27;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0347","card":"q-aws-037-create-an-iam-role-and-attach-it-to-an-e","sec":"aws-s038","q":"Why does an EC2 instance need an instance profile when the role already exists?","a":"<p>An instance profile is the container that passes an IAM role to an EC2 instance. EC2 can only attach profiles, not roles directly. The console creates a profile with the same name automatically, but the CLI makes you create the profile and add the role to it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0348","card":"q-aws-037-create-an-iam-role-and-attach-it-to-an-e","sec":"aws-s038","q":"In which order do the CLI commands for giving an EC2 instance S3 read access have to run, and why?","a":"<p>Create the role with its trust policy, attach the permission policy, create the instance profile, add the role to the profile, then associate the profile with the instance. Each step needs the output of the previous one to exist: you cannot add a role to a profile that does not exist, and you cannot attach a profile built from a role that is missing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0349","card":"q-aws-037-create-an-iam-role-and-attach-it-to-an-e","sec":"aws-s038","q":"How would you change the permissions of an EC2 instance without restarting it?","a":"<p>Edit the policies attached to the role. The change applies to the credentials the instance obtains from then on, usually within minutes. To swap to a different role, use <code>aws ec2 replace-iam-instance-profile-association</code>, which also needs no stop or start.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0350","card":"q-aws-037-create-an-iam-role-and-attach-it-to-an-e","sec":"aws-s038","q":"Why does a user who runs <code>aws ec2 run-instances</code> with an instance profile need the <code>iam:PassRole</code> permission?","a":"<p>Passing a role to a service lets that service act with the role's permissions. Without <code>iam:PassRole</code>, anyone who can launch instances could attach a more powerful role and escalate privileges. Grant <code>iam:PassRole</code> only for the specific role ARNs, and optionally restrict with the <code>iam:PassedToService</code> condition.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0351","card":"q-aws-038-create-and-attach-an-ebs-volume","sec":"aws-s039","q":"How do you create an EBS volume and attach it to an EC2 instance?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Create EBS volume\naws ec2 create-volume \\\n    --availability-zone us-east-1a \\\n    --size 10 \\\n    --volume-type gp3 \\\n    --region us-east-1\n# Attach volume to EC2 instance\naws ec2 attach-volume \\\n    --volume-id vol-1234567890abcdef0 \\\n    --instance-id i-1234567890abcdef0 \\\n    --device /dev/xvdf \\\n    --region us-east-1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0352","card":"q-aws-038-create-and-attach-an-ebs-volume","sec":"aws-s039","q":"After <code>aws ec2 attach-volume</code> succeeds, why can the instance still not use the disk, and what do you do?","a":"<p>A new EBS volume is a raw block device with no file system. Log in, find the device with <code>lsblk</code>, create a file system (for instance <code>mkfs -t xfs</code>), create a mount point, and mount it. Add an entry to <code>/etc/fstab</code> (using the UUID) so it mounts again after a reboot.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0353","card":"q-aws-038-create-and-attach-an-ebs-volume","sec":"aws-s039","q":"What is the <code>--device</code> value such as <code>/dev/xvdf</code> in <code>aws ec2 attach-volume</code>, and is it reliable inside the instance?","a":"<p>It is the device name requested at attach time. On Nitro-based instance types, volumes show up as NVMe devices such as <code>/dev/nvme1n1</code>, so the name inside the OS can differ. Use <code>lsblk</code> or the volume's UUID rather than relying on the requested name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0354","card":"q-aws-038-create-and-attach-an-ebs-volume","sec":"aws-s039","q":"Why choose <code>gp3</code> over <code>gp2</code> for a general-purpose EBS volume?","a":"<p>gp3 lets you set IOPS and throughput independently of size (baseline of 3,000 IOPS and 125 MiB/s), and it is priced lower per GB than gp2. With gp2, performance is tied to volume size. Use io2 or io1 only when you need higher, guaranteed IOPS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0355","card":"q-aws-039-create-a-vpc-with-a-public-subnet","sec":"aws-s040","q":"How do you create a VPC with a public subnet using the AWS CLI?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Create VPC\naws ec2 create-vpc \\\n    --cidr-block 10.0.0.0/16 \\\n    --region us-east-1\n# Create subnet\naws ec2 create-subnet \\\n    --vpc-id vpc-12345678 \\\n    --cidr-block 10.0.1.0/24 \\\n    --availability-zone us-east-1a \\\n    --region us-east-1\n# Create internet gateway\naws ec2 create-internet-gateway \\\n    --region us-east-1\n# Attach internet gateway to VPC\naws ec2 attach-internet-gateway \\\n    --internet-gateway-id igw-12345678 \\\n    --vpc-id vpc-12345678 \\\n    --region us-east-1\n# Create route table\naws ec2 create-route-table \\\n    --vpc-id vpc-12345678 \\\n    --region us-east-1\n# Add route to internet gateway\naws ec2 create-route \\\n    --route-table-id rtb-12345678 \\\n    --destination-cidr-block 0.0.0.0/0 \\\n    --gateway-id igw-12345678 \\\n    --region us-east-1\n# Associate route table with subnet\naws ec2 associate-route-table \\\n    --route-table-id rtb-12345678 \\\n    --subnet-id subnet-12345678 \\\n    --region us-east-1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0356","card":"q-aws-039-create-a-vpc-with-a-public-subnet","sec":"aws-s040","q":"What does <code>aws ec2 associate-route-table</code> do, and what happens to a subnet without an explicit association?","a":"<p>It links a route table to a subnet. A subnet with no explicit association uses the VPC's main route table. Keeping the main table private and creating a separate public route table is the safer pattern, so new subnets do not become public by accident.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0357","card":"q-aws-039-create-a-vpc-with-a-public-subnet","sec":"aws-s040","q":"What must the CIDR block of a subnet satisfy relative to the VPC CIDR?","a":"<p>It must be a subset of the VPC CIDR and must not overlap other subnets in the VPC. AWS reserves five addresses in every subnet (the first four and the last), so a /24 has 251 usable addresses.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0358","card":"q-aws-039-create-a-vpc-with-a-public-subnet","sec":"aws-s040","q":"Why does the VPC creation sequence put the internet gateway attach before the route to it?","a":"<p>The route target <code>igw-...</code> must be attached to the VPC for the route to be valid. Creating a route to a detached gateway fails or leaves the route inactive. In general, create the resource, attach it, then reference it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0359","card":"q-aws-039-create-a-vpc-with-a-public-subnet","sec":"aws-s040","q":"Which resources does AWS create automatically with a new VPC?","a":"<p>A main route table, a default network ACL (allowing all traffic) and a default security group. It does not create subnets (with the CLI), an internet gateway or NAT.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0360","card":"q-aws-039-create-a-vpc-with-a-public-subnet","sec":"aws-s040","q":"In an AWS VPC, why can an instance in a subnet routed to an internet gateway still have no public IPv4 address, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>A route to the internet gateway only makes a subnet public. The instance also needs a public IPv4 address, which the subnet hands out only if auto-assign is on. Enable it with <code>aws ec2 modify-subnet-attribute --subnet-id &lt;id&gt; --map-public-ip-on-launch</code>, request a public IP at launch, or attach an Elastic IP. Without a public address the instance cannot be reached from the internet or use the gateway to go out.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0361","card":"q-aws-040-create-and-attach-a-security-group","sec":"aws-s041","q":"How do you create a security group and attach it to an EC2 instance?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Create security group\naws ec2 create-security-group \\\n    --group-name my-security-group \\\n    --description &quot;My security group&quot; \\\n    --vpc-id vpc-12345678 \\\n    --region us-east-1\n# Add inbound rule\naws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress \\\n    --group-id sg-12345678 \\\n    --protocol tcp \\\n    --port 22 \\\n    --cidr 0.0.0.0/0 \\\n    --region us-east-1\n# Attach to EC2 instance\naws ec2 modify-instance-attribute \\\n    --instance-id i-1234567890abcdef0 \\\n    --groups sg-12345678 \\\n    --region us-east-1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0362","card":"q-aws-040-create-and-attach-a-security-group","sec":"aws-s041","q":"What is the problem with this rule, and how would you fix it?","a":"<p>It opens SSH to the whole internet. Restrict <code>--cidr</code> to your office or VPN range, or reference another security group instead of a CIDR, or remove port 22 entirely and use Systems Manager Session Manager. Bots scan for open SSH within minutes.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress \\\n    --group-id sg-12345678 \\\n    --protocol tcp \\\n    --port 22 \\\n    --cidr 0.0.0.0/0</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0363","card":"q-aws-040-create-and-attach-a-security-group","sec":"aws-s041","q":"What does <code>aws ec2 modify-instance-attribute --groups</code> do to security groups already on the instance?","a":"<p>It replaces the full set of groups on the instance's primary network interface. Any group you leave out of the list is removed, so include every group the instance should keep.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0364","card":"q-aws-040-create-and-attach-a-security-group","sec":"aws-s041","q":"Do security group changes require restarting the instance?","a":"<p>No. Rule changes and group changes apply immediately to the network interfaces, and because security groups are stateful, return traffic for an allowed connection is allowed automatically. Existing connections continue.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0365","card":"q-aws-040-create-and-attach-a-security-group","sec":"aws-s041","q":"What does a new security group allow by default?","a":"<p>It denies all inbound traffic and allows all outbound traffic. There are no deny rules, only allow rules, so access is granted by adding rules, never removed by adding deny rules. Use a network ACL if you need an explicit deny.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0366","card":"q-aws-040-create-and-attach-a-security-group","sec":"aws-s041","q":"Why does <code>aws ec2 create-security-group</code> ask for a <code>--vpc-id</code>?","a":"<p>Security groups are scoped to one VPC. In a non-default VPC you must give the VPC ID, and later refer to the group by ID rather than by name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0367","card":"q-aws-041-create-an-sns-topic-and-an-email-subscri","sec":"aws-s042","q":"How do you create an SNS topic and subscribe an email endpoint?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Create SNS topic\naws sns create-topic \\\n    --name my-topic \\\n    --region us-east-1\n# Subscribe email endpoint\naws sns subscribe \\\n    --topic-arn arn:aws:sns:us-east-1:123456789012:my-topic \\\n    --protocol email \\\n    --notification-endpoint user@example.com \\\n    --region us-east-1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0368","card":"q-aws-041-create-an-sns-topic-and-an-email-subscri","sec":"aws-s042","q":"After <code>aws sns subscribe --protocol email</code>, why does the subscriber receive nothing until they act?","a":"<p>Email subscriptions start as pending confirmation. SNS sends a confirmation message with a link, and messages are only delivered after the owner clicks it. Unconfirmed subscriptions are deleted after a while.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0369","card":"q-aws-041-create-an-sns-topic-and-an-email-subscri","sec":"aws-s042","q":"What is the fan-out pattern in SNS and when would you use it?","a":"<p>One SNS topic publishes a message to many subscribers at once, for instance several SQS queues, Lambda functions and an email address. Use it when one event must trigger several independent consumers, with each queue buffering at its own pace.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0370","card":"q-aws-041-create-an-sns-topic-and-an-email-subscri","sec":"aws-s042","q":"Why is an email subscription a poor choice for production alerting at scale?","a":"<p>Email is not reliable or structured. Mailboxes get filtered, nobody owns the inbox, and there is no acknowledgement or escalation. Route alarms to an incident tool (through an HTTPS endpoint or Lambda) or to chat integrations, and keep email as a fallback.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0371","card":"q-aws-041-create-an-sns-topic-and-an-email-subscri","sec":"aws-s042","q":"What allows a CloudWatch alarm or an S3 event to publish to an SNS topic, apart from the topic existing?","a":"<p>The topic access policy must allow the publishing service principal (for example <code>cloudwatch.amazonaws.com</code> or <code>s3.amazonaws.com</code>) with <code>sns:Publish</code>, ideally conditioned on the source ARN. Without it, the alarm action silently fails to notify.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0372","card":"q-aws-042-attach-an-elastic-ip","sec":"aws-s043","q":"How do you attach an Elastic IP to an EC2 instance?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Allocate Elastic IP\naws ec2 allocate-address \\\n    --domain vpc \\\n    --region us-east-1\n# Associate Elastic IP with instance\naws ec2 associate-address \\\n    --instance-id i-1234567890abcdef0 \\\n    --allocation-id eipalloc-12345678 \\\n    --region us-east-1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0373","card":"q-aws-042-attach-an-elastic-ip","sec":"aws-s043","q":"Why does <code>aws ec2 associate-address</code> use an <code>--allocation-id</code> in a VPC?","a":"<p>Allocating an Elastic IP creates an allocation with its own ID in your account, and associating uses that ID. The allocation stays yours until you release it, independent of any instance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0374","card":"q-aws-042-attach-an-elastic-ip","sec":"aws-s043","q":"How can an Elastic IP be used for quick failover?","a":"<p>Re-associate it from a failed instance to a standby with <code>associate-address</code> (with <code>--allow-reassociation</code>). Clients keep using the same public address, so no DNS change is needed. A load balancer with health checks is usually the better design.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0375","card":"q-aws-042-attach-an-elastic-ip","sec":"aws-s043","q":"Why should you generally avoid hard-wiring a public Elastic IP into a design?","a":"<p>It ties clients to one instance and defeats scaling. Use a DNS name pointing to an ALB or NLB (an NLB can also have static Elastic IPs if clients need fixed addresses to allow-list).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0376","card":"q-aws-043-create-an-ecs-cluster-and-run-a-fargate","sec":"aws-s044","q":"How do you create an ECS cluster and deploy a task definition?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Create ECS cluster\naws ecs create-cluster \\\n    --cluster-name my-ecs-cluster \\\n    --region us-east-1\n# Register task definition\naws ecs register-task-definition \\\n    --family my-task \\\n    --network-mode awsvpc \\\n    --requires-compatibilities FARGATE \\\n    --cpu &quot;256&quot; \\\n    --memory &quot;512&quot; \\\n    --execution-role-arn arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/ecs-task-execution-role \\\n    --container-definitions &#x27;[{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;my-container&quot;,&quot;image&quot;:&quot;amazon/amazon-ecs-sample&quot;,&quot;essential&quot;:true,&quot;portMappings&quot;:[{&quot;containerPort&quot;:80,&quot;hostPort&quot;:80}]}]&#x27; \\\n    --region us-east-1\n# Run task\naws ecs run-task \\\n    --cluster my-ecs-cluster \\\n    --task-definition my-task \\\n    --launch-type FARGATE \\\n    --network-configuration &quot;awsvpcConfiguration={subnets=[subnet-12345678],securityGroups=[sg-12345678],assignPublicIp=ENABLED}&quot; \\\n    --region us-east-1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0377","card":"q-aws-043-create-an-ecs-cluster-and-run-a-fargate","sec":"aws-s044","q":"What does <code>--network-mode awsvpc</code> mean for an ECS task, and why does Fargate require it?","a":"<p>Each task gets its own elastic network interface with a private IP in your VPC, and its own security group. This gives per-task network isolation and lets you use ordinary VPC controls. Fargate supports only <code>awsvpc</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0378","card":"q-aws-043-create-an-ecs-cluster-and-run-a-fargate","sec":"aws-s044","q":"In the Fargate <code>run-task</code> command, why is <code>assignPublicIp=ENABLED</code> set, and what is the alternative?","a":"<p>The task needs a route to pull its image and send logs. In a public subnet that means a public IP. In a private subnet, keep it <code>DISABLED</code> and use a NAT gateway or VPC endpoints for ECR, S3 and CloudWatch Logs. Private subnets are preferred for production.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ecs run-task \\\n    --cluster my-ecs-cluster \\\n    --task-definition my-task \\\n    --launch-type FARGATE \\\n    --network-configuration &quot;awsvpcConfiguration={subnets=[subnet-12345678],securityGroups=[sg-12345678],assignPublicIp=ENABLED}&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0379","card":"q-aws-043-create-an-ecs-cluster-and-run-a-fargate","sec":"aws-s044","q":"What does <code>\"essential\": true</code> mean in an ECS container definition?","a":"<p>If an essential container stops or fails, ECS stops the whole task. Mark the main application container essential. Sidecar containers that may end (such as an init job) can be non-essential.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0380","card":"q-aws-043-create-an-ecs-cluster-and-run-a-fargate","sec":"aws-s044","q":"What is the difference between <code>aws ecs run-task</code> and creating an ECS service?","a":"<p><code>run-task</code> starts a task once, which ends when its process exits (batch jobs, one-off tasks). A service keeps a desired number of tasks running, replaces failed ones, and can register them with a load balancer and scale them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0381","card":"q-aws-043-create-an-ecs-cluster-and-run-a-fargate","sec":"aws-s044","q":"Why does a Fargate task definition require <code>cpu</code> and <code>memory</code> at the task level, and what restriction applies?","a":"<p>Fargate bills and provisions by task size. CPU and memory must be one of the supported pairs (for instance 256 CPU units with 512 MB to 2 GB). An invalid combination is rejected at registration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0382","card":"q-aws-044-launch-template-and-auto-scaling-group","sec":"aws-s045","q":"How do you create an Auto Scaling group from an EC2 launch template?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Create launch template\naws ec2 create-launch-template \\\n    --launch-template-name my-launch-template \\\n    --version-description &quot;v1&quot; \\\n    --launch-template-data &#x27;{&quot;ImageId&quot;:&quot;ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0&quot;,&quot;InstanceType&quot;:&quot;t2.micro&quot;,&quot;KeyName&quot;:&quot;my-key-pair&quot;,&quot;SecurityGroupIds&quot;:[&quot;sg-12345678&quot;]}&#x27; \\\n    --region us-east-1\n# Create Auto Scaling group\naws autoscaling create-auto-scaling-group \\\n    --auto-scaling-group-name my-asg \\\n    --launch-template LaunchTemplateName=my-launch-template,Version=1 \\\n    --min-size 1 \\\n    --max-size 3 \\\n    --desired-capacity 2 \\\n    --vpc-zone-identifier subnet-12345678,subnet-87654321 \\\n    --region us-east-1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0383","card":"q-aws-044-launch-template-and-auto-scaling-group","sec":"aws-s045","q":"In <code>create-auto-scaling-group</code>, what does <code>Version=1</code> do in <code>--launch-template</code>, and what are the alternatives?","a":"<p>It pins the group to version 1 of the template. You can use <code>$Latest</code> or <code>$Default</code> so the group follows the newest or the default version. Pinning is safer, because editing a template cannot change the group by accident.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws autoscaling create-auto-scaling-group \\\n    --auto-scaling-group-name my-asg \\\n    --launch-template LaunchTemplateName=my-launch-template,Version=1 \\\n    --min-size 1 \\\n    --max-size 3 \\\n    --desired-capacity 2</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0384","card":"q-aws-044-launch-template-and-auto-scaling-group","sec":"aws-s045","q":"Why does <code>--vpc-zone-identifier</code> list several subnets?","a":"<p>It tells the group which subnets, and therefore which Availability Zones, it can launch into. The group spreads instances evenly across them, so losing one zone does not take out the service. Always give subnets in at least two zones for high availability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0385","card":"q-aws-044-launch-template-and-auto-scaling-group","sec":"aws-s045","q":"What rule must the desired capacity follow in an Auto Scaling group?","a":"<p>It must stay between the minimum and the maximum size. If you set a value outside the range the call fails, and scaling policies never move the group beyond those limits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0386","card":"q-aws-044-launch-template-and-auto-scaling-group","sec":"aws-s045","q":"In EC2 Auto Scaling, where do security groups and subnets get configured: the launch template or the group?","a":"<p>Instance-level settings (AMI, instance type, key pair, security groups, user data, IAM instance profile) belong to the launch template. The Auto Scaling group itself only holds placement and sizing: subnets (<code>VPCZoneIdentifier</code>), min, max and desired capacity, health check type and load balancer target groups. So the security group goes in the template and the subnets go on the group.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0387","card":"q-aws-045-lambda-function","sec":"aws-s046","q":"How do you create a Lambda function with CloudFormation (YAML)?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Resources:\n    MyLambdaFunction:\n        Type: AWS::Lambda::Function\n        Properties:\n            FunctionName: my-lambda-function\n            Handler: index.handler\n            Runtime: nodejs18.x\n            Role: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/lambda-exec-role\n            Code:\n                S3Bucket: my-code-bucket\n                S3Key: lambda-code.zip</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0388","card":"q-aws-045-lambda-function","sec":"aws-s046","q":"In a CloudFormation <code>AWS::Lambda::Function</code>, what does <code>Handler: index.handler</code> mean?","a":"<p>For Node.js it means: file <code>index</code> (index.js), exported function <code>handler</code>. Lambda calls that function for every invocation. The format depends on the runtime, for instance in Python it is <code>file_name.function_name</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>MyLambdaFunction:\n    Type: AWS::Lambda::Function\n    Properties:\n        Handler: index.handler\n        Runtime: nodejs18.x</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0389","card":"q-aws-045-lambda-function","sec":"aws-s046","q":"What is the <code>Role</code> property of a Lambda function, and what is the minimum it must permit?","a":"<p>It is the execution role the function assumes when it runs. At minimum it needs a trust policy for <code>lambda.amazonaws.com</code>, and permission to write logs to CloudWatch Logs (the managed policy <code>AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole</code>). Add whatever else the code calls, like S3 or DynamoDB access.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0390","card":"q-aws-045-lambda-function","sec":"aws-s046","q":"Why should you not rely on an old <code>Runtime</code> such as <code>nodejs18.x</code> in a template?","a":"<p>Lambda runtimes have an end-of-support date. After deprecation you cannot create new functions with that runtime and later cannot update existing ones. Check the supported runtime list in the Lambda documentation and plan upgrades.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0391","card":"q-aws-045-lambda-function","sec":"aws-s046","q":"What are the options for supplying the function's code in CloudFormation, and which would you use for larger packages?","a":"<p>Inline code with <code>ZipFile</code> (small, interpreted languages only), a zip in S3 with <code>S3Bucket</code> and <code>S3Key</code>, or a container image from ECR with <code>PackageType: Image</code>. Use S3 or an image for real applications; the zip limit is 50 MB zipped (250 MB unzipped), and images can go to 10 GB.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0392","card":"q-aws-045-lambda-function","sec":"aws-s046","q":"When would you use a Lambda function URL instead of API Gateway?","a":"<p>A function URL gives the function a built-in HTTPS endpoint with <code>AuthType</code> of <code>NONE</code> or <code>AWS_IAM</code>, at no extra charge. Use it for simple webhooks or internal calls. API Gateway adds throttling, usage plans and API keys, request validation, caching, custom authorizers, WAF and stages, so choose it for a managed public API.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0393","card":"q-aws-046-cloudwatch-alarm-for-ec2","sec":"aws-s047","q":"How do you create a CloudWatch alarm for an EC2 instance with CloudFormation?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Resources:\n    MyCloudWatchAlarm:\n        Type: AWS::CloudWatch::Alarm\n        Properties:\n            AlarmName: ec2-cpu-alarm\n            MetricName: CPUUtilization\n            Namespace: AWS/EC2\n            Statistic: Average\n            Period: 300\n            EvaluationPeriods: 2\n            Threshold: 80\n            ComparisonOperator: GreaterThanThreshold\n            Dimensions:\n                - Name: InstanceId\n                    Value: i-1234567890abcdef0\n            AlarmActions:\n                - arn:aws:sns:us-east-1:123456789012:my-topic</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0394","card":"q-aws-046-cloudwatch-alarm-for-ec2","sec":"aws-s047","q":"In a CloudWatch alarm, what do <code>Period: 300</code>, <code>EvaluationPeriods: 2</code> and <code>Threshold: 80</code> mean together?","a":"<p>CloudWatch looks at the metric statistic per 300-second period. The alarm goes to ALARM when the value is over 80 in 2 consecutive periods, so about 10 minutes of high usage. More periods reduce false alarms but delay the response.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Period: 300\nEvaluationPeriods: 2\nThreshold: 80\nComparisonOperator: GreaterThanThreshold</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0395","card":"q-aws-046-cloudwatch-alarm-for-ec2","sec":"aws-s047","q":"Why does an EC2 CPU alarm need a <code>Dimensions</code> entry with <code>InstanceId</code>?","a":"<p>A metric is identified by namespace, name and dimensions. <code>CPUUtilization</code> exists for every instance, so the dimension selects which one. Without the right dimension the alarm watches a metric that has no data and stays in <code>INSUFFICIENT_DATA</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0396","card":"q-aws-046-cloudwatch-alarm-for-ec2","sec":"aws-s047","q":"What does <code>TreatMissingData</code> control, and what would you choose for an alarm on a metric that stops when an instance dies?","a":"<p>It sets the alarm's behavior when data points are missing: <code>missing</code> (default), <code>notBreaching</code>, <code>breaching</code> or <code>ignore</code>. For a heartbeat-style metric, <code>breaching</code> makes silence raise the alarm. For a metric that is legitimately sparse, <code>notBreaching</code> avoids false alarms.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0397","card":"q-aws-046-cloudwatch-alarm-for-ec2","sec":"aws-s047","q":"What is the difference between <code>AlarmActions</code>, <code>OKActions</code> and <code>InsufficientDataActions</code>?","a":"<p>Each list is run on a state change: to <code>ALARM</code>, back to <code>OK</code>, or to <code>INSUFFICIENT_DATA</code>. Actions can be an SNS topic, an Auto Scaling policy, or an EC2 action such as reboot or recover. Add <code>OKActions</code> so people hear when it recovers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0398","card":"q-aws-047-api-gateway-in-front-of-lambda","sec":"aws-s048","q":"How do you create an API Gateway and attach it to a Lambda function with CloudFormation?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Resources:\n    MyApiGateway:\n        Type: AWS::ApiGateway::RestApi\n        Properties:\n            Name: my-api\n    MyResource:\n        Type: AWS::ApiGateway::Resource\n        Properties:\n            RestApiId: !Ref MyApiGateway\n            ParentId: !GetAtt MyApiGateway.RootResourceId\n            PathPart: &quot;hello&quot;\n    MyMethod:\n        Type: AWS::ApiGateway::Method\n        Properties:\n            RestApiId: !Ref MyApiGateway\n            ResourceId: !Ref MyResource\n            HttpMethod: GET\n            AuthorizationType: NONE\n            Integration:\n                Type: AWS\n                IntegrationHttpMethod: POST\n                Uri: !Sub arn:aws:apigateway:us-east-1:lambda:path/2015-03-31/functions/${MyLambdaFunction.Arn}/invocations\n    MyLambdaFunction:\n        Type: AWS::Lambda::Function\n        Properties:\n            FunctionName: my-lambda-function\n            Handler: index.handler\n            Runtime: nodejs18.x\n            Role: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/lambda-exec-role\n            Code:\n                S3Bucket: my-code-bucket\n                S3Key: lambda-code.zip\n    LambdaPermission:\n        Type: AWS::Lambda::Permission\n        Properties:\n            Action: lambda:InvokeFunction\n            FunctionName: !Ref MyLambdaFunction\n            Principal: apigateway.amazonaws.com\n            SourceArn: !Sub arn:aws:execute-api:us-east-1:123456789012:${MyApiGateway}/*/*/*</code></pre></div>\n<p>The <code>AWS::Lambda::Permission</code> resource lets API Gateway invoke the function.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0399","card":"q-aws-047-api-gateway-in-front-of-lambda","sec":"aws-s048","q":"Why does this setup include an <code>AWS::Lambda::Permission</code> resource?","a":"<p>Lambda uses a resource-based policy to decide who may invoke a function. The permission adds API Gateway as an allowed caller. Without it the API returns a 500 (execution failed due to configuration error: permissions) even though the integration looks right.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>LambdaPermission:\n    Type: AWS::Lambda::Permission\n    Properties:\n        Action: lambda:InvokeFunction\n        FunctionName: !Ref MyLambdaFunction\n        Principal: apigateway.amazonaws.com</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0400","card":"q-aws-047-api-gateway-in-front-of-lambda","sec":"aws-s048","q":"In an API Gateway REST API method, why is <code>IntegrationHttpMethod: POST</code> used when the client method is <code>GET</code>?","a":"<p>API Gateway calls Lambda through the Lambda Invoke API, which always uses POST. The client-side method and the backend call are independent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0401","card":"q-aws-047-api-gateway-in-front-of-lambda","sec":"aws-s048","q":"What is the difference between the <code>AWS</code> and <code>AWS_PROXY</code> integration types with Lambda?","a":"<p>With <code>AWS_PROXY</code> (Lambda proxy), API Gateway passes the whole request as an event and expects the function to return a response object with status code, headers and body. With <code>AWS</code> (custom), you write mapping templates to transform the request and response yourself. Proxy is simpler and the usual choice.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0402","card":"q-aws-047-api-gateway-in-front-of-lambda","sec":"aws-s048","q":"What is missing for a REST API defined with only a RestApi, Resource and Method before callers can use it?","a":"<p>A deployment and a stage (<code>AWS::ApiGateway::Deployment</code> and <code>AWS::ApiGateway::Stage</code>). In REST APIs, changes only take effect once deployed to a stage, which provides the invoke URL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0403","card":"q-aws-047-api-gateway-in-front-of-lambda","sec":"aws-s048","q":"Why is <code>AuthorizationType: NONE</code> a concern, and what options replace it?","a":"<p>It leaves the endpoint open to anyone. Use IAM authorization, a Cognito user pool authorizer, a Lambda authorizer, or API keys with usage plans (keys are for throttling, not real security).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0404","card":"q-aws-047-api-gateway-in-front-of-lambda","sec":"aws-s048","q":"When would you choose an API Gateway HTTP API over a REST API?","a":"<p>HTTP APIs are cheaper and lower latency, with simpler setup and JWT authorizers. REST APIs have more features: API keys and usage plans, request validation, caching, WAF integration and private endpoints. Choose HTTP unless you need those features.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0405","card":"q-aws-048-step-functions-state-machine","sec":"aws-s049","q":"How do you create a Step Functions state machine with CloudFormation?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Resources:\n    MyStateMachine:\n        Type: AWS::StepFunctions::StateMachine\n        Properties:\n            StateMachineName: my-state-machine\n            RoleArn: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/step-functions-role\n            DefinitionString: |\n                {\n                    &quot;StartAt&quot;: &quot;Hello&quot;,\n                    &quot;States&quot;: {\n                        &quot;Hello&quot;: {\n                            &quot;Type&quot;: &quot;Pass&quot;,\n                            &quot;Result&quot;: &quot;Hello World&quot;,\n                            &quot;End&quot;: true\n                        }\n                    }\n                }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0406","card":"q-aws-048-step-functions-state-machine","sec":"aws-s049","q":"Why use Step Functions rather than chaining Lambda functions by calling one from another?","a":"<p>Step Functions keeps workflow state outside the code. It gives built-in retry and catch, branching, parallelism, waiting, visual execution history, and no idle time billed while waiting. Chained Lambdas hide the flow in code, and a failure midway leaves no record or recovery path.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0407","card":"q-aws-048-step-functions-state-machine","sec":"aws-s049","q":"What do <code>StartAt</code> and <code>States</code> mean in an Amazon States Language definition?","a":"<p><code>StartAt</code> names the first state to run. <code>States</code> maps names to state definitions. Each state either has <code>\"End\": true</code> or a <code>Next</code> pointing to the following state. A <code>Pass</code> state just passes (or injects) data without doing work.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n    &quot;StartAt&quot;: &quot;Hello&quot;,\n    &quot;States&quot;: {\n        &quot;Hello&quot;: {&quot;Type&quot;: &quot;Pass&quot;, &quot;Result&quot;: &quot;Hello World&quot;, &quot;End&quot;: true}\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0408","card":"q-aws-048-step-functions-state-machine","sec":"aws-s049","q":"Name the main Step Functions state types and their roles.","a":"<p>Task (does work, for instance invokes Lambda or an AWS service), Choice (branching on input), Parallel (concurrent branches), Map (iterate over items), Wait (delay), Pass, Succeed and Fail.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0409","card":"q-aws-048-step-functions-state-machine","sec":"aws-s049","q":"What is the difference between Standard and Express workflows?","a":"<p>Standard workflows run up to one year, give exactly-once execution and full history, and are billed per state transition. Express workflows run up to 5 minutes, are at-least-once (or at-most-once when synchronous), are built for high-rate short jobs, and are billed by number of executions and duration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0410","card":"q-aws-048-step-functions-state-machine","sec":"aws-s049","q":"How do you handle transient errors in a Step Functions Task state?","a":"<p>Add a <code>Retry</code> block with error names, interval, max attempts and backoff rate, and a <code>Catch</code> block that routes remaining failures to a fallback or a Fail state. This replaces hand-written retry loops.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0411","card":"q-aws-048-step-functions-state-machine","sec":"aws-s049","q":"What is the <code>RoleArn</code> in the state machine resource used for?","a":"<p>It is the execution role Step Functions assumes to call other services. It needs permission for each action in the definition, such as <code>lambda:InvokeFunction</code> for a Task state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0412","card":"q-aws-048-step-functions-state-machine","sec":"aws-s049","q":"What is the difference between a Parallel state and a Map state in Step Functions?","a":"<p>A Parallel state runs a fixed set of different branches at the same time on the same input. A Map state runs the same steps once for every item of an input array, with a configurable concurrency limit. Use Parallel for independent different tasks and Map for fan-out over a list, such as processing each file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0413","card":"q-aws-048-step-functions-state-machine","sec":"aws-s049","q":"In AWS Step Functions, how do you pause a workflow until a human approves or an external system calls back, and why is it better than polling?","a":"<p>Use the <code>.waitForTaskToken</code> service integration on a Task state. Step Functions generates a task token and passes it to the target (for example an SQS message or a Lambda). The execution waits, with no cost for Standard workflows while paused, until something calls <code>SendTaskSuccess</code> or <code>SendTaskFailure</code> with that token. Set <code>HeartbeatSeconds</code> or <code>TimeoutSeconds</code> so it cannot wait forever. Polling wastes invocations and adds latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0414","card":"q-aws-049-launch-template-with-the-cloudwatch-agen","sec":"aws-s050","q":"How do you set up an Auto Scaling group that scales on CPU and memory utilization?","a":"<p>Step 1: create a launch template whose user data installs the CloudWatch agent.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 create-launch-template \\\n  --launch-template-name MyWebServerTemplate \\\n  --version-description &quot;v1&quot; \\\n  --launch-template-data &#x27;{\n    &quot;ImageId&quot;: &quot;&lt;ami-id&gt;&quot;,\n    &quot;InstanceType&quot;: &quot;t2.micro&quot;,\n    &quot;KeyName&quot;: &quot;&lt;key-pair-name&gt;&quot;,\n    &quot;NetworkInterfaces&quot;: [{&quot;AssociatePublicIpAddress&quot;: true, &quot;DeviceIndex&quot;: 0, &quot;SubnetId&quot;: &quot;&lt;subnet-id&gt;&quot;, &quot;Groups&quot;: [&quot;&lt;security-group-id&gt;&quot;]}],\n    &quot;UserData&quot;: &quot;&#x27;$(echo -e &quot;#!/bin/bash\\nyum install -y amazon-cloudwatch-agent\\n/opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/amazon-cloudwatch-agent-config-wizard&quot; | base64)&#x27;&quot;\n  }&#x27;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Step 2: create the Auto Scaling group.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws autoscaling create-auto-scaling-group \\\n  --auto-scaling-group-name MyWebServerASG \\\n  --launch-template &quot;LaunchTemplateName=MyWebServerTemplate,Version=1&quot; \\\n  --min-size 1 \\\n  --max-size 4 \\\n  --desired-capacity 2 \\\n  --vpc-zone-identifier &quot;&lt;subnet-id-1&gt;,&lt;subnet-id-2&gt;&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0415","card":"q-aws-049-launch-template-with-the-cloudwatch-agen","sec":"aws-s050","q":"What is wrong with running <code>amazon-cloudwatch-agent-config-wizard</code> from EC2 user data?","a":"<p>The wizard is interactive and expects answers from a terminal, so in an unattended boot script it does not finish, and no agent configuration is produced. Instead store a JSON config in Systems Manager Parameter Store (or a file) and start the agent with <code>amazon-cloudwatch-agent-ctl -a fetch-config -m ec2 -c ssm:&lt;parameter-name&gt; -s</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>#!/bin/bash\nyum install -y amazon-cloudwatch-agent\n/opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/amazon-cloudwatch-agent-config-wizard</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0416","card":"q-aws-049-launch-template-with-the-cloudwatch-agen","sec":"aws-s050","q":"Which permissions does the instance need to publish agent metrics?","a":"<p>An instance profile with the managed policy <code>CloudWatchAgentServerPolicy</code> (and <code>AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore</code> if the config is fetched from Parameter Store). Without it the agent runs but nothing arrives in CloudWatch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0417","card":"q-aws-049-launch-template-with-the-cloudwatch-agen","sec":"aws-s050","q":"Why is <code>base64</code> applied to the user data when creating a launch template through <code>create-launch-template</code>?","a":"<p>The <code>UserData</code> field in launch template data must be base64-encoded text. The CLI does not encode it for you inside the JSON data. Terraform's <code>base64encode</code> and the console do this encoding step in their own ways.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0418","card":"q-aws-049-launch-template-with-the-cloudwatch-agen","sec":"aws-s050","q":"Why use a launch template for an Auto Scaling group instead of installing the agent after launch?","a":"<p>Every instance the group creates then comes up configured the same way automatically, including replacements for failed or scaled-out instances. Manual installs would be missed on new instances. A baked AMI with the agent is the faster alternative.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0419","card":"q-aws-050-step-scaling-policy","sec":"aws-s051","q":"In a step scaling policy, what does <code>MetricIntervalLowerBound: 0</code> with <code>ScalingAdjustment: 1</code> mean?","a":"<p>The bounds are measured relative to the alarm threshold. A lower bound of 0 means \"when the metric is at or over the threshold\", and the adjustment adds 1 instance. More steps with larger bounds add more instances as the breach grows, for instance +1 at threshold and +3 at 20 over it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;AdjustmentType&quot;: &quot;ChangeInCapacity&quot;,\n  &quot;StepAdjustments&quot;: [{&quot;MetricIntervalLowerBound&quot;: 0, &quot;ScalingAdjustment&quot;: 1}]\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0420","card":"q-aws-050-step-scaling-policy","sec":"aws-s051","q":"What are the <code>AdjustmentType</code> choices in an Auto Scaling policy?","a":"<p><code>ChangeInCapacity</code> (add or remove a fixed count), <code>PercentChangeInCapacity</code> (a percentage of current capacity) and <code>ExactCapacity</code> (set a specific count). The first is easiest to reason about.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0421","card":"q-aws-050-step-scaling-policy","sec":"aws-s051","q":"A single step or simple scale-out policy on an EC2 Auto Scaling group never reduces capacity. What must you add so the group scales in?","a":"<p>A second policy with a negative adjustment, triggered by a low-metric alarm (for example CPU under 30%). Without it the group only grows until it hits the maximum and you pay for idle capacity. A target tracking policy avoids this, because it scales both out and in on its own.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0422","card":"q-aws-051-cpu-and-memory-alarms","sec":"aws-s052","q":"In CloudWatch, when an alarm drives an Auto Scaling group, why use the <code>AutoScalingGroupName</code> dimension instead of an <code>InstanceId</code>?","a":"<p>Instances come and go. A group-level dimension aggregates the metric across all current members, so one alarm tracks the whole fleet's average and keeps working as instances change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0423","card":"q-aws-051-cpu-and-memory-alarms","sec":"aws-s052","q":"In CloudWatch, why does an alarm on EC2 memory use the <code>CWAgent</code> namespace and not <code>AWS/EC2</code>?","a":"<p><code>AWS/EC2</code> holds metrics EC2 itself publishes (CPU, network, disk I/O). Memory comes from the agent as a custom metric, and the agent's default namespace is <code>CWAgent</code>. The alarm must name that namespace.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0424","card":"q-aws-051-cpu-and-memory-alarms","sec":"aws-s052","q":"A memory alarm stays in <code>INSUFFICIENT_DATA</code>. What do you check?","a":"<p>That the agent is running and its config includes memory metrics. That the instance profile allows publishing. That the alarm's namespace, metric name and dimensions match exactly what the agent sends: on Linux the agent's default metric is <code>mem_used_percent</code>, and the <code>AutoScalingGroupName</code> dimension is only present if the agent config appends it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0425","card":"q-aws-051-cpu-and-memory-alarms","sec":"aws-s052","q":"With a CPU alarm and a memory alarm both triggering the same scaling policy, what happens when both are in ALARM?","a":"<p>Each alarm invokes the policy. The group adds capacity per the policy, but warm-up or cooldown limits how quickly a second activity follows. Two alarms on one policy mean \"scale when either is high\" (an OR condition).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0426","card":"q-aws-052-verify-the-setup","sec":"aws-s053","q":"After building an Auto Scaling group with alarms, how do you confirm the scaling actually works?","a":"<p>Check the group with <code>describe-auto-scaling-groups</code> (capacity, instances healthy and in service), the alarms with <code>describe-alarms</code> (state and that they list the policy as an action), then generate load (for instance with a CPU stress tool) and watch <code>describe-scaling-activities</code> for new launches. Remember to check scale-in after load stops.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0427","card":"q-aws-052-verify-the-setup","sec":"aws-s053","q":"Which command shows why an Auto Scaling group launched or terminated an instance?","a":"<p><code>aws autoscaling describe-scaling-activities --auto-scaling-group-name &lt;name&gt;</code>. It lists each activity with status, cause (for instance which alarm triggered it) and failure messages such as an unavailable instance type or a missing key pair.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0428","card":"q-aws-052-verify-the-setup","sec":"aws-s053","q":"An alarm is in ALARM but the group did not scale out. What do you check?","a":"<p>That the policy ARN is in the alarm's actions. That the group is not already at its maximum size. That the policy's warm-up or cooldown has not blocked it. That the group is not suspended for the Launch process. And the scaling activity history for errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0429","card":"q-aws-053-ec2-commands","sec":"aws-s054","q":"How do you find EC2 instances in a specific VPC?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 describe-instances --filters &quot;Name=vpc-id,Values=vpc-12345678&quot; --query &quot;Reservations[].Instances[].InstanceId&quot; --output text</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0430","card":"q-aws-053-ec2-commands","sec":"aws-s054","q":"How do you stop all running EC2 instances in a region?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 stop-instances --instance-ids $(aws ec2 describe-instances --region us-east-1 --filters &quot;Name=instance-state-name,Values=running&quot; --query &quot;Reservations[].Instances[].InstanceId&quot; --output text)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0431","card":"q-aws-053-ec2-commands","sec":"aws-s054","q":"How do you tag untagged EC2 instances?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 describe-instances --query &#x27;Reservations[*].Instances[?not_null(Tags)==`false`].[InstanceId]&#x27; --output text | xargs -I {} aws ec2 create-tags --resources {} --tags Key=Environment,Value=Dev</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0432","card":"q-aws-053-ec2-commands","sec":"aws-s054","q":"How do you enable termination protection on EC2?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 modify-instance-attribute --instance-id &lt;instance-id&gt; --disable-api-termination</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0433","card":"q-aws-053-ec2-commands","sec":"aws-s054","q":"How do you check the IAM role on an EC2 instance?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 describe-instances --instance-ids &lt;instance-id&gt; --query &#x27;Reservations[0].Instances[0].IamInstanceProfile&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0434","card":"q-aws-053-ec2-commands","sec":"aws-s054","q":"How do you create a security group and allow port 80?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 create-security-group --group-name MySG --description &quot;Allow HTTP&quot; --vpc-id vpc-12345678\naws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress --group-name MySG --protocol tcp --port 80 --cidr 0.0.0.0/0</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0435","card":"q-aws-053-ec2-commands","sec":"aws-s054","q":"How do you update an EC2 security group to allow HTTPS?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress --group-id &lt;security-group-id&gt; --protocol tcp --port 443 --cidr 0.0.0.0/0</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0436","card":"q-aws-053-ec2-commands","sec":"aws-s054","q":"What is the difference between <code>--filters</code> and <code>--query</code> in <code>aws ec2 describe-instances</code>?","a":"<p><code>--filters</code> is applied by the service, so only matching resources come back. <code>--query</code> is a JMESPath expression applied by the CLI to the returned JSON, so it shapes output but does not reduce what the API returns. Use filters to limit data and query to extract fields.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0437","card":"q-aws-053-ec2-commands","sec":"aws-s054","q":"In the output of <code>aws ec2 describe-instances</code>, why do you query <code>Reservations[].Instances[]</code>?","a":"<p>The response nests instances inside reservations (the launch request that created them). Flattening with <code>[]</code> at both levels gives a single list of instances to pick fields from.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 describe-instances --query &quot;Reservations[].Instances[].InstanceId&quot; --output text</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0438","card":"q-aws-053-ec2-commands","sec":"aws-s054","q":"What happens with <code>aws ec2 stop-instances --instance-ids $(...)</code> if the inner query returns nothing?","a":"<p>The argument list is empty, so the command fails with a missing parameter error instead of doing nothing. Scripts should check for empty output first. Also remember that this is region-specific and stops every running instance matched, so test with a filter on tags first.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 stop-instances --instance-ids $(aws ec2 describe-instances --filters &quot;Name=instance-state-name,Values=running&quot; --query &quot;Reservations[].Instances[].InstanceId&quot; --output text)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0439","card":"q-aws-053-ec2-commands","sec":"aws-s054","q":"What does EC2 termination protection (<code>--disable-api-termination</code>) block, and what does it not block?","a":"<p>It blocks termination through the console, CLI or API until you turn it off. It does not stop an instance from being stopped, does not stop termination from inside the OS if the shutdown behavior is set to terminate, and does not protect against Auto Scaling scale-in (use scale-in protection for that).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0440","card":"q-aws-053-ec2-commands","sec":"aws-s054","q":"Why is tagging EC2 instances important beyond tidiness?","a":"<p>Tags drive cost allocation reports, access control (attribute-based policies), automation (backup and patch targeting) and cleanup of orphaned resources. Enforce them with tag policies or Config rules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0441","card":"q-aws-053-ec2-commands","sec":"aws-s054","q":"How do you see which IAM role an instance uses, and why does <code>IamInstanceProfile</code> show an ARN of an instance profile and not a role?","a":"<p><code>describe-instances</code> returns the instance profile ARN. The role is inside the profile, so call <code>aws iam get-instance-profile</code> to see its roles. An instance can have one profile with one role.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0442","card":"q-aws-054-ebs-and-ami-commands","sec":"aws-s055","q":"How do you attach an EBS volume to an EC2 instance?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 attach-volume --volume-id vol-12345678 --instance-id i-12345678 --device /dev/xvdf</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0443","card":"q-aws-054-ebs-and-ami-commands","sec":"aws-s055","q":"How do you create a snapshot of an EBS volume?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 create-snapshot --volume-id &lt;volume-id&gt; --description &quot;Snapshot&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0444","card":"q-aws-054-ebs-and-ami-commands","sec":"aws-s055","q":"How do you increase an EBS volume size?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 modify-volume --volume-id &lt;volume-id&gt; --size 100</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0445","card":"q-aws-054-ebs-and-ami-commands","sec":"aws-s055","q":"How do you deregister an AMI and delete its snapshots?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 deregister-image --image-id &lt;ami-id&gt;\naws ec2 describe-snapshots --filters Name=description,Values=&quot;Created by*for &lt;ami-id&gt;*&quot; --query &#x27;Snapshots[*].SnapshotId&#x27; --output text | xargs -n1 aws ec2 delete-snapshot --snapshot-id</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0446","card":"q-aws-054-ebs-and-ami-commands","sec":"aws-s055","q":"After <code>aws ec2 modify-volume --size 100</code>, why can the instance not yet use the extra space?","a":"<p>Only the block device grew. You must grow the partition (<code>growpart</code>) and the file system (<code>xfs_growfs</code> or <code>resize2fs</code>) inside the OS. You can do this online with no detach. EBS allows only increases (never decreasing), and you must wait before modifying the same volume again (about 6 hours).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0447","card":"q-aws-054-ebs-and-ami-commands","sec":"aws-s055","q":"How are EBS snapshots stored, and what does that mean for cost?","a":"<p>Snapshots are incremental: the first copies all used blocks, later ones store only changed blocks, saved in S3 managed by AWS. Deleting an old snapshot keeps the data other snapshots still need. So frequent snapshots are cheaper than full copies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0448","card":"q-aws-054-ebs-and-ami-commands","sec":"aws-s055","q":"Why does <code>aws ec2 deregister-image</code> not remove the cost of an AMI?","a":"<p>Deregistering only removes the AMI registration. The backing snapshots remain and keep costing money until deleted separately. This is why clean-up scripts delete snapshots too.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0449","card":"q-aws-054-ebs-and-ami-commands","sec":"aws-s055","q":"What is an AMI and what does it contain?","a":"<p>An Amazon Machine Image is a template for launching instances: a root volume snapshot (or a template for an instance store), launch permissions and block device mappings. Baking your software into an AMI makes launches faster and consistent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0450","card":"q-aws-054-ebs-and-ami-commands","sec":"aws-s055","q":"How can you take a consistent snapshot of a busy EBS volume?","a":"<p>Snapshots capture data at a point in time at the block level, so data cached in memory is not included. Quiesce the application or flush and freeze the file system first, or use application-aware tooling such as AWS Backup or Data Lifecycle Manager.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0451","card":"q-aws-055-s3-commands","sec":"aws-s056","q":"How do you check an S3 bucket's size?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws s3api list-objects-v2 --bucket my-bucket --query &quot;[sum(Contents[].Size)]&quot; --output text</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0452","card":"q-aws-055-s3-commands","sec":"aws-s056","q":"How do you delete all objects in an S3 bucket?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws s3 rm s3://my-bucket --recursive</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0453","card":"q-aws-055-s3-commands","sec":"aws-s056","q":"How do you list S3 buckets with versioning disabled?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws s3api list-buckets --query &#x27;Buckets[].Name&#x27; --output text | xargs -I {} sh -c &#x27;aws s3api get-bucket-versioning --bucket {} | grep -q &quot;Enabled&quot; || echo {}&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0454","card":"q-aws-055-s3-commands","sec":"aws-s056","q":"How do you copy a file between S3 buckets in different regions?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws s3 cp s3://source-bucket/file.txt s3://destination-bucket/file.txt --source-region us-east-1 --region us-west-2</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0455","card":"q-aws-055-s3-commands","sec":"aws-s056","q":"How do you enable encryption on an S3 bucket?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws s3api put-bucket-encryption --bucket &lt;bucket-name&gt; --server-side-encryption-configuration &#x27;{&quot;Rules&quot;:[{&quot;ApplyServerSideEncryptionByDefault&quot;:{&quot;SSEAlgorithm&quot;:&quot;AES256&quot;}}]}&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0456","card":"q-aws-055-s3-commands","sec":"aws-s056","q":"Why does <code>aws s3 rm s3://my-bucket --recursive</code> not empty a versioned bucket?","a":"<p>In a versioned bucket it only adds delete markers, so older versions remain and the bucket is not empty. To empty and delete it you must remove all versions and delete markers (for instance the console's \"Empty\" action, or <code>aws s3api delete-objects</code> per version).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0457","card":"q-aws-055-s3-commands","sec":"aws-s056","q":"How do you copy an object between buckets without it going through your machine?","a":"<p>A copy between S3 locations (<code>aws s3 cp s3://a/key s3://b/key</code>) runs server-side inside S3 as a copy request, so data does not flow through your machine. Cross-Region transfer is charged as data transfer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0458","card":"q-aws-055-s3-commands","sec":"aws-s056","q":"Why is checking bucket size with <code>list-objects-v2</code> and a sum a poor method for very large buckets?","a":"<p>It lists every object through paged API calls, which is slow and costs requests. Use CloudWatch's <code>BucketSizeBytes</code> metric, S3 Storage Lens, or S3 Inventory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0459","card":"q-aws-056-iam-cloudtrail-and-cloudwatch-commands","sec":"aws-s057","q":"How do you list IAM users created in the last 30 days?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws iam list-users --query &quot;Users[?CreateDate&gt;=`2025-03-08`].UserName&quot; --output text</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0460","card":"q-aws-056-iam-cloudtrail-and-cloudwatch-commands","sec":"aws-s057","q":"How do you rotate an IAM access key for a user?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws iam create-access-key --user-name &lt;username&gt;\naws iam delete-access-key --user-name &lt;username&gt; --access-key-id &lt;old-key-id&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0461","card":"q-aws-056-iam-cloudtrail-and-cloudwatch-commands","sec":"aws-s057","q":"How do you check CloudTrail events for an EC2 instance?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=ResourceName,AttributeValue=&lt;instance-id&gt; --query &#x27;Events[*].{Time:EventTime,Event:EventName,User:Username}&#x27; --output table</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0462","card":"q-aws-056-iam-cloudtrail-and-cloudwatch-commands","sec":"aws-s057","q":"How do you list CloudWatch alarms in the ALARM state?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws cloudwatch describe-alarms --state-value ALARM</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0463","card":"q-aws-056-iam-cloudtrail-and-cloudwatch-commands","sec":"aws-s057","q":"How do you troubleshoot a CloudWatch alarm?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws cloudwatch describe-alarms --alarm-names &lt;alarm-name&gt;\naws cloudwatch describe-alarm-history --alarm-name &lt;alarm-name&gt; --history-item-type StateUpdate</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0464","card":"q-aws-056-iam-cloudtrail-and-cloudwatch-commands","sec":"aws-s057","q":"How do you rotate an IAM access key without downtime?","a":"<p>An IAM user can have two keys at once. Create the second key, update the application to use it, check the old one is unused (the last-used information), deactivate the old key, and delete it after a safe period. The CLI sequence creating and deleting directly is only safe when nothing uses the old key.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0465","card":"q-aws-056-iam-cloudtrail-and-cloudwatch-commands","sec":"aws-s057","q":"What does <code>describe-alarm-history</code> add compared with <code>describe-alarms</code>?","a":"<p><code>describe-alarms</code> shows the current state and configuration. <code>describe-alarm-history</code> shows the state transitions, configuration changes and action results with timestamps and reasons, which is what you need to explain why an alarm fired or why an action failed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0466","card":"q-aws-056-iam-cloudtrail-and-cloudwatch-commands","sec":"aws-s057","q":"In the AWS CLI, why does filtering IAM users by creation date with <code>--query</code> run in the client rather than on the IAM server?","a":"<p>IAM has no server-side filter by creation date. The <code>--query</code> expression filters after the full list returns, so for large accounts it is slow. Use credential reports for audits.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws iam list-users --query &quot;Users[?CreateDate&gt;=&#x27;2024-01-01&#x27;]&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0467","card":"q-aws-057-scaling-load-balancer-and-database-comma","sec":"aws-s058","q":"How do you scale an Auto Scaling group to 5 instances?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws autoscaling set-desired-capacity --auto-scaling-group-name my-asg --desired-capacity 5</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0468","card":"q-aws-057-scaling-load-balancer-and-database-comma","sec":"aws-s058","q":"How do you check ELB health and remove an unhealthy instance?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws elb describe-instance-health --load-balancer-name &lt;elb-name&gt;\naws elb deregister-instances-from-load-balancer --load-balancer-name &lt;elb-name&gt; --instances &lt;instance-id&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0469","card":"q-aws-057-scaling-load-balancer-and-database-comma","sec":"aws-s058","q":"How do you check RDS status and reboot if it is not available?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>STATUS=$(aws rds describe-db-instances --db-instance-identifier my-rds --query &quot;DBInstances[0].DBInstanceStatus&quot; --output text); [ &quot;$STATUS&quot; != &quot;available&quot; ] &amp;&amp; aws rds reboot-db-instance --db-instance-identifier my-rds</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0470","card":"q-aws-057-scaling-load-balancer-and-database-comma","sec":"aws-s058","q":"How do you restore RDS from a snapshot?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws rds restore-db-instance-from-db-snapshot --db-instance-identifier &lt;new-db-name&gt; --db-snapshot-identifier &lt;snapshot-id&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0471","card":"q-aws-057-scaling-load-balancer-and-database-comma","sec":"aws-s058","q":"How do you list DynamoDB tables with their throughput?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>for table in $(aws dynamodb list-tables --query &#x27;TableNames&#x27; --output text); do aws dynamodb describe-table --table-name $table --query &#x27;Table.{Name:TableName,ReadCapacity:ProvisionedThroughput.ReadCapacityUnits,WriteCapacity:ProvisionedThroughput.WriteCapacityUnits}&#x27; --output table; done</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0472","card":"q-aws-057-scaling-load-balancer-and-database-comma","sec":"aws-s058","q":"What does <code>aws autoscaling set-desired-capacity</code> do when the value is outside min and max, and what about cooldown?","a":"<p>A value outside the group's limits is rejected. By default a manual change ignores the cooldown unless you pass <code>--honor-cooldown</code>. Policies may later move capacity again, so for a lasting change update min and max too.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0473","card":"q-aws-057-scaling-load-balancer-and-database-comma","sec":"aws-s058","q":"Why does the <code>aws elb</code> command not work for an Application Load Balancer?","a":"<p><code>aws elb</code> is for Classic Load Balancers. ALB and NLB use <code>aws elbv2</code> (with target groups, <code>describe-target-health</code> and <code>deregister-targets</code>). Using the wrong one returns not-found errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0474","card":"q-aws-057-scaling-load-balancer-and-database-comma","sec":"aws-s058","q":"Why is deregistering an instance from a target group usually followed by waiting?","a":"<p>Deregistration starts connection draining (the deregistration delay, default 300 seconds), so in-flight requests finish before the target is removed. Lower the delay for short requests to speed up deployments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0475","card":"q-aws-057-scaling-load-balancer-and-database-comma","sec":"aws-s058","q":"When you run <code>aws rds restore-db-instance-from-db-snapshot</code>, what happens to the original database?","a":"<p>Nothing. A restore always creates a new instance with a new endpoint, from the snapshot. Point the application at the new endpoint (or swap DNS names) and delete the old one when you are done. Security groups and parameter groups must be set again.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0476","card":"q-aws-057-scaling-load-balancer-and-database-comma","sec":"aws-s058","q":"Why might <code>describe-table</code> show 0 read and write capacity units for a DynamoDB table?","a":"<p>In on-demand mode there is no provisioned throughput, so the fields show 0. The <code>ProvisionedThroughput</code> values are only meaningful for tables in provisioned mode.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0477","card":"q-aws-057-scaling-load-balancer-and-database-comma","sec":"aws-s058","q":"Why is a script that reboots RDS whenever the status is not <code>available</code> risky?","a":"<p>Many non-available states are normal and temporary (backing up, modifying, upgrading), and rebooting during them can cause more harm. A reboot also interrupts connections. Alert and investigate instead of rebooting automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0478","card":"q-aws-058-lambda-containers-and-messaging-commands","sec":"aws-s059","q":"How do you download the latest CloudWatch logs for a Lambda function?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws logs get-log-events --log-group-name /aws/lambda/my-function --log-stream-name $(aws logs describe-log-streams --log-group-name /aws/lambda/my-function --order-by LastEventTime --descending --limit 1 --query &quot;logStreams[0].logStreamName&quot; --output text) --output text &gt; lambda_logs.txt</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0479","card":"q-aws-058-lambda-containers-and-messaging-commands","sec":"aws-s059","q":"How do you invoke a Lambda function and capture the output?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda invoke --function-name &lt;function-name&gt; output.json</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0480","card":"q-aws-058-lambda-containers-and-messaging-commands","sec":"aws-s059","q":"How do you update a Lambda timeout to 10 seconds?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda update-function-configuration --function-name &lt;function-name&gt; --timeout 10</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0481","card":"q-aws-058-lambda-containers-and-messaging-commands","sec":"aws-s059","q":"How do you list running ECS tasks in a cluster?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ecs list-tasks --cluster &lt;cluster-name&gt; --desired-status RUNNING</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0482","card":"q-aws-058-lambda-containers-and-messaging-commands","sec":"aws-s059","q":"How do you check an EKS cluster's status?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws eks describe-cluster --name &lt;cluster-name&gt; --query &#x27;cluster.status&#x27; --output text</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0483","card":"q-aws-058-lambda-containers-and-messaging-commands","sec":"aws-s059","q":"How do you list SNS topics and their subscriptions?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>for topic in $(aws sns list-topics --query &#x27;Topics[*].TopicArn&#x27; --output text); do aws sns list-subscriptions-by-topic --topic-arn $topic --query &#x27;Subscriptions[*].{Endpoint:Endpoint,Protocol:Protocol}&#x27; --output table; done</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0484","card":"q-aws-058-lambda-containers-and-messaging-commands","sec":"aws-s059","q":"What does <code>aws lambda invoke</code> do by default, and how do you make it asynchronous?","a":"<p>By default it invokes synchronously, waits for the result and writes the response to <code>output.json</code>. For asynchronous invocation add <code>--invocation-type Event</code>, which returns immediately with status 202. In AWS CLI v2, a JSON <code>--payload</code> must be given with <code>--cli-binary-format raw-in-base64-out</code> (or base64 encoded).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda invoke --function-name &lt;function-name&gt; output.json</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0485","card":"q-aws-058-lambda-containers-and-messaging-commands","sec":"aws-s059","q":"A Lambda function has to process a job that takes about 40 minutes. What are your options, given the Lambda timeout limit?","a":"<p>A single Lambda invocation is limited to 15 minutes (900 seconds), the maximum the function timeout accepts. Split the job into chunks and fan them out through SQS or a Step Functions Map state, so each piece fits in the limit. Or run the work in a container on Fargate or AWS Batch, which has no such limit. API Gateway integrations also have their own shorter timeout, so long work should be asynchronous.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0486","card":"q-aws-058-lambda-containers-and-messaging-commands","sec":"aws-s059","q":"How are Lambda logs organized in CloudWatch Logs?","a":"<p>Each function has a log group named <code>/aws/lambda/&lt;function-name&gt;</code>, with one or more log streams for execution environments. Streams are not one per invocation. The execution role needs permission to write logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0487","card":"q-aws-058-lambda-containers-and-messaging-commands","sec":"aws-s059","q":"An Amazon ECS task keeps stopping a few seconds after start. Where do you look for the reason, and what are common causes?","a":"<p>Run <code>aws ecs describe-tasks</code> and read <code>stoppedReason</code> and the container <code>exitCode</code>. Common causes: the essential container exited (application error), the image could not be pulled (missing ECR permission on the task execution role, or no route to the registry), out-of-memory kill (exit code 137), a failing load balancer health check that makes the service replace the task, or missing secrets. Also read the container's CloudWatch Logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0488","card":"q-aws-058-lambda-containers-and-messaging-commands","sec":"aws-s059","q":"What does <code>aws eks describe-cluster --query cluster.status</code> return, and does it tell you the workloads are healthy?","a":"<p>It returns the control plane status (<code>CREATING</code>, <code>ACTIVE</code>, <code>DELETING</code>, <code>FAILED</code>, <code>UPDATING</code>). It says nothing about nodes or pods, so also check node groups and <code>kubectl get nodes</code> and pod status.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0489","card":"q-aws-059-blue-green-deployment-process","sec":"aws-s060","q":"How does a blue-green deployment run on AWS and EKS?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Code commit</strong>: developers push to GitHub or Bitbucket.</li>\n  <li><strong>CI/CD trigger</strong>: GitHub Actions builds and tests Docker containers.</li>\n  <li><strong>Green deployment</strong>: the new version is deployed to the Green environment (Kubernetes on EKS).</li>\n  <li><strong>Validation</strong>: test Green with CloudWatch monitoring.</li>\n  <li><strong>Traffic switch</strong>: route traffic to Green through AWS ELB or Kubernetes Ingress.</li>\n  <li><strong>Monitoring</strong>: track CPU and latency in CloudWatch.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rollback</strong>: switch back to Blue if issues arise.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0490","card":"q-aws-059-blue-green-deployment-process","sec":"aws-s060","q":"What is the main cost and weakness of a blue-green deployment?","a":"<p>It needs two full production environments while the switch happens, which roughly doubles infrastructure cost for that time. Database schema and state shared between both versions need backward-compatible changes. Caches and sessions need care too.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0491","card":"q-aws-059-blue-green-deployment-process","sec":"aws-s060","q":"How do you implement the traffic switch for blue-green on Kubernetes without a service mesh?","a":"<p>Run two Deployments labelled <code>version: blue</code> and <code>version: green</code>. Point a Service selector at one label. To switch, patch the Service selector to the other label. Rollback is the reverse patch. With an ALB, change the Ingress or the target group weights instead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0492","card":"q-aws-059-blue-green-deployment-process","sec":"aws-s060","q":"What should you validate in the new environment before switching traffic?","a":"<p>Health checks, smoke tests against the green endpoint, error rate and latency compared with blue, dependency connectivity (database, queues) and logs. Keep blue running for a defined period after the switch so rollback is a single change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0493","card":"q-aws-060-delivery-metrics-affected","sec":"aws-s061","q":"Which delivery metrics does blue-green improve?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Deployment Frequency</strong>: faster through automation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Lead Time for Changes</strong>: reduced by 25%.</li>\n  <li><strong>Change Failure Rate</strong>: errors eliminated.</li>\n  <li><strong>Mean Time to Restore</strong>: minimized through rollback.</li>\n  <li><strong>Availability</strong>: preserved by the zero-downtime switch.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0494","card":"q-aws-060-delivery-metrics-affected","sec":"aws-s061","q":"What are the four DORA metrics, and what does each measure?","a":"<p>Deployment frequency (how often you release), lead time for changes (commit to production), change failure rate (share of deployments causing a failure) and time to restore service (how fast you recover). The first two measure speed, the others stability. Newer DORA guidance renames the last metric \"failed deployment recovery time\" and adds a fifth, rework rate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0495","card":"q-aws-060-delivery-metrics-affected","sec":"aws-s061","q":"How does blue-green deployment improve mean time to restore?","a":"<p>Rollback becomes a traffic switch back to the old environment, taking seconds or minutes rather than a rebuild and redeploy. The old version is still running and warm.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0496","card":"q-aws-061-deployment-strategies-compared","sec":"aws-s062","q":"What deployment strategies exist and how do they compare?","a":"<ol>\n  <li><strong>Blue-Green</strong> (used): two environments (Blue is current, Green is new). Zero downtime, low MTTR, high availability. Tools: AWS ELB, Kubernetes, Terraform, CloudWatch.</li>\n  <li><strong>Canary</strong>: gradual rollout to a small user subset. Reduces change failure rate through early issue detection.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rolling</strong>: incremental pod updates in Kubernetes. Resource-efficient but risks compatibility issues.</li>\n  <li><strong>Feature flag</strong>: hide features behind toggles. Flexible, reduces change failure rate through selective rollout.</li>\n  <li><strong>A/B testing</strong>: compare versions for user feedback. Improves CSAT and NPS.</li>\n  <li><strong>Shadow</strong>: test the new version with duplicated traffic. Safe, with no user impact.</li>\n  <li><strong>Recreate</strong>: terminate the old version, deploy the new one. High risk and not used because of downtime.</li>\n</ol>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0497","card":"q-aws-061-deployment-strategies-compared","sec":"aws-s062","q":"What is the difference between a canary release and an A/B test?","a":"<p>Canary exposes a small share of traffic to a new version to catch faults before a full rollout, judged by technical health (errors, latency). A/B testing splits users between variants to compare business outcomes (conversion, engagement) over a longer time. Canary is about risk, A/B about learning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0498","card":"q-aws-061-deployment-strategies-compared","sec":"aws-s062","q":"How do feature flags differ from deployment strategies such as blue-green?","a":"<p>Feature flags separate deploying code from releasing a feature: code ships turned off, then is enabled per user group at run time and can be switched off instantly without a redeploy. Blue-green switches whole versions of the application. They are often combined. Old flags should be removed to avoid clutter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0499","card":"q-aws-061-deployment-strategies-compared","sec":"aws-s062","q":"What is a shadow (traffic mirroring) deployment and what is its main risk?","a":"<p>Real production requests are copied to the new version, and its responses are discarded, so users are not affected. The risk is side effects: the shadow version may write to databases, send emails or charge cards twice, so it must use stubs or isolated stores.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0500","card":"q-aws-061-deployment-strategies-compared","sec":"aws-s062","q":"How does a rolling update work in Kubernetes, and which settings control it?","a":"<p>The Deployment replaces pods gradually. <code>maxSurge</code> sets how many extra pods may exist during the update and <code>maxUnavailable</code> how many may be down. Readiness probes decide when a new pod receives traffic. Both versions run side by side during the update, so they must be compatible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0501","card":"q-aws-061-deployment-strategies-compared","sec":"aws-s062","q":"Why is the Recreate strategy still used sometimes?","a":"<p>When two versions cannot run together (an incompatible database schema, or a singleton job) and downtime is acceptable, for example in dev environments or batch systems. It is the simplest and cheapest, but causes downtime.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0502","card":"q-aws-062-comparison-with-blue-green","sec":"aws-s063","q":"How does each strategy compare with blue-green?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Canary</strong>: safer for testing, slower lead time.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rolling</strong>: resource-efficient, risks disruptions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Feature flag</strong>: ideal for feature releases.</li>\n  <li><strong>A/B</strong>: user-focused, complements data systems.</li>\n  <li><strong>Shadow</strong>: safe testing, high resource use.</li>\n  <li><strong>Recreate</strong>: unsuitable for high availability.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0503","card":"q-aws-062-comparison-with-blue-green","sec":"aws-s063","q":"How do you choose between canary, rolling and blue-green for a stateless web service?","a":"<p>Rolling is the cheapest default when versions can coexist. Canary adds risk control with metric-based gates for sensitive changes. Blue-green gives instant rollback and clean cutover when you can afford duplicate capacity or need to test the full stack first. Stateful or schema-changing releases need extra care with any of them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0504","card":"q-aws-063-ec2-launch-template","sec":"aws-s064","q":"How do you build EC2 Auto Scaling with Terraform?","a":"<p>Start with a launch template.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_launch_template&quot; &quot;web_template&quot; {\n  name_prefix   = &quot;web-lt&quot;\n  image_id      = &quot;ami-0abcdef1234567890&quot;\n  instance_type = &quot;t3.micro&quot;\n  key_name      = &quot;my-key&quot;\n  network_interfaces {\n    associate_public_ip_address = true\n    security_groups             = [aws_security_group.web_sg.id]\n  }\n  user_data = base64encode(&lt;&lt;-EOF\n              #!/bin/bash\n              echo &quot;Hello from AutoScaling EC2&quot; &gt; index.html\n              nohup python3 -m http.server 80 &amp;\n              EOF\n            )\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0505","card":"q-aws-063-ec2-launch-template","sec":"aws-s064","q":"In Terraform, what is the difference between <code>name</code> and <code>name_prefix</code> on an <code>aws_launch_template</code>?","a":"<p><code>name</code> gives the template a fixed name. <code>name_prefix</code> makes Terraform add a unique suffix. The fixed name collides on re-create, while a prefix is safer when a replacement needs to be created before the old one is deleted.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_launch_template&quot; &quot;web_template&quot; {\n  name_prefix   = &quot;web-lt&quot;\n  image_id      = &quot;ami-0abcdef1234567890&quot;\n  instance_type = &quot;t3.micro&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0506","card":"q-aws-063-ec2-launch-template","sec":"aws-s064","q":"Why is <code>user_data</code> wrapped in <code>base64encode(...)</code> in a Terraform <code>aws_launch_template</code>?","a":"<p>Launch templates require user data to be base64-encoded. <code>aws_instance</code> accepts plain text for <code>user_data</code>, but <code>aws_launch_template</code> expects the encoded value, so wrap it with <code>base64encode</code> (or use <code>filebase64</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0507","card":"q-aws-063-ec2-launch-template","sec":"aws-s064","q":"What does <code>&lt;&lt;-EOF</code> do in a Terraform string, and how does it differ from <code>&lt;&lt;EOF</code>?","a":"<p>Both start a heredoc. The <code>-</code> form strips leading indentation, so you can indent the script to match the HCL block while the script itself starts at the left margin. The plain form keeps all whitespace, which would put spaces before <code>#!/bin/bash</code> and break the shebang.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0508","card":"q-aws-063-ec2-launch-template","sec":"aws-s064","q":"Where do you set the security group in a launch template that defines a <code>network_interfaces</code> block, and what must you avoid?","a":"<p>Set <code>security_groups</code> inside the <code>network_interfaces</code> block. Do not also set <code>vpc_security_group_ids</code> at the top level, because the two conflict (the template cannot specify the groups in both places).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>network_interfaces {\n  associate_public_ip_address = true\n  security_groups             = [aws_security_group.web_sg.id]\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0509","card":"q-aws-063-ec2-launch-template","sec":"aws-s064","q":"Why reference <code>aws_security_group.web_sg.id</code> rather than writing the ID as a string in Terraform?","a":"<p>The reference creates an implicit dependency, so Terraform creates the security group first and passes in its real ID. A hard-coded string would tie the config to a particular resource and break across environments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0510","card":"q-aws-063-ec2-launch-template","sec":"aws-s064","q":"When you change a launch template in Terraform, how do instances in the Auto Scaling group pick up the change?","a":"<p>A new template version is created, but running instances stay as they are. Point the group at <code>version = \"$Latest\"</code> (or the new version) and add an <code>instance_refresh</code> block to the Auto Scaling group so it replaces instances gradually.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0511","card":"q-aws-064-auto-scaling-group-and-scaling-policy","sec":"aws-s065","q":"In Terraform, what does <code>target_group_arns</code> on an <code>aws_autoscaling_group</code> do?","a":"<p>It registers every instance the group launches with the ALB target group, and deregisters it when the group terminates it, so you never attach instances by hand.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_autoscaling_group&quot; &quot;web_asg&quot; {\n  ...\n  target_group_arns = [aws_lb_target_group.web_tg.arn]\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0512","card":"q-aws-064-auto-scaling-group-and-scaling-policy","sec":"aws-s065","q":"A Terraform <code>aws_autoscaling_group</code> sets <code>desired_capacity = 2</code>, but scaling has moved the group to 4 instances. What happens on the next <code>terraform apply</code>, and how do you avoid it?","a":"<p>Terraform sees the difference as drift and tries to set the desired capacity back to 2, which can remove instances that scaling added. Either leave <code>desired_capacity</code> out of the configuration or add <code>lifecycle { ignore_changes = [desired_capacity] }</code> so that scaling policies own that value.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0513","card":"q-aws-064-auto-scaling-group-and-scaling-policy","sec":"aws-s065","q":"In AWS Auto Scaling, what kind of policy does a Terraform <code>aws_autoscaling_policy</code> create when you set <code>scaling_adjustment</code> and <code>adjustment_type</code>, and what is its limit compared with target tracking?","a":"<p>It is a simple scaling policy: when an alarm fires it adds a fixed amount (here one instance) and then waits for the cooldown. It does nothing by itself, since an alarm must invoke it. It reacts in fixed steps and does not size the response to the breach, which target tracking and step scaling do better.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_autoscaling_policy&quot; &quot;scale_out&quot; {\n  scaling_adjustment = 1\n  adjustment_type    = &quot;ChangeInCapacity&quot;\n  cooldown           = 300\n  ...\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0514","card":"q-aws-065-cloudwatch-alarm-that-triggers-scaling","sec":"aws-s066","q":"In AWS, which part of a Terraform <code>aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm</code> makes an Auto Scaling group scale, and what happens if you leave it out?","a":"<p><code>alarm_actions</code> holds the ARN of the scaling policy to run when the alarm enters the <code>ALARM</code> state. Without it the alarm only changes state and can notify nothing, so the group never scales. The alarm is the detector and the policy is the action.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm&quot; &quot;high_cpu&quot; {\n  ...\n  alarm_actions = [aws_autoscaling_policy.scale_out.arn]\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0515","card":"q-aws-065-cloudwatch-alarm-that-triggers-scaling","sec":"aws-s066","q":"In a CloudWatch alarm, how do you read <code>evaluation_periods = 2</code>, <code>period = 60</code>, <code>statistic = \"Average\"</code>, <code>threshold = 70</code> and <code>comparison_operator = \"GreaterThanThreshold\"</code> together?","a":"<p>The alarm goes to <code>ALARM</code> when the average of the metric over a 60-second period is greater than 70 for 2 periods in a row, so after about two minutes of sustained breach. A single spike is ignored because both periods must breach.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0516","card":"q-aws-065-cloudwatch-alarm-that-triggers-scaling","sec":"aws-s066","q":"When would you scale an Auto Scaling group on ALB request count per target instead of average CPU?","a":"<p>When load shows up as traffic before CPU rises, or when the work is I/O bound and CPU stays low. <code>ALBRequestCountPerTarget</code> follows demand directly and scales evenly across instances. CPU is a reasonable default for compute-heavy work. It is also a target tracking metric, so you do not need to write the alarm yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0517","card":"q-aws-066-alb-target-group-and-listener","sec":"aws-s067","q":"Why must an AWS Application Load Balancer be given subnets in at least two Availability Zones?","a":"<p>An ALB is a regional service with nodes in each AZ you choose. AWS requires at least two AZs so the load balancer survives an AZ failure. In Terraform this is why <code>aws_lb</code> takes two or more subnets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0518","card":"q-aws-066-alb-target-group-and-listener","sec":"aws-s067","q":"In Terraform, what does <code>internal = false</code> on an <code>aws_lb</code> do, and what do you need in the subnets?","a":"<p>It makes the ALB internet-facing, so its nodes get public IPs and public DNS. The subnets must be public: they route to an internet gateway. <code>internal = true</code> gives private IPs only, for traffic inside the VPC.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0519","card":"q-aws-066-alb-target-group-and-listener","sec":"aws-s067","q":"In an AWS ALB setup, how should the security groups of the load balancer and the instances relate?","a":"<p>The ALB security group allows inbound 80 or 443 from the internet. The instance security group allows the app port only from the ALB security group, not from <code>0.0.0.0/0</code>. Then instances cannot be reached directly, and traffic is forced through the load balancer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0520","card":"q-aws-066-alb-target-group-and-listener","sec":"aws-s067","q":"In an AWS ALB target group, what do the target group's <code>port</code> and <code>protocol</code> describe, and why can they differ from the listener's?","a":"<p>They describe how the load balancer talks to the targets. The listener's port and protocol describe how clients talk to the load balancer. They can differ: for example, a listener on HTTPS 443 terminates TLS and forwards plain HTTP 8080 to the targets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0521","card":"q-aws-066-alb-target-group-and-listener","sec":"aws-s067","q":"What are the target types of an ALB target group, and when do you use each?","a":"<p><code>instance</code> registers EC2 instances (the usual choice with Auto Scaling). <code>ip</code> registers IP addresses, such as pods with VPC-routable IPs or on-premises hosts reached over VPN or Direct Connect. <code>lambda</code> sends requests to a Lambda function. <code>alb</code> registers an Application Load Balancer as the target of a Network Load Balancer, which gives an ALB static IP addresses.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0522","card":"q-aws-066-alb-target-group-and-listener","sec":"aws-s067","q":"In an ALB listener, what does <code>default_action { type = \"forward\" ... }</code> do, and which other action types exist?","a":"<p>It sends any request that matches no other rule to the target group. Other action types are <code>redirect</code> (for example HTTP to HTTPS), <code>fixed-response</code> (return a set status and body) and the authentication actions (<code>authenticate-cognito</code>, <code>authenticate-oidc</code>). Path or host based routing uses extra listener rules.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_lb_listener&quot; &quot;web_listener&quot; {\n  port     = 80\n  protocol = &quot;HTTP&quot;\n  default_action {\n    type             = &quot;forward&quot;\n    target_group_arn = aws_lb_target_group.web_tg.arn\n  }\n  ...\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0523","card":"q-aws-066-alb-target-group-and-listener","sec":"aws-s067","q":"How do you redirect all HTTP traffic to HTTPS on an AWS ALB?","a":"<p>Create two listeners. The HTTPS listener on 443 holds the ACM certificate and forwards to the target group. The HTTP listener on 80 has a <code>redirect</code> default action to protocol HTTPS and port 443 with status 301. No instance needs to handle the redirect.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0524","card":"q-aws-066-alb-target-group-and-listener","sec":"aws-s067","q":"What settings of an ALB target group health check decide how fast a bad instance is taken out of rotation?","a":"<p><code>path</code> and <code>port</code> choose what is probed, <code>interval</code> sets the time between probes, <code>timeout</code> sets how long to wait for a reply, and <code>unhealthy_threshold</code> sets how many consecutive failures mark the target unhealthy. <code>healthy_threshold</code> sets how many successes bring it back. A short interval and a low threshold remove failures fast but can flap on slow responses, so the health path should be a light endpoint that checks the app itself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0525","card":"q-aws-066-alb-target-group-and-listener","sec":"aws-s067","q":"A target group health check path points at a page that calls the database. What can go wrong in an ALB setup?","a":"<p>When the database has a problem, every target fails its check at once. The load balancer then has no healthy target, and the whole site returns 503 even though the web servers are fine. A health check should test that the process can serve requests. Deep dependency checks belong in monitoring or a separate endpoint.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0526","card":"q-aws-067-alb-with-a-target-group-attachment","sec":"aws-s068","q":"How does an ALB with a target group work?","a":"<p>Attachments link instances to the target group.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_lb_target_group_attachment&quot; &quot;example&quot; {\n  target_group_arn = aws_lb_target_group.web_tg.arn\n  target_id        = aws_instance.example.id\n  port             = 80\n}</code></pre></div>\n<ul>\n  <li>The ALB is created in the given subnets and is internet-facing.</li>\n  <li>The target group routes traffic to EC2 instances.</li>\n  <li>Request flow: a user request hits the ALB, the ALB evaluates rules and selects a target group, the target group routes to a healthy instance, and the instance returns the response through the ALB.</li>\n  <li>Benefits: scalability (traffic spread across instances), high availability (routes only to healthy instances) and flexibility (supports various targets).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0527","card":"q-aws-067-alb-with-a-target-group-attachment","sec":"aws-s068","q":"In Terraform, when do you use <code>aws_lb_target_group_attachment</code>, and why not use it with an Auto Scaling group?","a":"<p>Use it to register a fixed, known instance (or IP) with a target group. An Auto Scaling group creates and removes instances itself, so manual attachments would go stale. Give the group <code>target_group_arns</code> (or an <code>aws_autoscaling_attachment</code>) and it registers its own instances.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0528","card":"q-aws-067-alb-with-a-target-group-attachment","sec":"aws-s068","q":"What does the <code>port</code> argument of <code>aws_lb_target_group_attachment</code> do, and what happens if you leave it out?","a":"<p>It overrides the port the load balancer uses for that target. If you leave it out, the target group's port is used. Overriding helps when several processes on one instance listen on different ports and all join the same target group.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0529","card":"q-aws-067-alb-with-a-target-group-attachment","sec":"aws-s068","q":"In an ALB setup, which component decides that an instance gets no traffic when it is unhealthy?","a":"<p>The target group's health check. The load balancer probes each target on the configured path and port, and it only routes requests to targets that pass. Unhealthy targets stay registered but get no traffic until they pass again. This is the high availability benefit of using a target group.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0530","card":"q-aws-068-kubernetes-autoscaling-on-eks-iam-for-th","sec":"aws-s069","q":"How does Kubernetes autoscaling work on EKS with an ALB?","a":"<p>First, the ALB controller needs an IAM policy and a role that the service account can assume through the cluster's OIDC provider.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_iam_policy&quot; &quot;alb_controller&quot; {\n  name   = &quot;AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy&quot;\n  policy = file(&quot;iam_policy_alb_controller.json&quot;)\n}\ndata &quot;aws_eks_cluster&quot; &quot;eks&quot; {\n  name = &quot;your-cluster-name&quot;\n}\ndata &quot;aws_iam_policy_document&quot; &quot;assume_role&quot; {\n  statement {\n    effect = &quot;Allow&quot;\n    principals {\n      type        = &quot;Federated&quot;\n      identifiers = [data.aws_eks_cluster.eks.identity[0].oidc.issuer]\n    }\n    actions = [&quot;sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity&quot;]\n    condition {\n      test     = &quot;StringEquals&quot;\n      variable = &quot;${replace(data.aws_eks_cluster.eks.identity[0].oidc.issuer, &quot;https://&quot;, &quot;&quot;)}:sub&quot;\n      values   = [&quot;system:serviceaccount:kube-system:aws-load-balancer-controller&quot;]\n    }\n  }\n}\nresource &quot;aws_iam_role&quot; &quot;alb_sa_role&quot; {\n  name               = &quot;AmazonEKSLoadBalancerControllerRole&quot;\n  assume_role_policy = data.aws_iam_policy_document.assume_role.json\n}\nresource &quot;aws_iam_role_policy_attachment&quot; &quot;alb_attach&quot; {\n  role       = aws_iam_role.alb_sa_role.name\n  policy_arn = aws_iam_policy.alb_controller.arn\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0531","card":"q-aws-068-kubernetes-autoscaling-on-eks-iam-for-th","sec":"aws-s069","q":"Why does the AWS Load Balancer Controller on EKS need an IAM role at all?","a":"<p>The controller runs as a pod and calls AWS APIs to create and manage ALBs, target groups, listeners and security group rules in your account. Those calls need IAM permissions, which AWS publishes as a policy for the controller. Without a role the controller cannot create the load balancer, and the Ingress never gets an address.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0532","card":"q-aws-068-kubernetes-autoscaling-on-eks-iam-for-th","sec":"aws-s069","q":"What is IRSA (IAM Roles for Service Accounts) on EKS, and why use it instead of the node's instance role?","a":"<p>IRSA lets one Kubernetes service account assume one IAM role through the cluster's OIDC identity provider, so permissions are scoped per workload. With the node role, every pod on that node inherits the same permissions, which breaks least privilege. IRSA also gives short-lived credentials without any stored keys.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0533","card":"q-aws-068-kubernetes-autoscaling-on-eks-iam-for-th","sec":"aws-s069","q":"In an EKS IRSA trust policy, what do the <code>sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity</code> action and the <code>:sub</code> condition do?","a":"<p><code>AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity</code> lets a caller exchange an OIDC token (the service account token) for temporary AWS credentials. The <code>sub</code> condition restricts the role to one namespace and service account name. Without it, any service account in the cluster could assume the role.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>actions = [&quot;sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity&quot;]\ncondition {\n  test     = &quot;StringEquals&quot;\n  variable = &quot;${...}:sub&quot;\n  values   = [&quot;system:serviceaccount:kube-system:aws-load-balancer-controller&quot;]\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0534","card":"q-aws-068-kubernetes-autoscaling-on-eks-iam-for-th","sec":"aws-s069","q":"What must exist in the AWS account before an EKS service account can assume a role through IRSA?","a":"<p>An IAM OIDC identity provider that points to the cluster's OIDC issuer URL. The role's trust policy names that provider as the federated principal. The cluster has an issuer URL by default, but the provider must be created in IAM once per cluster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0535","card":"q-aws-068-kubernetes-autoscaling-on-eks-iam-for-th","sec":"aws-s069","q":"What is EKS Pod Identity, and how does it differ from IRSA?","a":"<p>EKS Pod Identity (launched in late 2023) is a newer way to give pods IAM roles. You create an association between a service account and a role in EKS, with no OIDC provider and no annotation on the service account, and the trust policy names the <code>pods.eks.amazonaws.com</code> service. It needs the Pod Identity Agent add-on. IRSA still works and is the only choice for some setups such as self-managed clusters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0536","card":"q-aws-069-alb-controller-service-account-and-ingre","sec":"aws-s070","q":"In EKS, what does the <code>eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn</code> annotation on a service account do?","a":"<p>It ties the service account to an IAM role for IRSA. A webhook in the cluster sees the annotation when a pod starts and injects the <code>AWS_ROLE_ARN</code> and <code>AWS_WEB_IDENTITY_TOKEN_FILE</code> environment variables and a projected token volume. The AWS SDK in the pod then assumes the role with that token.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: ServiceAccount\nmetadata:\n  name: aws-load-balancer-controller\n  namespace: kube-system\n  annotations:\n    eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn: arn:aws:iam::&lt;account_id&gt;:role/AmazonEKSLoadBalancerControllerRole</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0537","card":"q-aws-069-alb-controller-service-account-and-ingre","sec":"aws-s070","q":"In the AWS Load Balancer Controller, what is the difference between <code>alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/target-type: ip</code> and <code>instance</code>?","a":"<p>With <code>ip</code>, the ALB registers pod IPs directly and sends traffic straight to pods (needs the VPC CNI so pod IPs are routable in the VPC). It skips a hop and works with a <code>ClusterIP</code> Service. With <code>instance</code>, the ALB sends traffic to node ports, so the Service must be <code>NodePort</code> or <code>LoadBalancer</code>, and <code>kube-proxy</code> forwards to a pod.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0538","card":"q-aws-069-alb-controller-service-account-and-ingre","sec":"aws-s070","q":"In an EKS Ingress, what does <code>alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/scheme: internet-facing</code> choose, and what is the alternative?","a":"<p>It makes the controller create a public ALB. The alternative is <code>internal</code>, which creates a private ALB reachable only inside the VPC or over connected networks. If you omit the annotation the default is internal.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0539","card":"q-aws-069-alb-controller-service-account-and-ingre","sec":"aws-s070","q":"How does an Ingress on EKS lead to an AWS Application Load Balancer being created?","a":"<p>The Ingress object is only a description. The AWS Load Balancer Controller watches Ingress resources with the ALB class, then calls AWS to create an ALB, a target group per backend Service, listeners and rules, and it keeps them in sync. Without the controller installed, the Ingress does nothing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0540","card":"q-aws-069-alb-controller-service-account-and-ingre","sec":"aws-s070","q":"In Kubernetes, what is the difference between the <code>kubernetes.io/ingress.class: alb</code> annotation and <code>ingressClassName</code>?","a":"<p>Both select which controller handles an Ingress. The annotation is the older, deprecated way (deprecated since Kubernetes 1.18). <code>spec.ingressClassName</code> refers to an <code>IngressClass</code> object and is the supported way. The AWS controller still honors the annotation, but new manifests should use <code>ingressClassName: alb</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0541","card":"q-aws-069-alb-controller-service-account-and-ingre","sec":"aws-s070","q":"When would you use a Kubernetes Service of type <code>LoadBalancer</code> instead of an Ingress on EKS?","a":"<p>Use <code>LoadBalancer</code> for non-HTTP traffic or when one service needs its own layer 4 load balancer (it normally creates an NLB through the AWS controller). Use an Ingress for HTTP and HTTPS routing, where one ALB can serve many services by host and path, which is cheaper than a load balancer per service.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0542","card":"q-aws-070-app-deployment-and-hpa","sec":"aws-s071","q":"In a Kubernetes HPA on CPU utilization, why must the Deployment's containers define <code>resources.requests.cpu</code>?","a":"<p>CPU utilization is measured as a percentage of the pod's request. With <code>averageUtilization: 60</code> and a request of 100m, the target is 60m per pod. With no request the HPA cannot compute a percentage and reports <code>&lt;unknown&gt;</code>, and no scaling happens.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resources:\n  requests:\n    cpu: &quot;100m&quot;\n  limits:\n    cpu: &quot;200m&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0543","card":"q-aws-070-app-deployment-and-hpa","sec":"aws-s071","q":"How does the Kubernetes HPA decide how many replicas it wants?","a":"<p>It uses <code>desiredReplicas = ceil(currentReplicas * currentMetric / targetMetric)</code>. For example, 2 pods at 120% of target CPU gives <code>ceil(2 * 1.2)</code> = 3 pods. The result is clamped between <code>minReplicas</code> and <code>maxReplicas</code>, and small differences within a tolerance of about 10% are ignored.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0544","card":"q-aws-070-app-deployment-and-hpa","sec":"aws-s071","q":"An HPA shows <code>&lt;unknown&gt;/60%</code> as its CPU target on EKS. What do you check?","a":"<p>First, that the Metrics Server is installed and healthy (<code>kubectl top pods</code> should work), because the HPA reads resource metrics through it. Then that the target containers set CPU requests, and that the <code>scaleTargetRef</code> points to a Deployment that exists.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0545","card":"q-aws-070-app-deployment-and-hpa","sec":"aws-s071","q":"In a HorizontalPodAutoscaler, what does <code>scaleTargetRef</code> do?","a":"<p>It names the workload whose replica count the HPA controls, by <code>apiVersion</code>, <code>kind</code> and <code>name</code> (here a Deployment). The HPA writes the <code>replicas</code> field on that object.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0546","card":"q-aws-070-app-deployment-and-hpa","sec":"aws-s071","q":"What happens if you set <code>replicas</code> in a Deployment and also attach an HPA, and you run <code>kubectl scale</code> by hand?","a":"<p>The HPA owns the replica count, so within one sync period it overwrites a manual value with what the metrics demand. A <code>replicas</code> value in the manifest can also reset the count on every <code>kubectl apply</code>, so many teams leave <code>replicas</code> out of the manifest when an HPA is used.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0547","card":"q-aws-070-app-deployment-and-hpa","sec":"aws-s071","q":"What is the difference between the HPA and the Cluster Autoscaler (or Karpenter) on EKS, and why might you need both?","a":"<p>The HPA changes the number of pods. The Cluster Autoscaler or Karpenter changes the number of nodes. If the HPA creates pods that no node can hold, they stay <code>Pending</code>, and the node autoscaler then adds nodes. Without it, pod scaling stops at the capacity of the existing nodes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0548","card":"q-aws-070-app-deployment-and-hpa","sec":"aws-s071","q":"What are the <code>apiVersion</code> options for HPA, and which should new manifests use?","a":"<p><code>autoscaling/v2</code> is the current stable version (Kubernetes 1.23 and later). It supports multiple metrics and the <code>behavior</code> field for scale-up and scale-down rate. <code>autoscaling/v2beta2</code> was removed in Kubernetes 1.26, and <code>autoscaling/v1</code> supports only CPU.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0549","card":"q-aws-070-app-deployment-and-hpa","sec":"aws-s071","q":"Why does an HPA scale down more slowly than it scales up?","a":"<p>Scale-down uses a stabilization window (default 300 seconds): the HPA picks the highest recommendation seen in that window, so a short dip in load does not remove pods that would soon be needed again. You can tune this with the <code>behavior</code> field.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0550","card":"q-aws-070-app-deployment-and-hpa","sec":"aws-s071","q":"What is the difference between the Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler and the Vertical Pod Autoscaler?","a":"<p>The HPA changes the number of pod replicas and suits stateless services. The VPA changes the CPU and memory requests of the pods (by recreating them), which suits workloads that cannot run many copies. VPA is a separate add-on, not part of core Kubernetes. Do not let the HPA and VPA both act on the same CPU or memory metric, because they fight each other.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0551","card":"q-aws-070-app-deployment-and-hpa","sec":"aws-s071","q":"How can a Kubernetes workload scale on a metric other than CPU and memory, such as queue length or requests per second?","a":"<p>With <code>autoscaling/v2</code> the HPA supports custom metrics (<code>Pods</code> and <code>Object</code> types) and external metrics, which need an adapter such as the Prometheus adapter to serve them through the metrics APIs. KEDA is a common alternative: it watches event systems such as SQS, Kafka or Prometheus queries and drives the HPA, and it can scale a Deployment down to zero.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0552","card":"q-aws-070-app-deployment-and-hpa","sec":"aws-s071","q":"In an <code>autoscaling/v2</code> HPA, which fields would you set to make scale-up fast but scale-down gradual?","a":"<p>The <code>behavior</code> block sets the rate limits. Here scale-up can double the replicas every minute, while scale-down waits for a 300-second stabilization window and then removes at most one pod per minute. This protects against flapping and capacity drops after a short dip in load.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>behavior:\n  scaleUp:\n    policies:\n    - type: Percent\n      value: 100\n      periodSeconds: 60\n  scaleDown:\n    stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300\n    policies:\n    - type: Pods\n      value: 1\n      periodSeconds: 60</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0553","card":"q-aws-071-eks-cluster-with-one-node-group","sec":"aws-s072","q":"How do you create an EKS cluster with a single node group in Terraform?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_eks_cluster&quot; &quot;example&quot; {\n  name     = &quot;my-eks-cluster&quot;\n  role_arn = aws_iam_role.eks_role.arn\n  vpc_config {\n    subnet_ids = [aws_subnet.private1.id, aws_subnet.private2.id]\n  }\n}\nresource &quot;aws_eks_node_group&quot; &quot;example&quot; {\n  cluster_name    = aws_eks_cluster.example.name\n  node_group_name = &quot;my-node-group&quot;\n  node_role_arn   = aws_iam_role.node_role.arn\n  subnet_ids      = [aws_subnet.private1.id, aws_subnet.private2.id]\n  scaling_config {\n    desired_size = 1\n    max_size     = 2\n    min_size     = 1\n  }\n}\nresource &quot;aws_iam_role&quot; &quot;eks_role&quot; {\n  name = &quot;eks-role&quot;\n  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({\n    Version = &quot;2012-10-17&quot;\n    Statement = [{\n      Action = &quot;sts:AssumeRole&quot;\n      Effect = &quot;Allow&quot;\n      Principal = { Service = &quot;eks.amazonaws.com&quot; }\n    }]\n  })\n}\nresource &quot;aws_iam_role&quot; &quot;node_role&quot; {\n  name = &quot;eks-node-role&quot;\n  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({\n    Version = &quot;2012-10-17&quot;\n    Statement = [{\n      Action = &quot;sts:AssumeRole&quot;\n      Effect = &quot;Allow&quot;\n      Principal = { Service = &quot;ec2.amazonaws.com&quot; }\n    }]\n  })\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0554","card":"q-aws-071-eks-cluster-with-one-node-group","sec":"aws-s072","q":"Why does an EKS cluster and its node group each need a separate IAM role in Terraform?","a":"<p>The cluster role is assumed by the EKS service (<code>eks.amazonaws.com</code>) to manage control plane resources such as network interfaces in your VPC. The node role is assumed by the worker EC2 instances (<code>ec2.amazonaws.com</code>) so the kubelet can join the cluster and pull images. They trust different principals and need different policies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0555","card":"q-aws-071-eks-cluster-with-one-node-group","sec":"aws-s072","q":"Which managed policies does an EKS managed node group role normally need?","a":"<p><code>AmazonEKSWorkerNodePolicy</code>, <code>AmazonEKS_CNI_Policy</code> (for the VPC CNI plugin) and <code>AmazonEC2ContainerRegistryReadOnly</code> (to pull images from ECR). The cluster role needs <code>AmazonEKSClusterPolicy</code>. Missing attachments are a common reason nodes fail to join.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0556","card":"q-aws-071-eks-cluster-with-one-node-group","sec":"aws-s072","q":"In Terraform, why add <code>depends_on</code> for the IAM policy attachments of an <code>aws_eks_cluster</code> or <code>aws_eks_node_group</code>?","a":"<p>The cluster and node group reference only the role, not the attachments, so Terraform could create the cluster before the policies are attached. Then creation fails, or destruction removes the policy before the cluster is gone. <code>depends_on</code> on the attachments fixes the order.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0557","card":"q-aws-071-eks-cluster-with-one-node-group","sec":"aws-s072","q":"What are the options for running worker capacity on EKS, and how do you choose?","a":"<p>Managed node groups: AWS manages the Auto Scaling group and node upgrades, which suits most teams. Self-managed nodes: full control of the AMI and bootstrap, at the cost of more work. Fargate: serverless pods with no nodes to patch, but with limits (no DaemonSets, no GPUs, higher per-pod cost). Karpenter: dynamic node provisioning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0558","card":"q-aws-071-eks-cluster-with-one-node-group","sec":"aws-s072","q":"In an EKS <code>aws_eks_node_group</code>, why put the nodes in private subnets, and what do they then need for outbound traffic?","a":"<p>Private subnets keep worker nodes off the internet. They still need to reach the EKS API endpoint, ECR and other AWS APIs, so they need a NAT gateway or VPC endpoints for those services.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0559","card":"q-aws-071-eks-cluster-with-one-node-group","sec":"aws-s072","q":"What does the <code>scaling_config</code> block of an <code>aws_eks_node_group</code> control, and how does it relate to an Auto Scaling group?","a":"<p>It sets the minimum, maximum and desired node count. EKS creates an Auto Scaling group for the node group and applies those values to it. The node group does not scale on its own; the Cluster Autoscaler or Karpenter changes the desired size when pods cannot be scheduled.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0560","card":"q-aws-072-vpc-peering","sec":"aws-s073","q":"How do you set up VPC peering in Terraform?","a":"<p>Create the peering connection, then add a route in each VPC to the other's CIDR.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_vpc&quot; &quot;vpc_a&quot; { cidr_block = &quot;10.0.0.0/16&quot; }\nresource &quot;aws_vpc&quot; &quot;vpc_b&quot; { cidr_block = &quot;10.1.0.0/16&quot; }\nresource &quot;aws_vpc_peering_connection&quot; &quot;peer&quot; {\n  vpc_id        = aws_vpc.vpc_a.id\n  peer_vpc_id   = aws_vpc.vpc_b.id\n  auto_accept   = true\n}\nresource &quot;aws_route&quot; &quot;vpc_a_route&quot; {\n  route_table_id            = aws_vpc.vpc_a.main_route_table_id\n  destination_cidr_block    = aws_vpc.vpc_b.cidr_block\n  vpc_peering_connection_id = aws_vpc_peering_connection.peer.id\n}\nresource &quot;aws_route&quot; &quot;vpc_b_route&quot; {\n  route_table_id            = aws_vpc.vpc_b.main_route_table_id\n  destination_cidr_block    = aws_vpc.vpc_a.cidr_block\n  vpc_peering_connection_id = aws_vpc_peering_connection.peer.id\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0561","card":"q-aws-072-vpc-peering","sec":"aws-s073","q":"Is AWS VPC peering transitive, and what does that mean for a hub-and-spoke design?","a":"<p>No. If VPC A is peered with B and B with C, A cannot reach C through B. Each pair needs its own peering connection, which grows into a full mesh. For many VPCs, use a Transit Gateway as the hub.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0562","card":"q-aws-072-vpc-peering","sec":"aws-s073","q":"Why must the VPCs in a peering connection not have overlapping CIDR blocks?","a":"<p>Routes are based on the destination CIDR. If both VPCs use the same range, a route cannot tell whether a destination is local or in the peer, so AWS rejects peering with overlapping ranges. Plan non-overlapping CIDRs from the start.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0563","card":"q-aws-072-vpc-peering","sec":"aws-s073","q":"In Terraform, why does VPC peering need an <code>aws_route</code> in each VPC, and not only the peering connection?","a":"<p>The peering connection creates the link, but traffic only uses it if route tables send it there. Each side needs a route whose destination is the other VPC's CIDR and whose target is the peering connection. Security groups and network ACLs must also allow the traffic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0564","card":"q-aws-072-vpc-peering","sec":"aws-s073","q":"In Terraform, when does <code>auto_accept = true</code> work on an <code>aws_vpc_peering_connection</code>, and what do you do otherwise?","a":"<p>It works only when both VPCs are in the same account and region, because Terraform can then accept its own request. For cross-account or cross-region peering the other side must accept, using <code>aws_vpc_peering_connection_accepter</code> with the second account's provider.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0565","card":"q-aws-072-vpc-peering","sec":"aws-s073","q":"In Terraform, when routes are added to <code>aws_vpc.vpc_a.main_route_table_id</code>, what pitfall exists if subnets use their own route tables?","a":"<p>The route goes into the main route table only. Subnets associated with a custom route table do not use it, so their traffic does not take the peering route. Add the route to every route table whose subnets need the peer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0566","card":"q-aws-072-vpc-peering","sec":"aws-s073","q":"What is the difference between VPC peering and Transit Gateway?","a":"<p>Peering is a one-to-one, non-transitive link with no hourly charge (data transfer is billed). Transit Gateway is a regional hub that connects many VPCs and on-premises networks with transitive routing and central route tables, at a cost per attachment and per GB. Use peering for a few VPCs and Transit Gateway when the count grows.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0567","card":"q-aws-073-s3-bucket-with-encryption-versioning-and","sec":"aws-s074","q":"How do you create a secure S3 bucket in Terraform?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_s3_bucket&quot; &quot;bucket&quot; { bucket = &quot;my-encrypted-bucket&quot; }\nresource &quot;aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration&quot; &quot;encryption&quot; {\n  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.bucket.bucket\n  rule {\n    apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {\n      sse_algorithm = &quot;AES256&quot;\n    }\n  }\n}\nresource &quot;aws_s3_bucket_versioning&quot; &quot;versioning&quot; {\n  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.bucket.bucket\n  versioning_configuration {\n    status = &quot;Enabled&quot;\n  }\n}\nresource &quot;aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block&quot; &quot;block&quot; {\n  bucket                  = aws_s3_bucket.bucket.bucket\n  block_public_acls       = true\n  block_public_policy     = true\n  ignore_public_acls      = true\n  restrict_public_buckets = true\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0568","card":"q-aws-073-s3-bucket-with-encryption-versioning-and","sec":"aws-s074","q":"In the AWS Terraform provider, why are S3 encryption, versioning and public access block separate resources and not arguments on <code>aws_s3_bucket</code>?","a":"<p>From version 4 of the AWS provider, bucket settings were split into their own resources, such as <code>aws_s3_bucket_versioning</code> and <code>aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration</code>. The old inline arguments are deprecated. Each resource names the bucket it configures.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0569","card":"q-aws-073-s3-bucket-with-encryption-versioning-and","sec":"aws-s074","q":"What are the four settings of an S3 public access block, and what does each stop?","a":"<p><code>block_public_acls</code> rejects new public ACLs. <code>ignore_public_acls</code> ignores existing public ACLs. <code>block_public_policy</code> rejects bucket policies that grant public access. <code>restrict_public_buckets</code> limits access to a bucket with a public policy to AWS services and authorized users. Setting all four is the safe default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0570","card":"q-aws-073-s3-bucket-with-encryption-versioning-and","sec":"aws-s074","q":"Can you turn off S3 versioning once it is enabled?","a":"<p>No. You can only suspend it. Existing versions remain and are billed, and new uploads get the version ID <code>null</code>. To control cost, add a lifecycle rule that expires noncurrent versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0571","card":"q-aws-073-s3-bucket-with-encryption-versioning-and","sec":"aws-s074","q":"What does <code>sse_algorithm = \"AES256\"</code> do in an S3 bucket, and how does it differ from <code>aws:kms</code>? (S3, current behavior)","a":"<p><code>AES256</code> is SSE-S3: S3 manages the keys, with no extra cost. <code>aws:kms</code> uses a KMS key, which gives key policies, audit logs in CloudTrail and the option to disable a key, but adds KMS request cost and limits. Since January 2023, S3 encrypts all new objects with SSE-S3 by default, so an explicit rule matters mostly when you want KMS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0572","card":"q-aws-074-rds-instance-with-a-security-group","sec":"aws-s075","q":"How do you create an RDS instance in Terraform?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_db_instance&quot; &quot;example&quot; {\n  allocated_storage    = 20\n  engine               = &quot;mysql&quot;\n  engine_version       = &quot;8.0.28&quot;\n  instance_class       = &quot;db.t2.micro&quot;\n  db_name              = &quot;exampledb&quot;\n  username             = &quot;exampleuser&quot;\n  password             = &quot;&lt;db-password&gt;&quot;\n  parameter_group_name = &quot;default.mysql8.0&quot;\n  skip_final_snapshot  = true\n  vpc_security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.rds.id]\n}\nresource &quot;aws_security_group&quot; &quot;rds&quot; {\n  name        = &quot;rds-sg&quot;\n  description = &quot;Security group for RDS instance&quot;\n  ingress {\n    from_port   = 3306\n    to_port     = 3306\n    protocol    = &quot;tcp&quot;\n    cidr_blocks = [&quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot;]\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Opening 3306 to <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> is for demonstration only. Restrict the source to the application's security group or CIDR in real use.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0573","card":"q-aws-074-rds-instance-with-a-security-group","sec":"aws-s075","q":"What does <code>skip_final_snapshot = true</code> do on an RDS instance, and why is it risky in production?","a":"<p>It lets <code>terraform destroy</code> or a deletion remove the database without taking a final snapshot, so the data is gone with no backup. It is acceptable for throwaway environments. In production leave it <code>false</code> and set <code>final_snapshot_identifier</code>, and consider <code>deletion_protection</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0574","card":"q-aws-074-rds-instance-with-a-security-group","sec":"aws-s075","q":"In AWS, how should the security group of an RDS instance restrict MySQL access?","a":"<p>Allow port 3306 only from the application's security group (using <code>security_groups</code> or <code>source_security_group_id</code>) or from a specific CIDR, and never from <code>0.0.0.0/0</code>. Also keep <code>publicly_accessible = false</code> and put the database in private subnets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0575","card":"q-aws-074-rds-instance-with-a-security-group","sec":"aws-s075","q":"Why should you pin or review <code>engine_version</code> on an <code>aws_db_instance</code>?","a":"<p>A change to the engine version triggers an upgrade, with possible downtime and compatibility issues. Pin the version so changes are deliberate, and check that minor version auto-upgrade settings do not conflict with Terraform, which would show drift.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0576","card":"q-aws-075-add-an-iam-policy-to-a-role","sec":"aws-s076","q":"How do you add an IAM policy to a role in Terraform?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_iam_role&quot; &quot;existing_role&quot; {\n  name = &quot;my-role&quot;\n  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({\n    Version = &quot;2012-10-17&quot;\n    Statement = [{\n      Action = &quot;sts:AssumeRole&quot;\n      Effect = &quot;Allow&quot;\n      Principal = { Service = &quot;ec2.amazonaws.com&quot; }\n    }]\n  })\n}\nresource &quot;aws_iam_policy&quot; &quot;new_policy&quot; {\n  name = &quot;new-policy&quot;\n  policy = jsonencode({\n    Version = &quot;2012-10-17&quot;\n    Statement = [{\n      Action = &quot;s3:GetObject&quot;\n      Effect = &quot;Allow&quot;\n      Resource = &quot;arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket/*&quot;\n    }]\n  })\n}\nresource &quot;aws_iam_role_policy_attachment&quot; &quot;attach&quot; {\n  role       = aws_iam_role.existing_role.name\n  policy_arn = aws_iam_policy.new_policy.arn\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0577","card":"q-aws-075-add-an-iam-policy-to-a-role","sec":"aws-s076","q":"In Terraform, what is the difference between <code>aws_iam_policy</code> plus <code>aws_iam_role_policy_attachment</code> and <code>aws_iam_role_policy</code>?","a":"<p>The first creates a standalone managed policy that you can attach to many roles and that gets its own ARN and version history. <code>aws_iam_role_policy</code> creates an inline policy that lives and dies with one role. Prefer managed policies for reuse, and inline for a policy that must stay tied to a single role.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0578","card":"q-aws-075-add-an-iam-policy-to-a-role","sec":"aws-s076","q":"Why avoid <code>aws_iam_policy_attachment</code> in Terraform and use <code>aws_iam_role_policy_attachment</code> instead?","a":"<p><code>aws_iam_policy_attachment</code> is exclusive: it takes over all attachments of that policy in the account, so it can detach roles and users that Terraform does not know about. <code>aws_iam_role_policy_attachment</code> manages one role and policy pair and leaves the rest alone.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0579","card":"q-aws-075-add-an-iam-policy-to-a-role","sec":"aws-s076","q":"In an IAM policy that allows <code>s3:GetObject</code>, why does the resource end in <code>/*</code> and what do you add to list a bucket?","a":"<p><code>GetObject</code> acts on objects, and the object ARN is the bucket ARN plus <code>/</code> and the key, so <code>/*</code> matches every object. Listing the bucket (<code>s3:ListBucket</code>) acts on the bucket itself, so it needs a separate statement with the resource <code>arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket</code> and no <code>/*</code>. Mixing them up is a common cause of <code>AccessDenied</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Action   = &quot;s3:GetObject&quot;\nEffect   = &quot;Allow&quot;\nResource = &quot;arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket/*&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0580","card":"q-aws-075-add-an-iam-policy-to-a-role","sec":"aws-s076","q":"How do you apply least privilege when writing an IAM policy for a role?","a":"<p>Allow only the actions the workload calls, on only the resources it needs, and add conditions where possible. Start from the access the job really uses (for example IAM Access Analyzer can generate a policy from CloudTrail activity) and avoid <code>*</code> for actions and resources.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0581","card":"q-aws-076-symptoms-of-imagepullbackoff-from-ecr","sec":"aws-s077","q":"A Python Flask API pod on EKS shows ImagePullBackOff when pulling from a private ECR repository. How do you troubleshoot it?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><code>kubectl get pods</code> shows <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> or <code>ErrImagePull</code>.</li>\n  <li>Logs show a failure to pull from ECR, and CloudWatch alerts fire for pod health.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Step 1: verify the pod status.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl get pods -n &lt;namespace&gt;\nkubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Typical error:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Text</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Failed to pull image &quot;&lt;aws-ecr-repo&gt;/app:latest&quot;: pull access denied, repository does not exist or may require &#x27;docker login&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0582","card":"q-aws-076-symptoms-of-imagepullbackoff-from-ecr","sec":"aws-s077","q":"What is the difference between <code>ErrImagePull</code> and <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> in Kubernetes?","a":"<p><code>ErrImagePull</code> means a pull attempt just failed. <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> means Kubernetes has failed repeatedly and is waiting longer between retries (the delay grows up to about five minutes). They describe the same underlying problem at different moments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0583","card":"q-aws-076-symptoms-of-imagepullbackoff-from-ecr","sec":"aws-s077","q":"A pod on EKS is stuck in <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>. What is the fastest way to find the cause?","a":"<p>Run <code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;</code> and read the Events section at the end. It shows the exact pull error: authentication failure, <code>not found</code> for the tag, a network timeout or a manifest problem. Logs are empty because the container never started.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0584","card":"q-aws-076-symptoms-of-imagepullbackoff-from-ecr","sec":"aws-s077","q":"What are the usual causes of <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> for an image in a private ECR repository?","a":"<p>A wrong repository name, tag or region. Missing authentication, such as no <code>imagePullSecrets</code> and no ECR read access on the node role. An expired token. A cross-account repository without a repository policy. No network path to ECR and S3 (no NAT gateway or VPC endpoints). An image built for a different CPU architecture.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0585","card":"q-aws-076-symptoms-of-imagepullbackoff-from-ecr","sec":"aws-s077","q":"On EKS, why do pods usually pull private ECR images from the same account without any <code>imagePullSecrets</code>?","a":"<p>The kubelet uses the node's IAM role to get an ECR token, and the managed node role includes <code>AmazonEC2ContainerRegistryReadOnly</code>. So authentication works through the instance role. You need secrets mostly for other registries, other accounts without a repository policy, or Fargate or non-EKS clusters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0586","card":"q-aws-076-symptoms-of-imagepullbackoff-from-ecr","sec":"aws-s077","q":"How do <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> and <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> differ, and why does it matter for troubleshooting?","a":"<p><code>ImagePullBackOff</code> means the image could not be downloaded, so the container never started: look at registry, auth, network and image name. <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> means the container started and then exited repeatedly: look at logs (<code>kubectl logs --previous</code>), the command and the configuration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0587","card":"q-aws-077-check-the-image-name-and-tag","sec":"aws-s078","q":"What does a full Amazon ECR image reference look like, and which part often causes pull failures?","a":"<p>The pattern is <code>&lt;account&gt;.dkr.ecr.&lt;region&gt;.amazonaws.com/&lt;repository&gt;:&lt;tag&gt;</code>. ECR is regional and per account, so the wrong account ID or region gives a <code>not found</code> or access error even if the repository exists elsewhere. A missing or mistyped tag is the other frequent mistake.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>image: &lt;aws-account-id&gt;.dkr.ecr.&lt;region&gt;.amazonaws.com/app:latest</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0588","card":"q-aws-077-check-the-image-name-and-tag","sec":"aws-s078","q":"Why is using the <code>latest</code> tag for a Kubernetes image on ECR a bad idea?","a":"<p><code>latest</code> is a mutable name. Different nodes can run different images, a rollout does not happen when you push a new <code>latest</code> because the pod spec did not change, and rollback is unclear. Use immutable version or commit tags (and turn on tag immutability in ECR), or pin the image by digest.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0589","card":"q-aws-077-check-the-image-name-and-tag","sec":"aws-s078","q":"What is the default <code>imagePullPolicy</code> in Kubernetes, and how does it affect images on ECR?","a":"<p>If the tag is <code>latest</code> or absent, the default is <code>Always</code>: the kubelet checks the registry each time a container starts, so it needs working auth and network every time. For other tags the default is <code>IfNotPresent</code>, which reuses an image cached on the node. Cached images hide auth problems until a new node starts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0590","card":"q-aws-077-check-the-image-name-and-tag","sec":"aws-s078","q":"Which AWS CLI command checks that an image tag exists in ECR, and what does the output tell you?","a":"<p><code>aws ecr describe-images --repository-name app --region &lt;region&gt;</code> (add <code>--image-ids imageTag=&lt;tag&gt;</code> for one tag). It lists the image digests and tags in that repository, so you can confirm the exact tag. A <code>RepositoryNotFoundException</code> or <code>ImageNotFoundException</code> tells you whether the repository or only the tag is missing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0591","card":"q-aws-077-check-the-image-name-and-tag","sec":"aws-s078","q":"A pod fails with <code>no match for platform in manifest</code> when pulling from ECR. What is the likely cause?","a":"<p>The image was built for a different CPU architecture than the node, for example built on an Apple Silicon laptop (arm64) and run on amd64 nodes. Rebuild with <code>docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64</code> or push a multi-architecture image.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0592","card":"q-aws-078-fix-registry-authentication","sec":"aws-s079","q":"In Kubernetes, what does <code>imagePullSecrets</code> do, and in which namespace must the secret live?","a":"<p>It tells the kubelet which Secret of type <code>kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson</code> holds registry credentials for pulling the pod's images. The secret must be in the same namespace as the pod. A secret in another namespace is not found and the pull fails.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>spec:\n  imagePullSecrets:\n  - name: ecr-secret</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0593","card":"q-aws-078-fix-registry-authentication","sec":"aws-s079","q":"How long is an Amazon ECR authorization token valid, and what does that mean for a Kubernetes secret built from it?","a":"<p>12 hours. A secret created from <code>aws ecr get-login-password</code> stops working after that. New pods, or pods rescheduled onto nodes without the cached image, then fail to pull. Running pods are not affected.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0594","card":"q-aws-078-fix-registry-authentication","sec":"aws-s079","q":"Why is the username <code>AWS</code> used when creating a registry secret for ECR?","a":"<p>ECR uses a fixed username <code>AWS</code> with the temporary token from <code>aws ecr get-login-password</code> as the password. The token carries the identity and permissions of the caller, so only the password changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0595","card":"q-aws-078-fix-registry-authentication","sec":"aws-s079","q":"How can you apply a registry secret to every pod in a namespace without editing each pod spec?","a":"<p>Add the secret to the namespace's service account with <code>imagePullSecrets</code>, for example by patching the <code>default</code> service account. Pods that use that service account then inherit the secret.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0596","card":"q-aws-078-fix-registry-authentication","sec":"aws-s079","q":"What are the options for authenticating to ECR from Kubernetes, ordered from least to most maintenance?","a":"<p>Node IAM role with ECR read access on EKS (nothing to maintain). A kubelet credential provider (<code>ecr-credential-provider</code>) on self-managed nodes. A refreshed <code>docker-registry</code> secret (needs automation because of the 12-hour token). A manually created secret is the least maintainable and breaks regularly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0597","card":"q-aws-079-automate-ecr-token-refresh","sec":"aws-s080","q":"In a Python script that refreshes an ECR secret, what does this code do, and why is <code>split(':')[1]</code> needed?","a":"<p><code>get_authorization_token</code> returns a base64 string containing <code>AWS:&lt;password&gt;</code>. The code decodes it and splits on the colon to keep only the password, which is then used with the username <code>AWS</code> for a docker-registry secret. The response also includes <code>proxyEndpoint</code>, the registry address.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ecr_client = boto3.client(&#x27;ecr&#x27;, region_name=&#x27;&lt;region&gt;&#x27;)\ntoken = ecr_client.get_authorization_token()[&#x27;authorizationData&#x27;][0]\npassword = base64.b64decode(token[&#x27;authorizationToken&#x27;]).decode(&#x27;utf-8&#x27;).split(&#x27;:&#x27;)[1]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0598","card":"q-aws-079-automate-ecr-token-refresh","sec":"aws-s080","q":"In a script that updates a Kubernetes secret, why pipe <code>kubectl create secret ... --dry-run=client -o yaml</code> into <code>kubectl apply -f -</code>?","a":"<p><code>create</code> fails if the secret already exists. With <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> it only prints the manifest, and <code>kubectl apply</code> then creates the secret or updates it in place. The script becomes safe to run again every time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0599","card":"q-aws-079-automate-ecr-token-refresh","sec":"aws-s080","q":"How would you run the ECR token refresh on a schedule inside the cluster?","a":"<p>Use a Kubernetes <code>CronJob</code> that runs every few hours (less than the 12-hour validity), with a service account that has an IAM role for <code>ecr:GetAuthorizationToken</code> through IRSA, and RBAC permission to create or patch secrets in the target namespace. Missing RBAC or IAM permission is the common failure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0600","card":"q-aws-079-automate-ecr-token-refresh","sec":"aws-s080","q":"What is the risk of building a shell command with an f-string and <code>subprocess.run(cmd, shell=True)</code> in a refresh script?","a":"<p>Values such as the password or namespace are inserted into a shell string, so special characters or untrusted input can change the command (shell injection), and the token can show up in process listings and logs. Pass an argument list without <code>shell=True</code>, or use the Kubernetes Python client to write the secret.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0601","card":"q-aws-079-automate-ecr-token-refresh","sec":"aws-s080","q":"Instead of a refresh script, what is a cleaner way to avoid expiring ECR tokens on EKS?","a":"<p>Rely on the node IAM role (<code>AmazonEC2ContainerRegistryReadOnly</code>) so the kubelet fetches fresh tokens itself, with no secret to rotate. For other clusters, use a kubelet image credential provider.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0602","card":"q-aws-080-network-resource-and-ci-checks","sec":"aws-s081","q":"A pod in a private EKS cluster cannot pull images from ECR, but credentials are correct. Which network path must exist?","a":"<p>Nodes need a route to ECR. Either a NAT gateway for outbound internet access, or VPC endpoints: interface endpoints <code>ecr.api</code> and <code>ecr.dkr</code>, plus an S3 gateway endpoint, because image layers are stored in S3. Security groups must allow outbound HTTPS (443), and an endpoint's security group must allow inbound 443 from the nodes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0603","card":"q-aws-080-network-resource-and-ci-checks","sec":"aws-s081","q":"Why does <code>kubectl run -it test-pod --image=alpine -- sh</code> help when debugging ECR pull errors?","a":"<p>It starts a temporary pod inside the cluster network, so you test from the same place as the workload: DNS resolution and reachability of the registry. Testing from your laptop shows a different network path and can give a misleading answer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0604","card":"q-aws-080-network-resource-and-ci-checks","sec":"aws-s081","q":"In Kubernetes, what do <code>requests</code> and <code>limits</code> mean, and what happens when a container goes over its memory limit versus its CPU limit?","a":"<p><code>requests</code> are what the scheduler reserves for placement. <code>limits</code> are the cap at run time. Exceeding the memory limit gets the container killed (<code>OOMKilled</code>, exit code 137). Exceeding the CPU limit only throttles it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0605","card":"q-aws-080-network-resource-and-ci-checks","sec":"aws-s081","q":"A pod stays <code>Pending</code> on EKS and the events say <code>Insufficient cpu</code>. What do you check and what can you do?","a":"<p>Run <code>kubectl describe node</code> and <code>kubectl top nodes</code> to compare allocatable resources with requests already scheduled. Lower the pod's requests if they are inflated, add nodes (or let the Cluster Autoscaler or Karpenter add them), or use bigger instance types.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0606","card":"q-aws-080-network-resource-and-ci-checks","sec":"aws-s081","q":"Why add a CI step that checks <code>aws ecr describe-images</code> for the image tag before deploying to Kubernetes?","a":"<p>It fails the pipeline early when the image or tag does not exist, instead of deploying a pod that then sits in <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>. It moves a runtime failure to a build-time failure, where it is cheaper to see and fix.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0607","card":"q-aws-080-network-resource-and-ci-checks","sec":"aws-s081","q":"Why prefer GitHub Actions OIDC with <code>role-to-assume</code> over storing <code>AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID</code> and <code>AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY</code> as repository secrets?","a":"<p>With OIDC, the workflow gets short-lived credentials by assuming an IAM role, and the trust policy can limit it to one repository and branch. Static keys do not expire, can leak, and must be rotated by hand.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0608","card":"q-aws-081-eks-clusters-with-terraform","sec":"aws-s082","q":"How do you deploy a Kubernetes pod on EKS with Terraform and verify it?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>module &quot;eks&quot; { source = &quot;terraform-aws-modules/eks/aws&quot;, cluster_name = &quot;my-eks&quot; }\nresource &quot;kubernetes_pod&quot; &quot;nginx&quot; {\n  metadata: { name: &quot;nginx-pod&quot; }\n  spec: { container: [{ image: &quot;nginx:latest&quot;, name: &quot;nginx&quot; }] }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform apply\naws eks update-kubeconfig --name my-eks\nkubectl get pod nginx-pod</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0609","card":"q-aws-081-eks-clusters-with-terraform","sec":"aws-s082","q":"Terraform for EKS and a VPC, deployed through GitHub Actions?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>module &quot;vpc&quot; { source = &quot;terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws&quot; }\nmodule &quot;eks&quot; { source = &quot;terraform-aws-modules/eks/aws&quot;, subnet_ids = module.vpc.private_subnets, vpc_id = module.vpc.vpc_id }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Deploy VPC and EKS\non: push\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: hashicorp/setup-terraform@v2\n    - run: terraform init &amp;&amp; terraform apply -auto-approve\n      env: { AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID: ${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}, AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: ${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }} }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0610","card":"q-aws-081-eks-clusters-with-terraform","sec":"aws-s082","q":"GitHub Actions for Terraform EKS, then scale with <code>kubectl</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Deploy EKS\non: push\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: hashicorp/setup-terraform@v2\n    - run: terraform init &amp;&amp; terraform apply -auto-approve\n    - uses: azure/setup-kubectl@v3\n    - run: |\n        aws eks update-kubeconfig --name my-eks\n        kubectl scale deployment web-app --replicas=3\n      env: { AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID: ${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}, AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: ${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }} }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0611","card":"q-aws-081-eks-clusters-with-terraform","sec":"aws-s082","q":"Terraform for EKS, GitHub Actions to test pod scaling?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>module &quot;eks&quot; { source = &quot;terraform-aws-modules/eks/aws&quot;, cluster_name = &quot;test-eks&quot; }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Test Scaling\non: push\njobs:\n  scale:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - run: |\n        aws eks update-kubeconfig --name test-eks\n        kubectl scale deployment test-app --replicas=5\n      env: { AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID: ${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}, AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: ${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }} }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0612","card":"q-aws-081-eks-clusters-with-terraform","sec":"aws-s082","q":"Why use the community <code>terraform-aws-modules/eks/aws</code> module instead of writing raw <code>aws_eks_cluster</code> resources?","a":"<p>The module wraps the cluster, IAM roles, security groups, node groups, OIDC provider and add-ons in tested defaults, so you write far less code and follow common practice. The cost is less visibility into what it creates, and module major versions can introduce breaking changes, so pin the version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0613","card":"q-aws-081-eks-clusters-with-terraform","sec":"aws-s082","q":"What does <code>aws eks update-kubeconfig --name &lt;cluster&gt;</code> do, and why is it needed after <code>terraform apply</code>?","a":"<p>It writes a cluster entry and a user entry into your <code>~/.kube/config</code>. The user entry runs <code>aws eks get-token</code>, so <code>kubectl</code> authenticates to the cluster with your IAM identity. Terraform creates the cluster, but your <code>kubectl</code> does not know about it until the kubeconfig has been updated.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0614","card":"q-aws-081-eks-clusters-with-terraform","sec":"aws-s082","q":"What problem occurs when the Terraform <code>kubernetes</code> provider is configured from an EKS cluster created in the same Terraform run?","a":"<p>The provider needs the cluster endpoint and credentials, which do not exist until the cluster is created. This can give errors on the first plan or apply, and trouble when the cluster is replaced. Common fixes are to split cluster creation and in-cluster resources into separate Terraform configurations or to apply the cluster first with <code>-target</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0615","card":"q-aws-081-eks-clusters-with-terraform","sec":"aws-s082","q":"What are the risks of running <code>terraform apply -auto-approve</code> on every push in a CI workflow?","a":"<p>Nobody reviews the plan, so an unintended change (including destroying resources) is applied directly. Safer: run <code>terraform plan</code> on pull requests, show the plan for review, apply only on the main branch with an approval step, and use remote state with locking so runs do not collide.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0616","card":"q-aws-081-eks-clusters-with-terraform","sec":"aws-s082","q":"Where should Terraform state live when several people or CI runs apply the same EKS configuration?","a":"<p>In a remote backend with locking, such as an S3 bucket with versioning and encryption. State locking was done with a DynamoDB table, and an S3 lock file (<code>use_lockfile</code>) was introduced as experimental in Terraform 1.10 and became stable in 1.11, so a DynamoDB table is no longer needed. Local state files lead to conflicts and lost state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0617","card":"q-aws-082-eks-with-ansible-and-monitoring-pods","sec":"aws-s083","q":"Terraform for EKS, Ansible for a monitoring pod?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>module &quot;eks&quot; { source = &quot;terraform-aws-modules/eks/aws&quot;, cluster_name = &quot;monitoring-eks&quot; }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Deploy monitor\n  hosts: localhost\n  tasks:\n  - name: Apply\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl apply -f monitor.yaml</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata: { name: &quot;monitor&quot; }\nspec: { containers: [{ name: &quot;prometheus&quot;, image: &quot;prom/prometheus&quot; }] }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0618","card":"q-aws-082-eks-with-ansible-and-monitoring-pods","sec":"aws-s083","q":"Terraform for EKS, Ansible for the Kubernetes deploy, GitHub Actions to verify?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>module &quot;eks&quot; { source = &quot;terraform-aws-modules/eks/aws&quot;, cluster_name = &quot;my-eks&quot; }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Deploy K8s\n  hosts: localhost\n  tasks:\n  - name: Apply\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Verify\non: push\njobs:\n  verify:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - run: kubectl get deployment\n      env: { KUBECONFIG: ${{ secrets.KUBECONFIG }} }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0619","card":"q-aws-082-eks-with-ansible-and-monitoring-pods","sec":"aws-s083","q":"Terraform for ECS, GitHub Actions for a Kubernetes alternative?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>module &quot;ecs&quot; { source = &quot;terraform-aws-modules/ecs/aws&quot;, cluster_name = &quot;my-ecs&quot; }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Deploy K8s\non: push\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - run: kubectl apply -f k8s_deployment.yaml\n      env: { KUBECONFIG: ${{ secrets.KUBECONFIG }} }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0620","card":"q-aws-082-eks-with-ansible-and-monitoring-pods","sec":"aws-s083","q":"When Terraform builds an EKS cluster and Ansible deploys into it, what does each tool own, and why split it this way?","a":"<p>Terraform owns the AWS infrastructure (VPC, cluster, node groups, IAM) with state and a plan. Ansible runs procedural tasks after that, such as applying manifests or configuring hosts. The split avoids two tools managing the same resource, though for pure Kubernetes manifests many teams use <code>kubectl</code>, Helm or GitOps instead of Ansible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0621","card":"q-aws-082-eks-with-ansible-and-monitoring-pods","sec":"aws-s083","q":"In Ansible, why is <code>ansible.builtin.command: kubectl apply -f monitor.yaml</code> weaker than the <code>kubernetes.core.k8s</code> module?","a":"<p><code>command</code> runs a shell command and always reports \"changed\", so Ansible cannot tell if anything changed, and error handling is manual. The <code>kubernetes.core.k8s</code> module talks to the API directly, is idempotent, supports <code>state: present</code> and <code>absent</code>, and reports real changes. It needs the <code>kubernetes</code> Python library on the control host.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0622","card":"q-aws-082-eks-with-ansible-and-monitoring-pods","sec":"aws-s083","q":"Why run a Prometheus monitoring workload as a Deployment (or a Helm chart) rather than a bare Pod?","a":"<p>A bare pod has no controller. If the node fails or the pod is evicted, nothing recreates it. A Deployment or StatefulSet restarts and reschedules it, and supports rolling updates. Prometheus also needs persistent storage and a service account to scrape the cluster, which the <code>kube-prometheus-stack</code> chart sets up.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0623","card":"q-aws-082-eks-with-ansible-and-monitoring-pods","sec":"aws-s083","q":"What are the options for monitoring an EKS cluster on AWS, besides running your own Prometheus?","a":"<p>CloudWatch Container Insights (metrics and logs through the CloudWatch agent or the Amazon CloudWatch Observability add-on), Amazon Managed Service for Prometheus with Amazon Managed Grafana, or a third party tool. Managed options reduce the operations work, at a cost per metric.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0624","card":"q-aws-083-ansible-on-ec2-with-github-actions","sec":"aws-s084","q":"Ansible to install Docker on EC2, GitHub Actions to deploy a Kubernetes manifest?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Install Docker\n  hosts: ec2_hosts\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Install\n    ansible.builtin.package: { name: docker.io, state: present }\n  - name: Start\n    ansible.builtin.service: { name: docker, state: started }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Deploy K8s\non: push\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - run: ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini install_docker.yml\n    - uses: azure/setup-kubectl@v3\n    - run: kubectl apply -f pod.yaml</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata: { name: &quot;test-pod&quot; }\nspec: { containers: [{ name: &quot;test&quot;, image: &quot;nginx&quot; }] }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0625","card":"q-aws-083-ansible-on-ec2-with-github-actions","sec":"aws-s084","q":"Ansible to configure EC2, Terraform for a Kubernetes service?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Configure EC2\n  hosts: ec2_hosts\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Install\n    ansible.builtin.package: { name: curl, state: present }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;kubernetes_service&quot; &quot;web&quot; {\n  metadata: { name: &quot;web-service&quot; }\n  spec:\n    selector: { app: &quot;web&quot; }\n    port: [{ port: 80, target_port: &quot;8080&quot; }]\n    type: &quot;LoadBalancer&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0626","card":"q-aws-083-ansible-on-ec2-with-github-actions","sec":"aws-s084","q":"Ansible to update EC2, GitHub Actions to test Kubernetes?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Update EC2\n  hosts: ec2_hosts\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Update\n    ansible.builtin.yum: { name: &quot;*&quot;, state: latest }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Test K8s\non: push\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - run: kubectl get nodes\n      env: { KUBECONFIG: ${{ secrets.KUBECONFIG }} }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0627","card":"q-aws-083-ansible-on-ec2-with-github-actions","sec":"aws-s084","q":"Why is Ansible called agentless, and what does a GitHub Actions runner need to run a playbook against EC2 hosts?","a":"<p>Ansible connects over SSH (or WinRM) and runs modules remotely, so nothing is installed on the managed hosts except Python. The runner needs network access to the instances on port 22 (public IP, VPN or a bastion), an SSH key from secrets, and an inventory. Without network access the playbook cannot connect.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0628","card":"q-aws-083-ansible-on-ec2-with-github-actions","sec":"aws-s084","q":"How do you keep an Ansible inventory for EC2 current when instances come and go in an Auto Scaling group?","a":"<p>Use the dynamic inventory plugin <code>amazon.aws.aws_ec2</code>, which queries the EC2 API and groups hosts by tags, region or other attributes. A static <code>inventory.ini</code> goes stale when IPs change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0629","card":"q-aws-083-ansible-on-ec2-with-github-actions","sec":"aws-s084","q":"What does <code>become: yes</code> do in an Ansible play, and when is it needed?","a":"<p>It runs the tasks with privilege escalation (sudo by default). It is needed for actions that require root, such as installing packages or starting services. Use it per play or per task, so that only the steps that need it run as root.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0630","card":"q-aws-083-ansible-on-ec2-with-github-actions","sec":"aws-s084","q":"Why prefer <code>ansible.builtin.package</code> with <code>state: present</code> over <code>command: apt install</code> or <code>yum install</code>?","a":"<p><code>package</code> uses the host's package manager through a module, so it is idempotent (it does nothing if the package is already installed) and portable across distributions. Note that package names can still differ between distributions, such as <code>docker.io</code> on Debian and Ubuntu.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0631","card":"q-aws-083-ansible-on-ec2-with-github-actions","sec":"aws-s084","q":"Why is <code>ansible.builtin.yum</code> with <code>name: \"*\"</code> and <code>state: latest</code> risky to run from a pipeline on every push?","a":"<p>It upgrades every package, which can change versions unexpectedly, restart services and break the application. Updates are better done on a controlled schedule, or by baking a patched AMI and replacing instances.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0632","card":"q-aws-083-ansible-on-ec2-with-github-actions","sec":"aws-s084","q":"Besides SSH, how can Ansible reach EC2 instances that have no inbound access?","a":"<p>Through AWS Systems Manager Session Manager using the <code>community.aws.aws_ssm</code> connection plugin (it needs an S3 bucket for file transfer). It requires the SSM agent and an instance profile, and needs no open port 22 or key pairs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0633","card":"q-aws-084-ingress-security-group-and-cronjob-deplo","sec":"aws-s085","q":"GitHub Actions for Ansible on EC2, then deploy a Kubernetes Ingress?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Setup EC2\n  hosts: ec2_hosts\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Install\n    ansible.builtin.package: { name: nginx, state: present }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Deploy Ingress\non: push\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - run: ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini setup_ec2.yml\n    - run: kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml\n      env: { KUBECONFIG: ${{ secrets.KUBECONFIG }} }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Ingress\nmetadata: { name: &quot;web-ingress&quot; }\nspec: { rules: [{ http: { paths: [{ path: &quot;/&quot;, pathType: &quot;Prefix&quot;, backend: { service: { name: &quot;web&quot;, port: { number: 80 } } } } ] } } ] }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0634","card":"q-aws-084-ingress-security-group-and-cronjob-deplo","sec":"aws-s085","q":"Ansible to update an EC2 security group, GitHub Actions to deploy a Kubernetes service?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Update SG\n  hosts: localhost\n  tasks:\n  - name: Add rule\n    amazon.aws.ec2_security_group: { name: &quot;my-sg&quot;, rules: [{ proto: &quot;tcp&quot;, from_port: 80, to_port: 80, cidr_ip: &quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot; }] }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Deploy Service\non: push\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - run: ansible-playbook update_sg.yml\n    - run: kubectl apply -f service.yaml\n      env: { KUBECONFIG: ${{ secrets.KUBECONFIG }} }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0635","card":"q-aws-084-ingress-security-group-and-cronjob-deplo","sec":"aws-s085","q":"Ansible for EC2, GitHub Actions to deploy a Kubernetes CronJob?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Deploy CronJob\non: push\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - run: kubectl apply -f cronjob.yaml\n      env: { KUBECONFIG: ${{ secrets.KUBECONFIG }} }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: batch/v1\nkind: CronJob\nmetadata: { name: &quot;test-cron&quot; }\nspec:\n  schedule: &quot;*/5 * * * *&quot;\n  jobTemplate: { spec: { template: { spec: { containers: [{ name: &quot;test&quot;, image: &quot;busybox&quot;, command: [&quot;echo&quot;, &quot;Hello&quot;] }] } } } }</code></pre></div>\n<p>The Ansible step is the same <code>curl</code> install play as above.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0636","card":"q-aws-084-ingress-security-group-and-cronjob-deplo","sec":"aws-s085","q":"Why does applying a Kubernetes <code>Ingress</code> do nothing in a cluster that has no ingress controller?","a":"<p>An Ingress is only a set of routing rules stored in the API. A controller (such as the AWS Load Balancer Controller or NGINX) reads them and configures a load balancer or proxy. With no controller nothing acts on the object.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0637","card":"q-aws-084-ingress-security-group-and-cronjob-deplo","sec":"aws-s085","q":"In the Ansible <code>amazon.aws.ec2_security_group</code> module, what happens to existing rules that are not listed in <code>rules</code>?","a":"<p>By default <code>purge_rules</code> is true, so the module removes rules that are not in your list. Defining only the new rule can delete other rules, which can lock you out of SSH. Set <code>purge_rules: false</code> or list all rules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0638","card":"q-aws-084-ingress-security-group-and-cronjob-deplo","sec":"aws-s085","q":"In Kubernetes, which fields of a CronJob control overlapping runs and missed runs?","a":"<p><code>concurrencyPolicy</code>: <code>Allow</code> (default), <code>Forbid</code> (skip the new run) or <code>Replace</code> (cancel the running job). <code>startingDeadlineSeconds</code> is how late a missed run may still start. <code>successfulJobsHistoryLimit</code> and <code>failedJobsHistoryLimit</code> control how many finished Jobs are kept.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0639","card":"q-aws-084-ingress-security-group-and-cronjob-deplo","sec":"aws-s085","q":"What time zone does a Kubernetes CronJob schedule use, and how do you change it? (Kubernetes 1.27 and later)","a":"<p>By default the schedule runs in the time zone of the controller manager, usually UTC. From Kubernetes 1.27 the <code>spec.timeZone</code> field is stable, for example <code>timeZone: \"Europe/London\"</code>. Before that, you had to calculate the offset yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0640","card":"q-aws-084-ingress-security-group-and-cronjob-deplo","sec":"aws-s085","q":"What does the schedule <code>*/5 * * * *</code> mean in a Kubernetes CronJob?","a":"<p>It runs every 5 minutes. The five fields are minute, hour, day of month, month and day of week. <code>*/5</code> in the minute field means every 5th minute.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0641","card":"q-aws-084-ingress-security-group-and-cronjob-deplo","sec":"aws-s085","q":"In a CronJob's <code>jobTemplate</code>, why must the pod's <code>restartPolicy</code> be <code>OnFailure</code> or <code>Never</code>?","a":"<p>Jobs run to completion, and the default <code>Always</code> policy would restart the container forever. Kubernetes rejects <code>Always</code> for Job pods. <code>OnFailure</code> restarts the container in the same pod and <code>Never</code> creates a new pod for each retry.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0642","card":"q-aws-085-building-kubernetes-on-ec2","sec":"aws-s086","q":"Ansible to configure EC2 as a Kubernetes node, GitHub Actions to verify?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Configure K8s\n  hosts: ec2_hosts\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Install\n    ansible.builtin.package: { name: kubelet, state: present }</code></pre></div>\n<p>Verification uses the same <code>kubectl get nodes</code> workflow as above, with <code>KUBECONFIG</code> from secrets.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0643","card":"q-aws-085-building-kubernetes-on-ec2","sec":"aws-s086","q":"Terraform for ASG, Ansible for a Kubernetes agent?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>module &quot;asg&quot; {\n  source = &quot;terraform-aws-modules/autoscaling/aws&quot;\n  name = &quot;my-asg&quot;\n  min_size = 1\n  max_size = 3\n  vpc_zone_identifier = [&quot;subnet-1&quot;]\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Install K8s agent\n  hosts: asg_hosts\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Install\n    ansible.builtin.package: { name: kubeadm, state: present }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0644","card":"q-aws-085-building-kubernetes-on-ec2","sec":"aws-s086","q":"Terraform for EC2, Ansible to join the node to a Kubernetes cluster?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_instance&quot; &quot;k8s_node&quot; { ami = &quot;ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0&quot;, instance_type = &quot;t2.medium&quot; }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Join K8s\n  hosts: ec2_hosts\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Install\n    ansible.builtin.package: { name: kubeadm, state: present }\n  - name: Join\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubeadm join &lt;master-ip&gt;:6443 --token &lt;token&gt; --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash &lt;hash&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0645","card":"q-aws-085-building-kubernetes-on-ec2","sec":"aws-s086","q":"Ansible to build a Kubernetes cluster on EC2, GitHub Actions to verify?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Install K8s\n  hosts: ec2_hosts\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Install\n    ansible.builtin.package: { name: &quot;{{ item }}&quot;, state: present }\n    loop: [kubeadm, kubelet, kubectl]\n  - name: Init master\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16\n    when: inventory_hostname == &quot;master&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0646","card":"q-aws-085-building-kubernetes-on-ec2","sec":"aws-s086","q":"Ansible to install <code>kubectl</code> on EC2, GitHub Actions to deploy a pod?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Install kubectl\n  hosts: ec2_hosts\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Install\n    ansible.builtin.shell: |\n      curl -LO &quot;https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl&quot;\n      chmod +x kubectl &amp;&amp; mv kubectl /usr/local/bin/</code></pre></div>\n<p>The deploy step is <code>kubectl apply -f pod.yaml</code> with <code>KUBECONFIG</code> from secrets, as in the other workflows.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0647","card":"q-aws-085-building-kubernetes-on-ec2","sec":"aws-s086","q":"What do <code>kubeadm init</code> and <code>kubeadm join</code> each do when building a cluster on EC2?","a":"<p><code>kubeadm init</code> bootstraps the control plane on one node: it creates certificates, static pods for the API server, etcd, scheduler and controller manager, and prints a join command. <code>kubeadm join</code> runs on a worker with the token and CA certificate hash, so the node can securely connect and register with the API server.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0648","card":"q-aws-085-building-kubernetes-on-ec2","sec":"aws-s086","q":"A <code>kubeadm join</code> fails because the token is invalid. How do you get a new one?","a":"<p>Join tokens expire after 24 hours by default. On the control plane node run <code>kubeadm token create --print-join-command</code> to get a fresh token and the full command, including the discovery CA cert hash.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0649","card":"q-aws-085-building-kubernetes-on-ec2","sec":"aws-s086","q":"What must be ready on an EC2 instance before it can run <code>kubeadm</code>?","a":"<p>A container runtime (such as containerd) and its cgroup driver set to match the kubelet, <code>kubelet</code>, <code>kubeadm</code> and <code>kubectl</code> installed, swap disabled, required kernel modules and sysctl settings for bridged traffic, and open ports (6443 for the API server, 10250 for the kubelet, and so on).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0650","card":"q-aws-085-building-kubernetes-on-ec2","sec":"aws-s086","q":"In <code>kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16</code>, what does the flag do and why must it match the CNI plugin?","a":"<p>It sets the range from which pod IPs are assigned to nodes. The CNI plugin must be configured with the same range. Flannel's default manifest assumes <code>10.244.0.0/16</code>, so a different value breaks pod networking.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0651","card":"q-aws-085-building-kubernetes-on-ec2","sec":"aws-s086","q":"Why must you install a CNI plugin after <code>kubeadm init</code>, and what do you see if you do not?","a":"<p>Kubeadm does not install pod networking. Without a CNI plugin the nodes stay <code>NotReady</code> and CoreDNS pods stay <code>Pending</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0652","card":"q-aws-085-building-kubernetes-on-ec2","sec":"aws-s086","q":"When would you build Kubernetes on EC2 with kubeadm instead of using EKS?","a":"<p>Only when you need full control (custom control plane flags, unsupported versions, or an air-gapped or learning environment). EKS runs and patches the control plane across AZs for you, and costs a flat hourly fee per cluster. Self-managed adds work: upgrades, etcd backups and HA control plane.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0653","card":"q-aws-085-building-kubernetes-on-ec2","sec":"aws-s086","q":"In an Ansible play that runs <code>kubeadm init</code> with <code>ansible.builtin.command</code>, how do you make it safe to run twice?","a":"<p>The <code>command</code> module is not idempotent, so a second <code>kubeadm init</code> fails or does damage. Add <code>args: creates: /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf</code>, which skips the task when that file already exists, or check state with a <code>when</code> condition.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0654","card":"q-aws-085-building-kubernetes-on-ec2","sec":"aws-s086","q":"Why should the <code>kubectl</code> version installed on a host be chosen carefully, and what is wrong with downloading <code>stable.txt</code> in a playbook?","a":"<p><code>kubectl</code> is supported within one minor version of the API server. Downloading <code>stable.txt</code> fetches whatever is newest on the day the playbook runs, so results change over time and it can be outside that skew. Pin a version that matches the cluster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0655","card":"q-aws-086-s3-cloudfront-and-lambda-with-github-act","sec":"aws-s087","q":"Terraform for an S3 bucket, GitHub Actions to upload files?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_s3_bucket&quot; &quot;my_bucket&quot; { bucket = &quot;my-unique-bucket-123&quot; }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Upload S3\non: push\njobs:\n  upload:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v2\n      with: { aws-access-key-id: ${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}, aws-secret-access-key: ${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }} }\n    - run: aws s3 cp file.txt s3://my-unique-bucket-123/</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0656","card":"q-aws-086-s3-cloudfront-and-lambda-with-github-act","sec":"aws-s087","q":"Terraform for CloudFront, GitHub Actions to sync assets?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_cloudfront_distribution&quot; &quot;cdn&quot; {\n  origin: { domain_name: &quot;my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com&quot;, origin_id: &quot;S3-my-bucket&quot; }\n  enabled: true\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Sync CloudFront\non: push\njobs:\n  sync:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v2\n      with: { aws-access-key-id: ${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}, aws-secret-access-key: ${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }} }\n    - run: aws s3 sync ./assets s3://my-bucket/</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0657","card":"q-aws-086-s3-cloudfront-and-lambda-with-github-act","sec":"aws-s087","q":"GitHub Actions to deploy AWS Lambda, and a Kubernetes pod to monitor it?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Deploy Lambda\non: push\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v2\n      with: { aws-access-key-id: ${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}, aws-secret-access-key: ${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }} }\n    - run: zip -r lambda.zip . &amp;&amp; aws lambda update-function-code --function-name my-lambda --zip-file fileb://lambda.zip</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata: { name: &quot;lambda-monitor&quot; }\nspec: { containers: [{ name: &quot;monitor&quot;, image: &quot;amazon/aws-cli&quot;, command: [&quot;aws&quot;, &quot;lambda&quot;, &quot;get-function&quot;, &quot;--function-name&quot;, &quot;my-lambda&quot;] }] }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0658","card":"q-aws-086-s3-cloudfront-and-lambda-with-github-act","sec":"aws-s087","q":"GitHub Actions to deploy Lambda, a Kubernetes pod to log invocations?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: aws lambda update-function-code --function-name my-lambda --zip-file fileb://lambda.zip\n- run: kubectl apply -f logger.yaml\n  env: { KUBECONFIG: ${{ secrets.KUBECONFIG }} }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata: { name: &quot;lambda-logger&quot; }\nspec: { containers: [{ name: &quot;logger&quot;, image: &quot;amazon/aws-cli&quot;, command: [&quot;aws&quot;, &quot;logs&quot;, &quot;filter-log-events&quot;, &quot;--log-group-name&quot;, &quot;/aws/lambda/my-lambda&quot;] }] }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0659","card":"q-aws-086-s3-cloudfront-and-lambda-with-github-act","sec":"aws-s087","q":"Terraform for a Lambda timeout fix, GitHub Actions to trigger a Kubernetes job?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_lambda_function&quot; &quot;fix&quot; { function_name = &quot;my-lambda&quot;, timeout = 60, filename = &quot;lambda.zip&quot;, handler = &quot;index.handler&quot;, runtime = &quot;nodejs16.x&quot; }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: batch/v1\nkind: Job\nmetadata: { name: &quot;lambda-check&quot; }\nspec:\n  template:\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: check\n        image: amazon/aws-cli\n        command: [&quot;aws&quot;, &quot;lambda&quot;, &quot;get-function&quot;, &quot;--function-name&quot;, &quot;my-lambda&quot;]</code></pre></div>\n<p>The trigger workflow runs <code>kubectl apply -f job.yaml</code> with <code>KUBECONFIG</code> from secrets.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0660","card":"q-aws-086-s3-cloudfront-and-lambda-with-github-act","sec":"aws-s087","q":"What is the difference between <code>aws s3 cp</code> and <code>aws s3 sync</code> in a deployment pipeline, and what does <code>--delete</code> add?","a":"<p><code>cp</code> copies the files you name. <code>sync</code> compares source and destination and copies only new or changed files. <code>sync --delete</code> also removes files in the destination that no longer exist in the source, so the bucket mirrors the build output. Use <code>--delete</code> carefully because it deletes objects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0661","card":"q-aws-086-s3-cloudfront-and-lambda-with-github-act","sec":"aws-s087","q":"After syncing a static site to S3 behind CloudFront, users still see old files. Why, and what do you do?","a":"<p>CloudFront caches objects at edge locations until the TTL expires. Create an invalidation (<code>aws cloudfront create-invalidation --paths \"/*\"</code>) after the deployment, or give assets versioned file names (content hashes) so new files get new URLs and need no invalidation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0662","card":"q-aws-086-s3-cloudfront-and-lambda-with-github-act","sec":"aws-s087","q":"When CloudFront serves a private S3 bucket, what is Origin Access Control (OAC) and how does it compare with Origin Access Identity (OAI)?","a":"<p>OAC lets CloudFront sign its requests to S3 so the bucket can stay private and only the distribution can read it, with a bucket policy that allows the CloudFront service principal for that distribution. It is the current recommendation and supports SSE-KMS and all regions. OAI is the older mechanism with fewer features.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0663","card":"q-aws-086-s3-cloudfront-and-lambda-with-github-act","sec":"aws-s087","q":"In <code>aws lambda update-function-code --zip-file fileb://lambda.zip</code>, why is the prefix <code>fileb://</code> and not <code>file://</code>?","a":"<p><code>fileb://</code> tells the AWS CLI to read the file as raw binary, which a zip archive needs. <code>file://</code> reads it as text, which corrupts binary content.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0664","card":"q-aws-086-s3-cloudfront-and-lambda-with-github-act","sec":"aws-s087","q":"Why should a pipeline that updates Lambda code publish a version and update an alias?","a":"<p><code>update-function-code</code> changes <code>$LATEST</code> immediately, so a bad build is live at once. Publishing a version (<code>aws lambda publish-version</code>) creates an immutable snapshot, and an alias such as <code>prod</code> can point to it, with weighted routing for gradual rollout and an instant rollback by moving the alias back.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0665","card":"q-aws-086-s3-cloudfront-and-lambda-with-github-act","sec":"aws-s087","q":"After <code>aws lambda update-function-code</code>, why might a following command fail with <code>ResourceConflictException</code>?","a":"<p>The function is still in an <code>InProgress</code> update state. Wait with <code>aws lambda wait function-updated --function-name &lt;name&gt;</code> before running the next update, such as <code>update-function-configuration</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0666","card":"q-aws-086-s3-cloudfront-and-lambda-with-github-act","sec":"aws-s087","q":"How do you monitor a Lambda function in production?","a":"<p>Lambda reports to CloudWatch on its own: metrics (invocations, errors, duration, throttles, concurrent executions), logs in <code>/aws/lambda/&lt;name&gt;</code>, and alarms on those metrics. Add X-Ray tracing to follow requests across services. A custom poller adds cost and gives less information.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0667","card":"q-aws-087-rds-alb-and-vpc-with-github-actions","sec":"aws-s088","q":"GitHub Actions for Terraform RDS, connect a Kubernetes app?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_db_instance&quot; &quot;db&quot; { identifier = &quot;my-rds&quot;, engine = &quot;mysql&quot;, instance_class = &quot;db.t3.micro&quot; }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Deploy RDS\non: push\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: hashicorp/setup-terraform@v2\n    - run: terraform init &amp;&amp; terraform apply -auto-approve\n    - run: kubectl apply -f app.yaml\n      env: { KUBECONFIG: ${{ secrets.KUBECONFIG }} }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata: { name: &quot;app&quot; }\nspec: { containers: [{ name: &quot;app&quot;, image: &quot;mysql:5.7&quot;, env: [{ name: &quot;MYSQL_HOST&quot;, value: &quot;my-rds.endpoint&quot; }] }] }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0668","card":"q-aws-087-rds-alb-and-vpc-with-github-actions","sec":"aws-s088","q":"GitHub Actions for a Terraform ALB, Kubernetes Ingress?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_lb&quot; &quot;app_lb&quot; { name = &quot;app-lb&quot;, load_balancer_type = &quot;application&quot;, subnets = [&quot;subnet-1&quot;, &quot;subnet-2&quot;] }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Deploy ALB\non: push\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: hashicorp/setup-terraform@v2\n    - run: terraform init &amp;&amp; terraform apply -auto-approve\n    - run: kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml\n      env: { KUBECONFIG: ${{ secrets.KUBECONFIG }} }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Ingress\nmetadata: { name: &quot;app-ingress&quot; }\nspec: { rules: [{ http: { paths: [{ path: &quot;/&quot;, pathType: &quot;Prefix&quot;, backend: { service: { name: &quot;app&quot;, port: { number: 80 } } } } ] } } ] }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0669","card":"q-aws-087-rds-alb-and-vpc-with-github-actions","sec":"aws-s088","q":"Terraform for an ALB, expose a Kubernetes service with <code>kubectl</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_lb&quot; &quot;my_alb&quot; { name = &quot;my-alb&quot;, load_balancer_type = &quot;application&quot;, subnets = [&quot;subnet-1&quot;, &quot;subnet-2&quot;] }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl expose service web-service --type=LoadBalancer --name=web-lb --port=80\nkubectl annotate service web-lb &quot;service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-arn=arn:aws:elasticloadbalancing:us-east-1:123:loadbalancer/app/my-alb/abc123&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0670","card":"q-aws-087-rds-alb-and-vpc-with-github-actions","sec":"aws-s088","q":"Terraform for a VPC, Ansible for Kubernetes networking?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>module &quot;vpc&quot; { source = &quot;terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws&quot;, vpc_name = &quot;k8s-vpc&quot; }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Setup networking\n  hosts: localhost\n  tasks:\n  - name: Apply\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/coreos/flannel/master/Documentation/kube-flannel.yml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0671","card":"q-aws-087-rds-alb-and-vpc-with-github-actions","sec":"aws-s088","q":"How should an application pod on EKS get the RDS endpoint and password, and what is wrong with putting them in plain <code>env</code> values in a manifest?","a":"<p>Plain <code>env</code> values sit in the manifest in source control. Keep the host in a ConfigMap and the password in a Kubernetes Secret, or better in AWS Secrets Manager read through the External Secrets Operator or the Secrets Store CSI driver. Use the RDS endpoint DNS name, not an IP, because the IP can change after a failover.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0672","card":"q-aws-087-rds-alb-and-vpc-with-github-actions","sec":"aws-s088","q":"Why does a pod in EKS fail to connect to an RDS instance even though both are in the same VPC?","a":"<p>Check the RDS security group: it must allow port 3306 (or the engine port) from the nodes' or pods' security group. Also check network ACLs, that the DB subnet group is reachable, DNS resolution of the endpoint and the credentials. By default only the security group rules decide reachability inside a VPC.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0673","card":"q-aws-087-rds-alb-and-vpc-with-github-actions","sec":"aws-s088","q":"Which tags do VPC subnets need so the AWS Load Balancer Controller can find them for an EKS cluster?","a":"<p><code>kubernetes.io/role/elb = 1</code> on public subnets (internet-facing load balancers) and <code>kubernetes.io/role/internal-elb = 1</code> on private subnets, plus the <code>kubernetes.io/cluster/&lt;name&gt;</code> tag in older setups. Without these tags the controller cannot choose subnets automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0674","card":"q-aws-087-rds-alb-and-vpc-with-github-actions","sec":"aws-s088","q":"What is the difference between <code>kubectl expose service ... --type=LoadBalancer</code> and an Ingress for AWS load balancing?","a":"<p><code>type=LoadBalancer</code> makes one layer 4 load balancer for that service. In older clusters the in-tree provider creates a Classic Load Balancer, while the AWS Load Balancer Controller creates an NLB. An Ingress describes layer 7 HTTP rules, one ALB can serve many services, and it needs the controller.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0675","card":"q-aws-087-rds-alb-and-vpc-with-github-actions","sec":"aws-s088","q":"Why is attaching an existing Terraform-created ALB to a Kubernetes Service by annotating the Service usually the wrong approach?","a":"<p>A Service's annotations do not adopt an existing load balancer. Two tools would manage the same ALB, which causes drift. Let the AWS Load Balancer Controller create the ALB from an Ingress. If the ALB must exist outside Kubernetes, register pods with a <code>TargetGroupBinding</code> pointing at a target group you created.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0676","card":"q-aws-087-rds-alb-and-vpc-with-github-actions","sec":"aws-s088","q":"Which CNI does EKS use by default, and why is flannel not what you install on EKS?","a":"<p>The Amazon VPC CNI plugin. It gives each pod a real VPC IP address from the node's subnets, so pods are directly routable in the VPC and work with security groups and ALB <code>ip</code> targets. Flannel is an overlay CNI used when you build your own cluster, and it does not provide VPC native pod IPs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0677","card":"q-aws-088-messaging-data-and-alerting-integrations","sec":"aws-s089","q":"Terraform for SQS, a Kubernetes pod to process it, deployed with Ansible?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_sqs_queue&quot; &quot;my_queue&quot; { name = &quot;my-queue&quot; }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata: { name: &quot;sqs-processor&quot; }\nspec: { containers: [{ name: &quot;processor&quot;, image: &quot;amazon/aws-cli&quot;, command: [&quot;aws&quot;, &quot;sqs&quot;, &quot;receive-message&quot;, &quot;--queue-url&quot;, &quot;&lt;queue-url&gt;&quot;] }] }</code></pre></div>\n<p>The Ansible play runs <code>kubectl apply -f processor.yaml</code> on localhost.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0678","card":"q-aws-088-messaging-data-and-alerting-integrations","sec":"aws-s089","q":"Terraform for DynamoDB, Ansible to populate it from a Kubernetes pod?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_dynamodb_table&quot; &quot;my_table&quot; { name = &quot;my-table&quot;, billing_mode = &quot;PAY_PER_REQUEST&quot;, hash_key = &quot;id&quot;, attribute: [{ name: &quot;id&quot;, type: &quot;S&quot; }] }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata: { name: &quot;dynamo-populator&quot; }\nspec: { containers: [{ name: &quot;populator&quot;, image: &quot;amazon/aws-cli&quot;, command: [&quot;aws&quot;, &quot;dynamodb&quot;, &quot;put-item&quot;, &quot;--table-name&quot;, &quot;my-table&quot;, &quot;--item&quot;, &quot;{\\&quot;id\\&quot;: {\\&quot;S\\&quot;: \\&quot;1\\&quot;}, \\&quot;data\\&quot;: {\\&quot;S\\&quot;: \\&quot;test\\&quot;}}&quot;] }] }</code></pre></div>\n<p>The Ansible play runs <code>kubectl apply -f populator.yaml</code> on localhost.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0679","card":"q-aws-088-messaging-data-and-alerting-integrations","sec":"aws-s089","q":"Terraform for S3 and a ConfigMap, Ansible to sync them?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_s3_bucket&quot; &quot;config&quot; { bucket = &quot;config-bucket-123&quot; }\nresource &quot;kubernetes_configmap&quot; &quot;app&quot; {\n  metadata: { name: &quot;app-config&quot; }\n  data: { key: &quot;value&quot; }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Sync ConfigMap\n  hosts: localhost\n  tasks:\n  - name: Export\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl get configmap app-config -o yaml &gt; config.yaml\n  - name: Upload\n    amazon.aws.aws_s3: { bucket: config-bucket-123, src: config.yaml, dest: config.yaml, mode: put }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0680","card":"q-aws-088-messaging-data-and-alerting-integrations","sec":"aws-s089","q":"Terraform for a CloudWatch alarm, Ansible to alert from a Kubernetes pod?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm&quot; &quot;cpu&quot; {\n  alarm_name = &quot;high-cpu&quot;\n  comparison_operator = &quot;GreaterThanThreshold&quot;\n  threshold = &quot;80&quot;\n  metric_name = &quot;CPUUtilization&quot;\n  namespace = &quot;AWS/EC2&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata: { name: &quot;alert-pod&quot; }\nspec: { containers: [{ name: &quot;alert&quot;, image: &quot;busybox&quot;, command: [&quot;sh&quot;, &quot;-c&quot;, &quot;echo Alarm &amp;&amp; sleep 3600&quot;] }] }</code></pre></div>\n<p>The Ansible play runs <code>kubectl apply -f alert.yaml</code> on localhost.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0681","card":"q-aws-088-messaging-data-and-alerting-integrations","sec":"aws-s089","q":"Terraform for SNS, GitHub Actions to notify on pod failure?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_sns_topic&quot; &quot;alerts&quot; { name = &quot;pod-failure-alerts&quot; }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Notify Failure\non: workflow_run\njobs:\n  notify:\n    if: failure()\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v2\n      with: { aws-access-key-id: ${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}, aws-secret-access-key: ${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }} }\n    - run: aws sns publish --topic-arn ${{ secrets.SNS_TOPIC_ARN }} --message &quot;Pod failed&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0682","card":"q-aws-088-messaging-data-and-alerting-integrations","sec":"aws-s089","q":"Terraform for EBS, Ansible to attach it to a Kubernetes pod?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_ebs_volume&quot; &quot;data&quot; { availability_zone = &quot;us-east-1a&quot;, size = 10 }\nresource &quot;aws_volume_attachment&quot; &quot;attach&quot; { device_name = &quot;/dev/xvdf&quot;, volume_id = aws_ebs_volume.data.id, instance_id = &quot;i-123&quot; }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: PersistentVolumeClaim\nmetadata: { name: &quot;data-pvc&quot; }\nspec: { accessModes: [&quot;ReadWriteOnce&quot;], resources: { requests: { storage: &quot;10Gi&quot; } } }</code></pre></div>\n<p>The Ansible play runs <code>kubectl apply -f pvc.yaml</code> on localhost.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0683","card":"q-aws-088-messaging-data-and-alerting-integrations","sec":"aws-s089","q":"How should a worker pod on EKS read messages from SQS reliably?","a":"<p>Use long polling (<code>--wait-time-seconds</code> up to 20) to cut empty responses, process the message, and then call <code>delete-message</code>. If the pod crashes before it deletes the message, the message reappears after the visibility timeout. Set the timeout longer than the processing time, and use a dead-letter queue for repeated failures.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0684","card":"q-aws-088-messaging-data-and-alerting-integrations","sec":"aws-s089","q":"What IAM setup should a pod that reads from SQS or writes to DynamoDB use on EKS?","a":"<p>A role for the pod's service account (IRSA or Pod Identity) with only the needed actions, such as <code>sqs:ReceiveMessage</code>, <code>sqs:DeleteMessage</code> and the queue ARN. Avoid giving the node role those permissions, because then every pod on the node gets them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0685","card":"q-aws-088-messaging-data-and-alerting-integrations","sec":"aws-s089","q":"In a Terraform <code>aws_dynamodb_table</code>, why are only the key attributes declared in the <code>attribute</code> blocks?","a":"<p>DynamoDB is schemaless for non-key attributes. Terraform needs the <code>attribute</code> blocks only for attributes used as the table's or an index's key (<code>hash_key</code>, <code>range_key</code> and index keys). Declaring other attributes causes an error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0686","card":"q-aws-088-messaging-data-and-alerting-integrations","sec":"aws-s089","q":"Why does a Kubernetes PersistentVolumeClaim for EBS on EKS normally use a StorageClass and dynamic provisioning instead of creating an <code>aws_ebs_volume</code> and attaching it by hand?","a":"<p>With a StorageClass, the EBS CSI driver creates an EBS volume when the claim is made, in the right AZ, and attaches it to the node where the pod runs. A hand-made volume is tied to one AZ and one instance, and must be attached and mapped manually. The EBS CSI driver add-on has to be installed on EKS (Kubernetes 1.23 and later).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0687","card":"q-aws-088-messaging-data-and-alerting-integrations","sec":"aws-s089","q":"What does the access mode <code>ReadWriteOnce</code> mean for an EBS-backed PVC, and what happens when the pod is rescheduled?","a":"<p><code>ReadWriteOnce</code> means the volume can be mounted read-write by one node at a time. EBS volumes live in one AZ, so a rescheduled pod must run in the same AZ as its volume, or it stays <code>Pending</code>. For shared access across nodes, use EFS with <code>ReadWriteMany</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0688","card":"q-aws-088-messaging-data-and-alerting-integrations","sec":"aws-s089","q":"When a CloudWatch alarm should notify people, what is the usual path from the alarm to a message?","a":"<p>Set the alarm's action to an SNS topic, and subscribe email, SMS, a chat webhook or a Lambda function to that topic. For paging, send it to an incident tool through SNS or EventBridge.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0689","card":"q-aws-088-messaging-data-and-alerting-integrations","sec":"aws-s089","q":"Why is a Kubernetes ConfigMap a poor place to store secrets, and what would you use?","a":"<p>A ConfigMap is plain data, readable by anyone with access to it, and not meant for sensitive values. Use a Kubernetes Secret (with encryption at rest enabled on EKS using a KMS key) or AWS Secrets Manager integrated through the Secrets Store CSI driver.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0690","card":"q-aws-089-troubleshooting-pods-against-aws-service","sec":"aws-s090","q":"How do you troubleshoot a Kubernetes pod on EKS and update the AWS security group with Terraform?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl get pod &lt;pod-name&gt;\nkubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt;\nkubectl logs &lt;pod-name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_security_group_rule&quot; &quot;allow_pod&quot; {\n  type = &quot;ingress&quot;\n  from_port = 80\n  to_port = 80\n  protocol = &quot;tcp&quot;\n  cidr_blocks = [&quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot;]\n  security_group_id = &quot;sg-123&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0691","card":"q-aws-089-troubleshooting-pods-against-aws-service","sec":"aws-s090","q":"How do you troubleshoot an RDS connection from a Kubernetes pod, with a Terraform fix?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod-name&gt; -- ping &lt;rds-endpoint&gt;\nkubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_security_group_rule&quot; &quot;rds_access&quot; {\n  type = &quot;ingress&quot;\n  from_port = 3306\n  to_port = 3306\n  protocol = &quot;tcp&quot;\n  source_security_group_id = &quot;sg-pod&quot;\n  security_group_id = &quot;sg-rds&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0692","card":"q-aws-089-troubleshooting-pods-against-aws-service","sec":"aws-s090","q":"How do you troubleshoot a pod failing to pull from ECR, with a Terraform fix?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt;\naws ecr get-login-password | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin &lt;ecr-repo&gt;\nkubectl create secret docker-registry ecr-secret --docker-server=&lt;ecr-repo&gt; --docker-username=AWS --docker-password=&lt;password&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;kubernetes_pod&quot; &quot;fix&quot; { spec: { image_pull_secrets: [{ name: &quot;ecr-secret&quot; }] } }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0693","card":"q-aws-089-troubleshooting-pods-against-aws-service","sec":"aws-s090","q":"How do you troubleshoot pod networking on EKS, with a Terraform VPC fix?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod-name&gt; -- ping 8.8.8.8\nkubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_vpc&quot; &quot;eks_vpc&quot; { cidr_block = &quot;10.0.0.0/16&quot;, enable_dns_hostnames = true }\nresource &quot;aws_route&quot; &quot;internet&quot; { route_table_id = &quot;rt-123&quot;, destination_cidr_block = &quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot;, gateway_id = &quot;igw-123&quot; }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0694","card":"q-aws-089-troubleshooting-pods-against-aws-service","sec":"aws-s090","q":"How do you troubleshoot an AWS ELB that is not routing to Kubernetes pods?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl get svc &lt;service-name&gt; -o wide\nkubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_lb_target_group_attachment&quot; &quot;fix&quot; { target_group_arn = &quot;arn:aws:elasticloadbalancing:us-east-1:123:targetgroup/my-tg/abc&quot;, target_id = &quot;i-123&quot; }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0695","card":"q-aws-089-troubleshooting-pods-against-aws-service","sec":"aws-s090","q":"How do you troubleshoot S3 access from a Kubernetes pod, with a Terraform IAM fix?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod-name&gt; -- aws s3 ls s3://my-bucket\nkubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_iam_role_policy&quot; &quot;s3_access&quot; {\n  role = &quot;eks-node-role&quot;\n  policy = jsonencode({ Statement: [{ Effect: &quot;Allow&quot;, Action: &quot;s3:*&quot;, Resource: &quot;arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket/*&quot; }] })\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0696","card":"q-aws-089-troubleshooting-pods-against-aws-service","sec":"aws-s090","q":"How do you troubleshoot a Kubernetes pod crash on EKS, with a Terraform resource fix?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod-name&gt;\nkubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_eks_node_group&quot; &quot;fix&quot; {\n  cluster_name = &quot;my-eks&quot;\n  node_group_name = &quot;fixed-nodes&quot;\n  scaling_config: { desired_size: 2, max_size: 3, min_size: 1 }\n  instance_types: [&quot;t3.large&quot;]\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0697","card":"q-aws-089-troubleshooting-pods-against-aws-service","sec":"aws-s090","q":"What is the standard order of <code>kubectl</code> commands for troubleshooting a failing pod?","a":"<p><code>kubectl get pod</code> for the status, <code>kubectl describe pod</code> for events (scheduling, image pull, probe and mount errors), then <code>kubectl logs</code> (add <code>--previous</code> after a crash) for the application output. Add <code>kubectl exec</code> for checks from inside, and <code>kubectl get events</code> for the namespace view.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0698","card":"q-aws-089-troubleshooting-pods-against-aws-service","sec":"aws-s090","q":"A pod cannot reach an RDS endpoint. Why can <code>ping</code> give a misleading result and what do you use?","a":"<p>ICMP is often blocked by security groups and RDS does not answer it, and the container may not have <code>ping</code>. Test the TCP port instead, for example <code>nc -zv &lt;endpoint&gt; 3306</code>, and check DNS with <code>nslookup</code>. A successful TCP connection means network and security groups are right and the problem is credentials or configuration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0699","card":"q-aws-089-troubleshooting-pods-against-aws-service","sec":"aws-s090","q":"A pod in a private subnet on EKS cannot reach the internet. Why does adding a route to an internet gateway to the private route table not solve it?","a":"<p>An internet gateway only translates public IPs. A private subnet's resources have no public IP, so they need a NAT gateway placed in a public subnet, with the private route table sending <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> to the NAT gateway. The IGW route belongs to public subnets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0700","card":"q-aws-089-troubleshooting-pods-against-aws-service","sec":"aws-s090","q":"A Kubernetes Service has no traffic reaching the pods. What do you check?","a":"<p><code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;service&gt;</code> (empty endpoints means the selector does not match the pod labels, or the pods are not Ready), then the readiness probe, the <code>targetPort</code> against the container port, and for a load balancer, the target group health and security groups.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0701","card":"q-aws-089-troubleshooting-pods-against-aws-service","sec":"aws-s090","q":"A pod gets <code>AccessDenied</code> calling S3 from EKS. How do you find which identity it is using?","a":"<p>Run <code>aws sts get-caller-identity</code> inside the pod. It shows whether the pod uses the IRSA role or falls back to the node role, so you know which policy to fix. Then check that role's policy for the bucket and object ARNs, and any bucket policy or KMS key policy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0702","card":"q-aws-089-troubleshooting-pods-against-aws-service","sec":"aws-s090","q":"Why is giving the EKS node role <code>s3:*</code> on a bucket a poor fix for a pod's AccessDenied error?","a":"<p>Every pod on those nodes gains write and delete access, and <code>s3:*</code> is more than the pod needs. Create a dedicated IAM role for the pod's service account with just the required actions (for example <code>s3:GetObject</code>) on the specific bucket.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0703","card":"q-aws-089-troubleshooting-pods-against-aws-service","sec":"aws-s090","q":"A pod keeps restarting with exit code 137. What does it mean?","a":"<p>Exit code 137 is 128 + signal 9, meaning the process was killed by SIGKILL. In Kubernetes this is usually <code>OOMKilled</code> because the container went over its memory limit (<code>kubectl describe pod</code> shows the reason). Raise the limit or fix the memory use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0704","card":"q-aws-089-troubleshooting-pods-against-aws-service","sec":"aws-s090","q":"An ELB is not routing to pods on EKS. Why is registering a node instance in a target group by hand not a lasting fix?","a":"<p>Pods and nodes change, so manual registration goes stale. Let the AWS Load Balancer Controller manage target registration from an Ingress or a Service, or use a <code>TargetGroupBinding</code> that keeps the group in sync with the Service's endpoints.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0705","card":"q-aws-090-ansible-vs-terraform-on-aws","sec":"aws-s091","q":"What are the key differences between Ansible and Terraform when managing AWS resources?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Approach</strong>: Terraform is declarative (defines desired state). Ansible is procedural (defines steps to reach a state).</li>\n  <li><strong>Focus</strong>: Terraform provisions infrastructure (for example EC2 instances). Ansible configures it (for example installing software on instances).</li>\n  <li><strong>State</strong>: Terraform keeps a state file. Ansible is stateless.</li>\n  <li><strong>Python integration</strong>: Terraform through <code>python-terraform</code> or <code>subprocess</code>. Ansible through the <code>ansible</code> Python API or custom modules.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0706","card":"q-aws-090-ansible-vs-terraform-on-aws","sec":"aws-s091","q":"What does it mean that Terraform is declarative and Ansible is procedural, and how does it change day-to-day use?","a":"<p>With Terraform you describe the end state and it works out the steps through a plan, comparing with its state. With Ansible you list tasks in order, though well-written modules are idempotent. Terraform shows what will change before it happens. Ansible runs the tasks and reports what changed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0707","card":"q-aws-090-ansible-vs-terraform-on-aws","sec":"aws-s091","q":"Why does Terraform need a state file and Ansible does not?","a":"<p>Terraform maps each resource in the configuration to a real object, and uses the state to compute the diff on the next plan. Ansible checks the live system with each module run, so it needs no state. The trade-off is that Terraform state needs care (remote storage, locking, secrets) and Ansible cannot show a full plan of removals.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0708","card":"q-aws-090-ansible-vs-terraform-on-aws","sec":"aws-s091","q":"How would you use Terraform and Ansible together for a fleet of EC2 web servers?","a":"<p>Terraform creates the VPC, security groups, instances or Auto Scaling group and load balancer. Ansible, using a dynamic EC2 inventory, installs and configures the software. For immutable setups, replace Ansible with a baked AMI (built by Packer, possibly using Ansible) and a launch template.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0709","card":"q-aws-090-ansible-vs-terraform-on-aws","sec":"aws-s091","q":"Can Ansible create AWS resources, and when would you choose it over Terraform for that?","a":"<p>Yes, through the <code>amazon.aws</code> collection (EC2, S3, security groups and more). It suits small, one-off or imperative jobs, or teams that use only Ansible. For long-lived infrastructure, Terraform's state, plans and dependency graph give safer changes and clearer drift detection.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0710","card":"q-aws-091-securing-aws-credentials","sec":"aws-s092","q":"How do you keep AWS credentials secure in Python scripts?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>IAM roles</strong>: assign roles to EC2 instances or Lambda functions so no credentials are hardcoded.</li>\n  <li><strong>AWS Secrets Manager</strong>: store credentials there and retrieve them with boto3.</li>\n  <li><strong>Environment variables or CLI profiles</strong>: <code>AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID</code> and <code>AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY</code> in the environment, or named profiles in <code>~/.aws/credentials</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>No hardcoding</strong>: never embed credentials in source code.</li>\n  <li><strong>Least privilege</strong>: give roles and users minimal permissions.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3\nclient = boto3.client(&#x27;secretsmanager&#x27;)\nsecret = client.get_secret_value(SecretId=&#x27;my-secret&#x27;)[&#x27;SecretString&#x27;]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0711","card":"q-aws-091-securing-aws-credentials","sec":"aws-s092","q":"In what order does boto3 look for credentials, and why does it matter?","a":"<p>It checks, in order, credentials passed in code, environment variables, the shared credentials and config files (including profiles and SSO), container credentials, and finally the EC2 instance profile through the metadata service. The first match wins. So a stale <code>AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID</code> in the environment can override an instance role and cause surprising permission errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0712","card":"q-aws-091-securing-aws-credentials","sec":"aws-s092","q":"Why should EC2 instances use IMDSv2, and how do you enforce it?","a":"<p>IMDSv1 answers a plain GET request, so a server-side request forgery bug can leak the instance role credentials. IMDSv2 requires a session token obtained with a PUT request. Enforce it by setting <code>HttpTokens = required</code> in the instance's metadata options (or the launch template).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0713","card":"q-aws-091-securing-aws-credentials","sec":"aws-s092","q":"An AWS access key was committed to a public repository. What do you do?","a":"<p>Deactivate or delete the key at once in IAM, then create a new one if still needed. Review CloudTrail for what the key did, and check for new resources, users or roles. Removing the commit from history is not enough, because the key may have been copied already. Add secret scanning and pre-commit checks to prevent a repeat.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0714","card":"q-aws-091-securing-aws-credentials","sec":"aws-s092","q":"What are the benefits of temporary credentials from AWS STS over long-lived IAM user keys?","a":"<p>They expire (from minutes to hours), so a leak has limited time. They come from assumed roles and can be scoped by session policies and conditions. They remove the need to store and rotate secrets, which is why roles for EC2, Lambda, EKS service accounts and OIDC for CI are preferred.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0715","card":"q-aws-092-api-rate-limits","sec":"aws-s093","q":"How do you handle AWS API rate limits in scripts?","a":"<ul>\n  <li>Use boto3's built-in retry mechanism, or <code>tenacity</code> for custom retries.</li>\n  <li>Apply exponential backoff for transient errors.</li>\n  <li>Check AWS service quotas and adjust the script logic.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0716","card":"q-aws-092-api-rate-limits","sec":"aws-s093","q":"A boto3 script gets <code>ThrottlingException</code> or <code>RequestLimitExceeded</code>. What should it do?","a":"<p>Retry with exponential backoff and jitter. The SDK can do it for you: configure the retry mode and the number of attempts. Also reduce call volume by using pagination properly, batching, caching results and using APIs that accept many items.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0717","card":"q-aws-092-api-rate-limits","sec":"aws-s093","q":"In boto3, how do you configure retries for a client, and what is the difference between the retry modes?","a":"<p><code>Config(retries=...)</code> sets <code>max_attempts</code> and <code>mode</code>. <code>legacy</code> is the older behavior. <code>standard</code> uses a consistent set of retryable errors with exponential backoff and jitter. <code>adaptive</code> is standard plus client-side rate limiting that slows requests when it sees throttling, which is useful when many workers share one account.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3\nfrom botocore.config import Config\n\ncfg = Config(retries={&quot;max_attempts&quot;: 10, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;adaptive&quot;})\nec2 = boto3.client(&quot;ec2&quot;, config=cfg)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0718","card":"q-aws-092-api-rate-limits","sec":"aws-s093","q":"Why add jitter to exponential backoff when retrying API calls?","a":"<p>Without jitter, many clients that were throttled at the same moment retry at the same moments again, and cause a new burst (a thundering herd). Random jitter spreads the retries out so the service recovers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0719","card":"q-aws-092-api-rate-limits","sec":"aws-s093","q":"Are AWS API rate limits and service quotas the same thing?","a":"<p>No. API throttling limits control requests per second to an API. Service quotas limit how many resources you can have, such as the number of VPCs or vCPUs. Quotas are mostly adjustable through Service Quotas, and some API rate limits can be raised through support. The fix for throttling is mostly better client behavior.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0720","card":"q-aws-092-api-rate-limits","sec":"aws-s093","q":"Why is retrying a non-idempotent AWS API call a risk, and how does AWS help?","a":"<p>A retry after a timeout can create a duplicate resource, if the first call actually succeeded. Many create APIs accept a client token (such as <code>ClientToken</code> on EC2 <code>RunInstances</code>) so that the same request repeated with the same token returns the original result instead of creating a second resource.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0721","card":"q-aws-093-challenges-of-multi-cloud-automation","sec":"aws-s094","q":"What are the challenges of managing multi-cloud resources (for example AWS and Kubernetes) from scripts?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Complexity</strong>: each API has its own nuances.</li>\n  <li><strong>Authentication</strong>: different mechanisms (AWS IAM vs. Kubernetes RBAC).</li>\n  <li><strong>State</strong>: reconciling Terraform state with Kubernetes resources.</li>\n  <li><strong>Error handling</strong>: varied error formats and retry policies.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dependencies</strong>: library versions and compatibility.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0722","card":"q-aws-093-challenges-of-multi-cloud-automation","sec":"aws-s094","q":"How does <code>kubectl</code> authenticate to an EKS cluster, and how does that differ from AWS API authentication?","a":"<p><code>kubectl</code> runs <code>aws eks get-token</code>, which creates a token from your IAM identity, and EKS maps that IAM identity to Kubernetes users and groups, using EKS access entries or the <code>aws-auth</code> ConfigMap. Then Kubernetes RBAC decides what you can do. AWS APIs are authorized by IAM policy alone, so you need both layers right.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0723","card":"q-aws-093-challenges-of-multi-cloud-automation","sec":"aws-s094","q":"Why is it a problem when two tools, such as Terraform and <code>kubectl</code>, manage the same resource?","a":"<p>Each tool believes it owns the desired state. One changes the object and the other sees drift, undoes the change or fails. Give each resource one owner, for example Terraform for AWS infrastructure and a GitOps tool or Helm for in-cluster resources.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0724","card":"q-aws-093-challenges-of-multi-cloud-automation","sec":"aws-s094","q":"How do you handle the different error formats and retry behavior of several APIs in one automation script?","a":"<p>Wrap each client in a thin layer that maps errors to a common type (retryable or fatal), and apply a consistent retry policy with backoff. Log the request ID of every failed call. Use each SDK's own retry settings where they exist.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0725","card":"q-aws-093-challenges-of-multi-cloud-automation","sec":"aws-s094","q":"What is the benefit of using Terraform to manage both AWS and Kubernetes resources, and what is the drawback?","a":"<p>One workflow, one state and one dependency graph, so a cluster and the objects in it can be planned together. The drawback is that the Kubernetes provider needs the cluster to exist first, the state can grow large, and Kubernetes objects change outside Terraform often, which causes drift.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0726","card":"q-aws-094-idempotency-across-tools","sec":"aws-s095","q":"How do you ensure idempotency across tools?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Terraform</strong>: state files ensure resources are created or updated only when needed.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ansible</strong>: use <code>state</code> parameters (for example <code>state=present</code>) and check conditions.</li>\n  <li><strong>boto3</strong>: check that a resource exists before creating it (for example <code>head_bucket</code> for S3).</li>\n  <li><strong>Kubernetes</strong>: use <code>apply</code> semantics to reconcile desired and actual state.</li>\n  <li><strong>GitHub Actions</strong>: use conditional steps to avoid redundant actions.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"aws-f0727","card":"q-aws-094-idempotency-across-tools","sec":"aws-s095","q":"What does idempotent mean for infrastructure automation, and why does it matter?","a":"<p>Running the same operation several times gives the same end state as running it once, with no duplicates and no errors. It makes pipelines safe to retry after a failure, lets you re-run to fix drift, and avoids side effects from partial runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0728","card":"q-aws-094-idempotency-across-tools","sec":"aws-s095","q":"In boto3, how do you create an S3 bucket only if it does not exist, and what does a 403 from <code>head_bucket</code> mean?","a":"<p><code>head_bucket</code> succeeds if the bucket exists and you can access it. A 404 means it does not exist, so the script creates it. A 403 means the name exists but belongs to someone else or you lack access, so the script re-raises and must not try to create it. Outside <code>us-east-1</code>, <code>create_bucket</code> also needs a <code>CreateBucketConfiguration</code> with a <code>LocationConstraint</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3\nfrom botocore.exceptions import ClientError\n\ns3 = boto3.client(&quot;s3&quot;)\ntry:\n    s3.head_bucket(Bucket=&quot;my-bucket&quot;)\nexcept ClientError as e:\n    if e.response[&quot;Error&quot;][&quot;Code&quot;] == &quot;404&quot;:\n        s3.create_bucket(Bucket=&quot;my-bucket&quot;)\n    else:\n        raise</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"aws-f0729","card":"q-aws-094-idempotency-across-tools","sec":"aws-s095","q":"Why is <code>kubectl apply</code> considered more idempotent than <code>kubectl create</code>?","a":"<p><code>create</code> fails with <code>AlreadyExists</code> if the object exists. <code>apply</code> compares the manifest with the last applied configuration and the live object, then creates or patches as needed, so repeated runs converge. Server-side apply (<code>--server-side</code>) adds field ownership to handle conflicts between tools.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0730","card":"q-aws-094-idempotency-across-tools","sec":"aws-s095","q":"In Ansible, how do you make a <code>command</code> or <code>shell</code> task idempotent?","a":"<p>Use <code>creates:</code> or <code>removes:</code> to skip the task when a file exists or not, add <code>changed_when</code> and <code>failed_when</code> to report the real result, or use a dedicated module (<code>package</code>, <code>service</code>, <code>file</code>, <code>kubernetes.core.k8s</code>), which checks state itself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"aws-f0731","card":"q-aws-094-idempotency-across-tools","sec":"aws-s095","q":"How does Terraform achieve idempotency, and when can it fail to?","a":"<p>It compares the configuration with state and refreshed real infrastructure, and changes only the differences, so a second apply with no changes does nothing. It breaks down if state is lost or out of sync, if resources are changed by hand (drift), or if a resource exists but is not in state, which needs <code>terraform import</code>.</p>","hand":true}];
